findings.technical_details:
In `NextGenCore.mint` (`smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol`), lines 191–195 handle supply accounting and token minting:

```solidity
collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionCirculationSupply = collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionCirculationSupply + 1;
if (collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionTotalSupply >= collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionCirculationSupply) {
    _mintProcessing(mintIndex, _mintTo, _tokenData, _collectionID, _saltfun_o);
    if (phase == 1) {
        tokensMintedAllowlistAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] = tokensMintedAllowlistAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] + 1;
    } else {
        tokensMintedPerAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] = tokensMintedPerAddress[_collectionID][_mintingAddress] + 1;
    }
}
```

There are two coupled flaws in this logic:
1. `collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].collectionCirculationSupply` is incremented on line 191 unconditionally, outside the supply cap check on line 192.
2. Line 192 guards `_mintProcessing` with an `if` condition (`collectionTotalSupply >= collectionCirculationSupply`) instead of a `require` statement that reverts on supply exhaustion.

In `NextGenMinterContract.mint` (lines 231–237), the supply check `collectionTokenMintIndex <= gencore.viewTokensIndexMax(col)` is performed only once prior to the `for (uint256 i = 0; i < _numberOfTokens; i++)` loop. Inside the loop, each iteration calls `gencore.mint(...)`, which invokes `_mintProcessing` -> `_safeMint(_mintTo, _mintIndex)` and triggers an `onERC721Received` callback on `_mintTo`.

If a recipient contract re-enters `NextGenMinterContract.mint`, `burnToMint`, or `burnOrSwapExternalToMint` during an earlier iteration of a batch mint (while purely respecting per-address allowances) and mints the remaining supply of the collection, the outer loop in `NextGenMinterContract.mint` continues executing the remaining iterations. In each remaining iteration:
- `NextGenMinterContract.mint` computes `mintIndex = gencore.viewTokensIndexMin(col) + gencore.viewCirSupply(col)` (which now exceeds `reservedMaxTokensIndex`) and calls `gencore.mint`.
- `NextGenCore.mint` increments `collectionCirculationSupply` on line 191 so that `collectionCirculationSupply > collectionTotalSupply`.
- The `if` check on line 192 evaluates to `false`, so `_mintProcessing` is silently skipped without reverting.

Consequently:
- `collectionCirculationSupply` diverges from the actual number of minted tokens and exceeds `collectionTotalSupply`.
- `viewCirSupply(_collectionID)` and `totalSupplyOfCollection(_collectionID)` return inflated values.
- `setFinalSupply(_collectionID)` (line 309) will set `collectionTotalSupply` and `reservedMaxTokensIndex` to the corrupted `collectionCirculationSupply`.
- The caller pays the full `msg.value` for `_numberOfTokens` tokens (recorded in `collectionTotalAmount[col]`), even though the excess iterations in the batch did not mint any tokens.

findings.remediation:
Replace the `if` statement with a `require` statement so that any attempt to mint beyond `collectionTotalSupply` reverts the transaction and rolls back the increment to `collectionCirculationSupply` (as well as updating state before external calls):

