findings.technical_details:
In `hardhat/smart-contracts/AuctionDemo.sol`, the `cancelBid` function allows a bidder to cancel an active bid before the auction ends and receive a refund of their deposited ETH:

```solidity
function cancelBid(uint256 _tokenid, uint256 index) public {
    require(block.timestamp <= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid), "Auction ended");
    require(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bidder == msg.sender && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].status == true);
    auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].status = false;
    (bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bid}("");
    emit CancelBid(msg.sender, _tokenid, index, success, auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bid);
}
```

At line 127, `auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].status` is set to `false` to deactivate the bid. Immediately afterward, at line 128, a low-level `.call{value: ...}("")` is used to return the bid amount (`auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bid`) to `auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bidder`.

According to Solidity semantics, low-level `.call` does not automatically revert the transaction when the sub-call fails or reverts; instead, it returns `success = false`. Because `cancelBid` only logs `success` in the `CancelBid` event (line 129) without asserting `require(success)`, a failed ETH transfer does not revert the state transition at line 127.

Once `auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].status` is persisted as `false`:
1. Subsequent calls to `cancelBid` or `cancelAllBids` for that bid will fail or skip the entry because both require `auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].status == true`.
2. `returnHighestBid` and `returnHighestBidder` ignore bids with `status == false`, so the bid is no longer eligible to win the auction.
3. `claimAuction` only refunds non-winning bids where `auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true` (line 115), so the bidder will not be refunded at auction settlement either.

As a result, failed-disbursement state divergence occurs: the contract clears its recorded liability (`status = false`) while retaining the bidder's ETH, permanently trapping the funds inside `auctionDemo`.

findings.remediation:
Check the `success` boolean returned by the low-level `.call` using a `require` statement so that the entire transaction (including setting `status = false`) reverts if the refund transfer fails.

```solidity
    function cancelBid(uint256 _tokenid, uint256 index) public {
        require(block.timestamp <= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid), "Auction ended");
        require(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bidder == msg.sender && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].status == true);
        auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].status = false;
        (bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bid}("");
        require(success, "Refund failed");
        emit CancelBid(msg.sender, _tokenid, index, success, auctionInfoData[_tokenid][index].bid);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `AuctionDemo.sol`, every call to `participateToAuction(uint256 _tokenid)` appends a new `auctionInfoStru` record to the `auctionInfoData[_tokenid]` array via `.push(newBid)`. Even when a bid is cancelled via `cancelBid` or `cancelAllBids`, the entry is not removed from the array; instead, its `status` boolean is simply set to `false` (lines 127 and 138).

When `cancelAllBids(uint256 _tokenid)` is invoked (lines 134–143):
```solidity
function cancelAllBids(uint256 _tokenid) public {
    require(block.timestamp <= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid), "Auction ended");
    for (uint256 i=0; i<auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length; i++) {
        if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder == msg.sender && auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status == true) {
            auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].status = false;
            (bool success, ) = payable(auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bidder).call{value: auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid}("");
            emit CancelBid(msg.sender, _tokenid, i, success, auctionInfoData[_tokenid][i].bid);
        } else {}
    }
}
```
The `for` loop at line 136 traverses every historical bid entry in `auctionInfoData[_tokenid]` regardless of whether the entry is still active or belongs to `msg.sender`. Additionally, `returnBids(uint256 _tokenid)` at lines 147–148 copies the entire unbounded `auctionInfoData[_tokenid]` storage array into memory.

Because `participateToAuction` enforces no minimum bid increment beyond `msg.value > returnHighestBid(_tokenid)` (allowing 1-wei increments) and allows bidders to repeatedly bid and cancel via `cancelBid` to recycle their ETH while permanently increasing `auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length`, the array length can grow arbitrarily large at minimal cost. Once `auctionInfoData[_tokenid].length` becomes sufficiently large, iterating through all storage slots in `cancelAllBids` (and copying the entire array in `returnBids`) consumes more gas than the block gas limit, rendering `cancelAllBids` permanently unusable for that `_tokenid`.

findings.remediation:
Avoid storing and iterating over an unbounded array of all historical bids per token. Instead, track only the current highest bid and refund the previous highest bidder immediately upon being outbid (or credit a pull-based withdrawal mapping `mapping(uint256 => mapping(address => uint256)) public pendingReturns`), or track each user's active bid total in a direct mapping so `cancelAllBids` executes in `O(1)` time without looping.

If the array structure must be retained for indexing, aggregate a bidder's active balance or indices, or accumulate the total refund and bound the number of entries processed:

```solidity
    mapping(uint256 => mapping(address => uint256[])) private userBidIndices;

    function cancelAllBids(uint256 _tokenid) public {
        require(block.timestamp <= minter.getAuctionEndTime(_tokenid), "Auction ended");
        uint256[] storage indices = userBidIndices[_tokenid][msg.sender];
        uint256 len = indices.length;
        uint256 totalRefund = 0;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < len; ) {
            uint256 bidIndex = indices[i];
            if (auctionInfoData[_tokenid][bidIndex].status == true) {
                uint256 bidAmount = auctionInfoData[_tokenid][bidIndex].bid;
                auctionInfoData[_tokenid][bidIndex].status = false;
                totalRefund += bidAmount;
                emit CancelBid(msg.sender, _tokenid, bidIndex, true, bidAmount);
            }
            unchecked { ++i; }
        }
        delete userBidIndices[_tokenid][msg.sender];
        if (totalRefund > 0) {
            (bool success, ) = payable(msg.sender).call{value: totalRefund}("");
            require(success, "Refund failed");
