findings.technical_details:
In `Guard.checkTransaction`, the only length validation performed on the transaction payload `data` is:
```solidity
if (data.length < 4) {
    revert Errors.GuardPolicy_FunctionSelectorRequired();
}
```
When no transaction hook is active for the target contract `to`, `checkTransaction` invokes `_checkTransaction(msg.sender, to, data)`. For `e721_safe_transfer_from_2_selector` (`safeTransferFrom(address,address,uint256,bytes)`) and `e721_transfer_from_selector` (`transferFrom(address,address,uint256)`), `_checkTransaction` extracts `tokenId` on lines 213–215 and 221–223 via:
```solidity
uint256 tokenId = uint256(
    _loadValueFromCalldata(data, e721_transfer_from_token_id_offset)
);
```
where `_loadValueFromCalldata` executes:
```solidity
assembly {
    value := mload(add(data, offset))
}
```
Because `data` is a `bytes memory` variable, its first 32 bytes (`[data, data + 0x20)`) store `data.length`, and its payload bytes reside at `[data + 0x20, data + 0x20 + data.length)`. Reading a full 32-byte word at `offset = 0x64` accesses memory range `[data + 0x64, data + 0x84)`, which corresponds to payload bytes `[0x44, 0x64)` (bytes 68 through 100 of the calldata).

However, `_checkTransaction` never checks that `data.length` is at least `0x64` (for `transferFrom`) or the minimum valid ABI-encoded size for `safeTransferFrom(address,address,uint256,bytes)`. In `Guard.checkTransaction`, `data` is followed in memory by the ABI-encoded `signatures` `bytes memory` array (which is at least 65 bytes of ECDSA signature data for the Safe owner). If a caller passes a `data` payload shorter than `0x64` bytes (for example, 68 bytes containing only `selector`, `from`, and `to`), `mload(add(data, 0x64))` reads out-of-bounds memory directly from the adjacent `signatures` buffer (specifically its length word and signature bytes), producing a non-zero `tokenId` in `Guard`.

When the Gnosis Safe subsequently executes the external call to the target ERC-721 contract (`to`) with the same truncated `data`, the EVM `CALLDATALOAD` opcode automatically pads any bytes beyond `calldatasize` with zeros. For ERC-721 contracts that do not revert on short calldata (such as contracts compiled with Solidity versions prior to `0.8.0` or contracts using inline assembly / `calldataload` directly), the target contract decodes the missing `tokenId` bytes as `0`. Because `Guard` checked `STORE.isRentedOut(safe, token, corruptedTokenId)` instead of the actual `tokenId` (`0`, or a `tokenId` with zero trailing bytes), `_revertSelectorOnActiveRental` does not revert, allowing the renter to transfer an actively rented ERC-721 token out of the rental Safe.

findings.remediation:
Validate that `data.length` is at least large enough to cover the full 32-byte word being loaded at `offset` (i.e., `data.length >= offset - 0x20 + 0x20`, which simplifies to `data.length >= offset`) before reading from memory in `_loadValueFromCalldata` or within each selector branch of `_checkTransaction`.

```solidity
        } else if (selector == e721_safe_transfer_from_2_selector) {
            if (data.length < e721_safe_transfer_from_2_token_id_offset) {
                revert Errors.GuardPolicy_UnauthorizedSelector(selector);
            }

            // Load the token ID from calldata.
            uint256 tokenId = uint256(
                _loadValueFromCalldata(data, e721_safe_transfer_from_2_token_id_offset)
            );

            // Check if the selector is allowed.
            _revertSelectorOnActiveRental(selector, from, to, tokenId);
        } else if (selector == e721_transfer_from_selector) {
            if (data.length < e721_transfer_from_token_id_offset) {
                revert Errors.GuardPolicy_UnauthorizedSelector(selector);
            }

            // Load the token ID from calldata.
            uint256 tokenId = uint256(
                _loadValueFromCalldata(data, e721_transfer_from_token_id_offset)
