findings.technical_details:
During rental creation in `Create._rentFromZone`, Seaport executes the token transfers specified in the order prior to invoking `validateOrder` on the `Create` zone contract. For any ERC20 item in the order, `_executionInvariantChecks` verifies that the recipient of the execution is `address(ESCRW)`.

However, in lines 599–603 of `Create.sol`:
```solidity
for (uint256 i = 0; i < items.length; ++i) {
    if (items[i].isERC20()) {
        ESCRW.increaseDeposit(items[i].token, items[i].amount);
    }
}
```
`Create._rentFromZone` passes `items[i].amount` (the nominal amount specified in the Seaport offer/consideration item) directly to `ESCRW.increaseDeposit(address token, uint256 amount)`. Inside `PaymentEscrow._increaseDeposit`, the internal accounting mapping `balanceOf[token]` is incremented directly by `amount`:
```solidity
function _increaseDeposit(address token, uint256 amount) internal {
    // Directly increase the synced balance.
    balanceOf[token] += amount;
}
```

When an ERC20 token charges a transfer fee (fee-on-transfer) or has deflationary/rebasing mechanics, the actual amount of tokens received by `ESCRW` during Seaport's execution is strictly less than `items[i].amount`. Because neither `Create._rentFromZone` nor `PaymentEscrow.increaseDeposit` checks the actual ERC20 balance of `address(ESCRW)` against `balanceOf[token]`, the internal accounting `ESCRW.balanceOf[token]` is credited with more tokens than `ESCRW` actually received.

This directly violates the documented protocol invariant in `README.md`:
> "Stored token balance of the `src/modules/PaymentEscrow.sol` contract should never be less than the true token balance of the contract" (i.e., the true token balance must always be at least the stored `balanceOf[token]` so all escrow obligations can be settled).

When `Stop.stopRent` or `Stop.stopRentBatch` is later called to terminate the rental:
1. `ESCRW.settlePayment(order)` attempts to transfer the full `items[i].amount` from `ESCRW` to the lender/renter.
2. If no other users have deposited the same ERC20 token into `ESCRW`, the ERC20 transfer in `settlePayment` reverts due to insufficient token balance in `ESCRW`. Because `stopRent` reverts, `_reclaimRentedItems(order)` cannot complete, permanently locking the lender's ERC721/ERC1155 NFT inside the renter's `rentalWallet` (`Safe`).
3. If other users have active rentals using the same ERC20 token, `settlePayment` consumes their escrowed tokens to cover the shortfall, leaving the last rental orders unable to be stopped and their NFTs and payments permanently stuck.

