Follow the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern in `Stop.stopRent` by calling `STORE.removeRentals` before executing `_removeHooks`, `_reclaimRentedItems`, and `ESCRW.settlePayment`. This ensures that the existence of the rental order is verified and its storage record is deleted prior to any external calls.

```solidity
    function stopRent(RentalOrder calldata order) external {
        // Check that the rental can be stopped.
        _validateRentalCanBeStoped(order.orderType, order.endTimestamp, order.lender);

        // Create an accumulator which will hold all of the rental asset updates, consisting of IDs and
        // the rented amount. From this point on, new memory cannot be safely allocated until the
        // accumulator no longer needs to include elements.
        bytes memory rentalAssetUpdates = new bytes(0);

        // Check if each item in the order is a rental. If so, then generate the rental asset update.
        // Memory will become safe again after this block.
        for (uint256 i; i < order.items.length; ++i) {
            if (order.items[i].isRental()) {
                // Insert the rental asset update into the dynamic array.
                _insert(
                    rentalAssetUpdates,
                    order.items[i].toRentalId(order.rentalWallet),
                    order.items[i].amount
                );
            }
        }

        // Interaction: Remove rentals from storage by computing the order hash.
        STORE.removeRentals(
            _deriveRentalOrderHash(order),
            _convertToStatic(rentalAssetUpdates)
        );

        // Interaction: process hooks so they no longer exist for the renter.
        if (order.hooks.length > 0) {
            _removeHooks(order.hooks, order.items, order.rentalWallet);
        }

        // Interaction: Transfer rentals from the renter back to lender.
        _reclaimRentedItems(order);

        // Interaction: Transfer ERC20 payments from the escrow contract to the respective recipients.
        ESCRW.settlePayment(order);

        // Emit rental order stopped.
        _emitRentalOrderStopped(order.seaportOrderHash, msg.sender);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
When a `BASE` or `PAY` rental order is created via `Create._rentFromZone`, the contract constructs a `RentalOrder` struct, computes its EIP-712 digest `orderHash = _deriveRentalOrderHash(order)`, and records the rental in the `Storage` module by calling `STORE.addRentals(orderHash, _convertToStatic(rentalAssetUpdates))` (line 595 of `Create.sol`).

In `Storage.addRentals` (`smart-contracts/src/modules/Storage.sol`, lines 189–203):

```solidity
function addRentals(
    bytes32 orderHash,
    RentalAssetUpdate[] memory rentalAssetUpdates
) external onlyByProxy permissioned {
    // Add the order to storage.
    orders[orderHash] = true;

    // Add the rented items to storage.
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < rentalAssetUpdates.length; ++i) {
        RentalAssetUpdate memory asset = rentalAssetUpdates[i];

        // Update the order hash for that item.
        rentedAssets[asset.rentalId] += asset.amount;
    }
}
```

Unlike `Storage.removeRentals` (lines 221–226) and `Storage.removeRentalsBatch` (lines 251–256), which explicitly check `if (!orders[orderHash]) revert Errors.StorageModule_OrderDoesNotExist(orderHash)` before deleting the entry, `Storage.addRentals` does not check whether `orders[orderHash]` is already `true`.

Because `RentalOrder` only depends on `seaportOrderHash`, `items`, `hooks`, `orderType`, `lender`, `renter`, `rentalWallet`, `startTimestamp` (`block.timestamp`), and `endTimestamp` (`block.timestamp + payload.metadata.rentDuration`), fulfilling the same Seaport order multiple times within the same block (for example, via partial fills of an ERC1155/ERC20 order in the same block with equal fill fractions, or via a reentrant hook/callback) produces the exact same `orderHash`.

When `addRentals` is called a second time with the same `orderHash`:
1. `orders[orderHash]` is silently overwritten with `true` (a single boolean flag cannot track multiple active instances of the same `orderHash`).
2. `rentedAssets[asset.rentalId]` is incremented a second time by `asset.amount`.
3. `ESCRW.increaseDeposit` is called a second time, increasing the tracked escrow balance.

However, when the rental period ends and `Stop.stopRent` (or `Stop.stopRentBatch`) is called:
1. The first call to `stopRent(order)` reclaims one set of items, settles one order's payment from `PaymentEscrow`, decrements `rentedAssets[asset.rentalId]` by `asset.amount` once, and deletes `orders[orderHash]` (`orders[orderHash] = false`).
2. Any subsequent attempt to call `stopRent(order)` for the second fulfillment reverts in `Storage.removeRentals` with `StorageModule_OrderDoesNotExist(orderHash)` because `orders[orderHash]` was already deleted.

Consequently, the second set of rented tokens and the remaining escrowed ERC20 payment can never be settled or reclaimed, and `rentedAssets[asset.rentalId]` remains permanently non-zero (`isRentedOut` stays `true` forever), permanently blocklisting the asset in the `rentalWallet` via `Guard`.

findings.remediation:
Check whether `orders[orderHash]` is already `true` in `Storage.addRentals` and revert if the order already exists in storage.

```solidity
    function addRentals(
        bytes32 orderHash,
        RentalAssetUpdate[] memory rentalAssetUpdates
    ) external onlyByProxy permissioned {
        // The order must not already exist in storage.
        if (orders[orderHash]) {
            revert Errors.StorageModule_OrderAlreadyExists(orderHash);
        }

        // Add the order to storage.
        orders[orderHash] = true;

        // Add the rented items to storage.
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < rentalAssetUpdates.length; ++i) {
            RentalAssetUpdate memory asset = rentalAssetUpdates[i];

            // Update the order hash for that item.
            rentedAssets[asset.rentalId] += asset.amount;
        }
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
When a rental order is created via `Create`, `STORE.addRentals(orderHash, rentalAssetUpdates)` (`Storage.sol:189-203`) records the order as active by setting `orders[orderHash] = true` and increments the aggregate rented token balance `rentedAssets[asset.rentalId] += asset.amount` for each rented item, where `asset.rentalId` is derived via `RentalUtils.getItemPointer(order.rentalWallet, item.token, item.identifier)`.

When stopping a rental in `Stop.stopRent` (`Stop.sol:265-306`) or `Stop.stopRentBatch` (`Stop.sol:313-364`):
1. Each item's `RentalId` is computed from the caller-supplied `order.rentalWallet` (`order.items[i].toRentalId(order.rentalWallet)` at `Stop.sol:281` and `Stop.sol:338`).
2. `_reclaimRentedItems(order)` (`Stop.sol:166-183`) instructs `ISafe(order.rentalWallet)` to transfer the rented ERC721/ERC1155 tokens back to `order.lender`.
3. `STORE.removeRentals(_deriveRentalOrderHash(order), _convertToStatic(rentalAssetUpdates))` (`Storage.sol:216-235`) is invoked to deactivate the order and decrement the aggregate rented asset balances:

```solidity
if (!orders[orderHash]) {
    revert Errors.StorageModule_OrderDoesNotExist(orderHash);
} else {
    delete orders[orderHash];
}

for (uint256 i = 0; i < rentalAssetUpdates.length; ++i) {
    RentalAssetUpdate memory asset = rentalAssetUpdates[i];
    rentedAssets[asset.rentalId] -= asset.amount;
}
```

However, `_deriveRentalOrderHash(RentalOrder memory order)` (`Signer.sol:162-195`) hashes `seaportOrderHash`, `itemHashes`, `hookHashes`, `orderType`, `lender`, `renter`, `startTimestamp`, and `endTimestamp`, **omitting `order.rentalWallet`**. Furthermore, `Storage.removeRentals` only checks `orders[orderHash]` (a boolean flag) and does not bind `orderHash` to the specific `RentalAssetUpdate` entries (`asset.rentalId` and `asset.amount`) that were registered during `addRentals`.

Consequently, when stopping a valid order (`orderA`), the caller can replace `orderA.rentalWallet` with the address of any other protocol-deployed `rentalWallet` (`victimSafe`) that holds the same ERC1155 token (or ERC721 token):
- `_deriveRentalOrderHash(orderA)` produces the exact `orderHash` of `orderA`, passing the `if (!orders[orderHash])` check in `Storage.removeRentals` (`Storage.sol:221`) and deleting `orders[orderHashA]` (`Storage.sol:225`).
- `_reclaimRentedItems` transfers the asset out of `victimSafe` to `orderA.lender`.
- `Storage.removeRentals` executes `rentedAssets[asset.rentalId] -= asset.amount` (`Storage.sol:233`) using the `RentalId` tied to `victimSafe` rather than `orderA.rentalWallet`.

This causes four interconnected failures in aggregate rental accounting:
1. **Cross-order quantity conservation failure**: Stopping `orderA` reclaims tokens and decrements `rentedAssets` belonging to `victimSafe`'s active rental (`orderB`).
2. **Duplicate or incomplete asset decrements**: `rentedAssets` for `orderA.rentalWallet` is never decremented (leaving the asset permanently marked as rented out in `Guard._revertSelectorOnActiveRental`), while `rentedAssets` for `victimSafe` is decremented prematurely.
3. **Order-status and asset-balance desynchronization**: `orders[orderHashA]` is `false` while `rentedAssets[rentalIdA] > 0`, and `orders[orderHashB]` remains `true` while `rentedAssets[rentalIdB]` is reduced to `0` (allowing `victimSafe` to bypass `Guard.sol:133` if any tokens remain).
4. **Accounting-induced rental cleanup failure**: When `orderB` expires and a user attempts to call `stopRent(orderB)`, `Storage.removeRentals` reverts with an arithmetic underflow panic at `Storage.sol:233` (`rentedAssets[asset.rentalId] -= asset.amount`)—and `_reclaimRentedItems` also fails if the tokens were already extracted from `victimSafe`—making `orderB` impossible to stop and permanently locking its ERC20 payments in `PaymentEscrow`.

findings.remediation:
Bind the `rentalWallet` (and thus the exact `RentalId` entries in `rentalAssetUpdates`) to `orderHash` by hashing `order.rentalWallet` in `_deriveRentalOrderHash`, and store/validate a commitment to `rentalAssetUpdates` per `orderHash` in `Storage.sol` so `removeRentals` only decrements the exact `RentalAssetUpdate` entries that were registered for that `orderHash`.

In `smart-contracts/src/modules/Storage.sol` (and correspondingly in `Signer._deriveRentalOrderHash`):

```solidity
    function removeRentals(
        bytes32 orderHash,
        RentalAssetUpdate[] calldata rentalAssetUpdates
    ) external onlyByProxy permissioned {
        // The order must exist to be deleted.
        if (!orders[orderHash]) {
            revert Errors.StorageModule_OrderDoesNotExist(orderHash);
        } else {
            // Delete the order from storage.
            delete orders[orderHash];
        }

        // Process each rental asset.
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < rentalAssetUpdates.length; ++i) {
            RentalAssetUpdate memory asset = rentalAssetUpdates[i];

            // Reduce the amount of tokens for the particular rental ID.
            rentedAssets[asset.rentalId] -= asset.amount;
        }
    }
```

And in `smart-contracts/src/packages/Signer.sol`, include `order.rentalWallet` in `_RENTAL_ORDER_TYPEHASH` and `_deriveRentalOrderHash`:

```solidity
        return
            keccak256(
                abi.encode(
                    _RENTAL_ORDER_TYPEHASH,
                    order.seaportOrderHash,
                    keccak256(abi.encodePacked(itemHashes)),
                    keccak256(abi.encodePacked(hookHashes)),
                    order.orderType,
                    order.lender,
                    order.renter,
                    order.rentalWallet,
                    order.startTimestamp,
                    order.endTimestamp
                )
            );
```

findings.technical_details:
According to the EIP-712 specification (`encodeData`), dynamic types such as `bytes` and `string` must be encoded as the `keccak256` hash of their byte contents (a 32-byte `bytes32` value) before being concatenated with the struct's `typeHash` and other encoded member values.

The `Hook` struct is defined in `RentalStructs.sol` as:
```solidity
struct Hook {
    address target;
    uint256 itemIndex;
    bytes extraData;
}
```
And its EIP-712 type string in `Signer._deriveRentalTypehashes` is:
```solidity
"Hook(address target,uint256 itemIndex,bytes extraData)"
```

However, in `Signer._deriveHookHash` (lines 147–153 of `smart-contracts/src/packages/Signer.sol`):
```solidity
function _deriveHookHash(Hook memory hook) internal view returns (bytes32) {
    // Derive and return the hook as specified by EIP-712.
    return
        keccak256(
            abi.encode(_HOOK_TYPEHASH, hook.target, hook.itemIndex, hook.extraData)
        );
}
```
Passing `hook.extraData` (type `bytes`) directly into `abi.encode` causes Solidity's ABI encoder to encode a 32-byte offset pointer (`0x80`), followed by the 32-byte length of `extraData`, followed by the padded byte content of `extraData`. Instead, EIP-712 requires encoding `keccak256(hook.extraData)` as a single 32-byte word at that position.

Because `_deriveHookHash` is used by:
1. `_deriveOrderMetadataHash` (which is used to verify `seaportPayload.zoneHash` in `_isValidOrderMetadata` and to compute `_deriveRentPayloadHash` for verifying the `CREATE_SIGNER` signature in `Create.validateOrder`), and
2. `_deriveRentalOrderHash` (which is used to record and remove rental order hashes in `Storage`),

any off-chain signer or client (such as `eth_signTypedData_v4`, Ethers.js, Viem, or standard EIP-712 tooling) that computes the `OrderMetadata`, `RentPayload`, or `RentalOrder` EIP-712 digest when `hooks` are present will produce a completely different hash from the one computed on-chain by `Signer.sol`.

findings.remediation:
Hash `hook.extraData` using `keccak256` before passing it to `abi.encode` in `Signer._deriveHookHash` so that the encoding adheres to the EIP-712 standard:

```solidity
    function _deriveHookHash(Hook memory hook) internal view returns (bytes32) {
        // Derive and return the hook as specified by EIP-712.
        return
            keccak256(
                abi.encode(
                    _HOOK_TYPEHASH,
                    hook.target,
                    hook.itemIndex,
                    keccak256(hook.extraData)
                )
            );
    }
```

Include `order.rentalWallet` in `Signer._deriveRentalOrderHash` so that the `orderHash` computed at `Create._rentFromZone` (line 592) and verified at `Stop.stopRent` / `Stop.stopRentBatch` cryptographically binds the `rentalWallet` address:

```solidity
    function _deriveRentalOrderHash(
        RentalOrder memory order
    ) internal view returns (bytes32) {
        // Create arrays for items and hooks.
        bytes32[] memory itemHashes = new bytes32[](order.items.length);
        bytes32[] memory hookHashes = new bytes32[](order.hooks.length);

        // Iterate over each item.
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < order.items.length; ++i) {
            // Hash the item.
            itemHashes[i] = _deriveItemHash(order.items[i]);
        }

        // Iterate over each hook.
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < order.hooks.length; ++i) {
            // Hash the hook.
            hookHashes[i] = _deriveHookHash(order.hooks[i]);
        }

        return
            keccak256(
                abi.encode(
                    _RENTAL_ORDER_TYPEHASH,
                    order.seaportOrderHash,
                    keccak256(abi.encodePacked(itemHashes)),
                    keccak256(abi.encodePacked(hookHashes)),
                    order.orderType,
                    order.lender,
                    order.renter,
                    order.rentalWallet,
                    order.startTimestamp,
                    order.endTimestamp
                )
            );
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
When a rental order is validated in `Create.validateOrder`, `_rentFromZone` processes the order items derived by `_convertToItems(seaportPayload.offer, seaportPayload.consideration, payload.metadata.orderType)`:

1. For a `BASE` order, ERC20 payment items are derived from `seaportPayload.consideration`.
2. For a `PAY` order, ERC20 payment items are derived from `seaportPayload.offer` (with `seaportPayload.consideration` required to be empty).

At lines 595–603 of `smart-contracts/src/policies/Create.sol`, for any `BASE` or `PAY` order, `_rentFromZone` registers the rental order in `STORE` and loops over `items` to increase the tracked escrow balance:

```solidity
STORE.addRentals(orderHash, _convertToStatic(rentalAssetUpdates));

for (uint256 i = 0; i < items.length; ++i) {
    if (items[i].isERC20()) {
        ESCRW.increaseDeposit(items[i].token, items[i].amount);
    }
}
```

However, `_executionInvariantChecks` (lines 691–714) only verifies that each execution present in `seaportPayload.totalExecutions` has `address(ESCRW)` as the recipient if it is an ERC20 item and `expectedRentalSafe` if it is a rental NFT. It does not verify that each ERC20 item in `items` corresponds to a distinct, actually executed transfer of `items[i].amount` tokens to `ESCRW`.

Because Seaport's `matchAdvancedOrders` allows matching arbitrary `Fulfillment` components across orders in a batch:
- **Duplicate Crediting Across Matched `PAY` and `BASE` Orders:** An attacker can match a `PAY` order (which offers `X` ERC20 tokens and an NFT) with a `BASE` order (which requires `X` ERC20 tokens as consideration to `ESCRW`). The `PAY` order's ERC20 offer directly satisfies the `BASE` order's ERC20 consideration, generating only **one** execution of `X` ERC20 tokens to `ESCRW` in `totalExecutions`. However, `validateOrder` is invoked for both orders, and `_rentFromZone` credits `ESCRW.increaseDeposit(token, X)` **twice** (recording `2 * X` in `ESCRW.balanceOf[token]`) and creates two active `RentalOrder` records in `STORE.orders`.
- **Unexecuted `PAY` Order Offer Items:** In `matchAdvancedOrders`, Seaport only requires all consideration items to be fulfilled and does not require every offer item of an order to be included in `Fulfillment[]`. Since a `PAY` order has zero consideration items, its ERC20 offer item can be omitted from `Fulfillment[]` (or matched only partially against a smaller consideration in a `PAYEE` order), resulting in zero (or fewer) ERC20 tokens transferred to `ESCRW` in `totalExecutions` while `_rentFromZone` still credits the full `items[i].amount` from `seaportPayload.offer`.

When the orders are subsequently settled via `Stop.stopRent` or `Stop.stopRentBatch`, `ESCRW.settlePayment` transfers the full nominal `items[i].amount` for each order, allowing the attacker to withdraw ERC20 tokens deposited by other users in `PaymentEscrow`.

findings.remediation:
Verify that the actual ERC20 tokens received by `PaymentEscrow` match the credited deposit amounts, or enforce a strict one-to-one mapping between order ERC20 items and `seaportPayload.totalExecutions` (and disallow matching a `PAY` order with a `BASE` order in the same batch). The most robust defense in `_rentFromZone` is to verify that `IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(ESCRW))` is at least the new tracked `ESCRW.balanceOf(token)` after increasing the deposit:

```solidity
            // Interaction: Update storage only if the order is a Base Order or Pay order.
            STORE.addRentals(orderHash, _convertToStatic(rentalAssetUpdates));

            // Interaction: Increase the deposit value on the payment escrow so
            // it knows how many tokens were sent to it, and verify actual escrow balance.
            for (uint256 i = 0; i < items.length; ++i) {
                if (items[i].isERC20()) {
                    ESCRW.increaseDeposit(items[i].token, items[i].amount);
                    if (
