findings.technical_details:
In the reNFT protocol, hooks are configured in `Storage` via `updateHookStatus(address hook, uint8 bitmap)`, where bit `1` (`0x01`) enables `onTransaction`, bit `2` (`0x02`) enables `onStart`, and bit `4` (`0x04`) enables `onStop`. A hook can be legitimately enabled for `onStart` or `onTransaction` without enabling `onStop` (for example, `WhitelistedFulfillment` is configured with `uint8(2)` for `onStart` only), or a hook's status may be updated or disabled while a rental order is active.

When a rental order is created, the `Hook[]` array is included in the `RentalOrder` struct and hashed into the active order hash stored in `STORE.orders` via `_deriveRentalOrderHash(order)`. Consequently, when stopping a rental in `Stop.stopRent` (lines 265–306), the caller must supply the exact `order.hooks` array from creation time, or `STORE.removeRentals` will revert with `StorageModule_OrderDoesNotExist`.

In `Stop.stopRent`, lines 288–290 execute:

```solidity
if (order.hooks.length > 0) {
    _removeHooks(order.hooks, order.items, order.rentalWallet);
}
```

Although the NatSpec on `_removeHooks` states that it should *"process each hook one by one but only if the hook's status is set to execute on a rental stop"*, `_removeHooks` (lines 209–212) instead enforces:

```solidity
if (!STORE.hookOnStop(target)) {
    revert Errors.Shared_DisabledHook(target);
}
```

And in lines 226–248, any revert in `IHook(target).onStop(...)` is caught and re-thrown to revert the entire `stopRent` transaction.

As a result:
1. Any rental order created with a hook that only implements/enables `onStart` (`bitmap = 2`) or `onTransaction` (`bitmap = 1`) has `STORE.hookOnStop(target) == false`. When the rental expires, `stopRent` calls `_removeHooks` and unconditionally reverts with `Errors.Shared_DisabledHook(target)`.
2. If an admin disables a malicious or broken hook (setting `hookStatus` to `0`) while a rental order using that hook is active, `STORE.hookOnStop(target)` becomes `false`, and `stopRent` again reverts with `Errors.Shared_DisabledHook(target)`.

Because `stopRent` (and `stopRentBatch`) always reverts before `_reclaimRentedItems` and `ESCRW.settlePayment` can complete, the rental order can never be settled.

findings.remediation:
Update `_removeHooks` in `Stop.sol` so that hooks without `hookOnStop` enabled are skipped instead of reverting the transaction (matching the documented behavior in the function's `@dev` comment). Additionally, consider ignoring or handling `onStop` reverts gracefully if a hook fails during settlement so that asset reclamation cannot be blocked by external hook state.

```solidity
    function _removeHooks(
        Hook[] calldata hooks,
        Item[] calldata rentalItems,
        address rentalWallet
    ) internal {
        // Define hook target, item index, and item.
        address target;
        uint256 itemIndex;
        Item memory item;

        // Loop through each hook in the payload.
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < hooks.length; ++i) {
            // Get the hook address.
            target = hooks[i].target;

            // Only execute onStop if the hook is reNFT-approved to execute on rental stop.
            if (STORE.hookOnStop(target)) {
                // Get the rental item index for this hook.
                itemIndex = hooks[i].itemIndex;

                // Get the rental item for this hook.
                item = rentalItems[itemIndex];

                // Make sure the item is a rented item.
                if (!item.isRental()) {
                    revert Errors.Shared_NonRentalHookItem(itemIndex);
                }

                // Call the hook with data about the rented item.
                try
                    IHook(target).onStop(
                        rentalWallet,
                        item.token,
                        item.identifier,
                        item.amount,
                        hooks[i].extraData
                    )
                {} catch Error(string memory revertReason) {
                    // Revert with reason given.
                    revert Errors.Shared_HookFailString(revertReason);
                } catch Panic(uint256 errorCode) {
                    // Convert solidity panic code to string.
                    string memory stringErrorCode = LibString.toString(errorCode);

                    // Revert with panic code.
                    revert Errors.Shared_HookFailString(
                        string.concat("Hook reverted: Panic code ", stringErrorCode)
                    );
                } catch (bytes memory revertData) {
                    // Fallback to an error that returns the byte data.
                    revert Errors.Shared_HookFailBytes(revertData);
                }
            }
        }
    }
```

Do not allow `IHook.onStop` failures or unbounded gas consumption during rental termination to revert the settlement transaction. Cap the gas forwarded to `onStop` and emit an event or ignore failures inside the `catch` blocks of `_removeHooks` instead of reverting, ensuring that asset reclamation and payment settlement always succeed.

```solidity
            // Call the hook with a gas cap and do not revert settlement if the hook fails.
            try
                IHook(target).onStop{gas: 100_000}(
                    rentalWallet,
                    item.token,
                    item.identifier,
                    item.amount,
                    hooks[i].extraData
                )
            {} catch {
                // Ignore hook revert on stop so asset reclamation and settlement cannot be blocked.
            }
```

Replace the custom assembly `Accumulator` in `Stop.stopRent` with a standard pre-counted Solidity memory array (or pre-allocate the exact number of rental items before populating the array), and accept `Item calldata` in helper functions where possible so no uncoordinated manual writes to `0x40` occur.

```solidity
        // Count the number of rental items in the order.
        uint256 rentalCount;
        for (uint256 i; i < order.items.length; ++i) {
            if (order.items[i].isRental()) {
                ++rentalCount;
            }
        }

        // Allocate a properly sized static array for the rental asset updates.
        RentalAssetUpdate[] memory rentalAssetUpdates = new RentalAssetUpdate[](rentalCount);

        // Populate the rental asset updates array safely without manual memory manipulation.
        uint256 updateIndex;
        for (uint256 i; i < order.items.length; ++i) {
            if (order.items[i].isRental()) {
                rentalAssetUpdates[updateIndex++] = RentalAssetUpdate(
                    order.items[i].toRentalId(order.rentalWallet),
                    order.items[i].amount
                );
            }
        }

        // Interaction: process hooks so they no longer exist for the renter.
        if (order.hooks.length > 0) {
            _removeHooks(order.hooks, order.items, order.rentalWallet);
        }

        // Interaction: Transfer rentals from the renter back to lender.
        _reclaimRentedItems(order);

        // Interaction: Transfer ERC20 payments from the escrow contract to the respective recipients.
        ESCRW.settlePayment(order);

        // Interaction: Remove rentals from storage by computing the order hash.
        STORE.removeRentals(
            _deriveRentalOrderHash(order),
            rentalAssetUpdates
        );
```

Add a reentrancy guard (`nonReentrant`) to `stopRent` (and `stopRentBatch`) and follow the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern by removing the rental record from `STORE` before executing external interactions (`_removeHooks`, `_reclaimRentedItems`, and `ESCRW.settlePayment`), or ensure `STORE.orders[orderHash]` is verified and cleared prior to any external hook or token callbacks.

```solidity
        // Check if each item in the order is a rental. If so, then generate the rental asset update.
        // Memory will become safe again after this block.
        for (uint256 i; i < order.items.length; ++i) {
            if (order.items[i].isRental()) {
                // Insert the rental asset update into the dynamic array.
                _insert(
                    rentalAssetUpdates,
                    order.items[i].toRentalId(order.rentalWallet),
                    order.items[i].amount
                );
            }
        }

        // Effect/Interaction: Remove rentals from storage first to verify order existence
        // and prevent reentrancy before external callbacks.
        STORE.removeRentals(
            _deriveRentalOrderHash(order),
            _convertToStatic(rentalAssetUpdates)
        );

        // Interaction: process hooks so they no longer exist for the renter.
        if (order.hooks.length > 0) {
            _removeHooks(order.hooks, order.items, order.rentalWallet);
        }

        // Interaction: Transfer rentals from the renter back to lender.
        _reclaimRentedItems(order);

        // Interaction: Transfer ERC20 payments from the escrow contract to the respective recipients.
        ESCRW.settlePayment(order);

        // Emit rental order stopped.
        _emitRentalOrderStopped(order.seaportOrderHash, msg.sender);
```

Decouple the success of `stopRent` from external recipient failures by converting `PaymentEscrow` to a pull-over-push pattern (or wrapping `_safeTransfer` in a try/catch that credits failed transfers to a claimable mapping `claimableBalances[token][recipient]`), and handle failed NFT returns gracefully (or use standard `transferFrom` for ERC721 and a fallback escrow for rejected ERC1155/ERC721 returns).

Specifically, in `PaymentEscrow` and `Stop.stopRent`, ensure that payment settlement records claimable balances or falls back to pull-based withdrawal when a direct transfer fails, and isolate asset reclamation from blocking settlement:

```solidity
