findings.technical_details:
When `ASDRouter.lzCompose` swaps `asdUSDC` for `$NOTE` on the Ambient DEX (`_swapOFTForNote`) and mints `ASDOFT` 1:1 via `_depositNoteToASDVault`, the resulting `amountNote` (`_amount`) is an arbitrary 18-decimal token quantity determined by pool liquidity and price impact. `lzCompose` then calls `_sendASD(_guid, payload, amountNote)` (starting at line 135 of `contracts/asd/asdRouter.sol`).

When the destination chain is not Canto (`_payload._dstLzEid != cantoLzEID`), `_sendASD` constructs the LayerZero `SendParam` struct on line 153 and invokes `IOFT.send` on line 157:

```solidity
SendParam memory sendParams = SendParam({
    dstEid: _payload._dstLzEid,
    to: OFTComposeMsgCodec.addressToBytes32(_payload._dstReceiver),
    amountLD: _amount,
    minAmountLD: _amount,
    extraOptions: sendOptions,
    composeMsg: "0x",
    oftCmd: "0x"
});
```

`ASDOFT` inherits from `OFT` and `OFTCore` (`@layerzerolabs/lz-evm-oapp-v2`), which uses `decimals() = 18` as `_localDecimals` and `sharedDecimals() = 6`. In `OFTCore`'s constructor, `decimalConversionRate` is initialized to:

$$\text{decimalConversionRate} = 10^{18 - 6} = 10^{12}$$

When `IOFT.send` is called on `_payload._cantoAsdAddress`, `OFTCore.send` calls `_debit(_sendParam.amountLD, _sendParam.minAmountLD, _sendParam.dstEid)`, which delegates to `OFTCore._debitView`:

```solidity
function _debitView(
    uint256 _amountLD,
    uint256 _minAmountLD,
    uint32 /*_dstEid*/
) internal view virtual returns (uint256 amountSentLD, uint256 amountReceivedLD) {
    amountSentLD = _removeDust(_amountLD);
    amountReceivedLD = amountSentLD;

    if (amountReceivedLD < _minAmountLD) {
        revert SlippageExceeded(amountReceivedLD, _minAmountLD);
    }
}
```

where `_removeDust(_amountLD)` computes `(_amountLD / decimalConversionRate) * decimalConversionRate`.

Whenever `_amount % 1e12 != 0`—which occurs for virtually every DEX swap output—`amountReceivedLD` is strictly less than `_amount`. Because `_sendASD` sets `minAmountLD: _amount`, the check `amountReceivedLD < _minAmountLD` evaluates to `true` and reverts with `SlippageExceeded(amountReceivedLD, _amount)`. Consequently, `successfulSend` on line 157 is `false`, and `_sendASD` falls back to refunding the `ASDOFT` tokens on Canto instead of bridging them to the destination chain.

Furthermore, even if `minAmountLD` is rounded down so `IOFT.send` succeeds, `OFT._debit` only burns `amountSentLD = _removeDust(_amount)`, leaving `_amount - amountSentLD` dust tokens stranded in `ASDRouter` and violating the protocol invariant that `ASDRouter`'s token balance must be zero after `lzCompose` completes.

findings.remediation:
Round `minAmountLD` down using the OFT's `decimalConversionRate` (or set `minAmountLD` to `(_amount / 1e12) * 1e12`) and use empty bytes `""` for `composeMsg` and `oftCmd`, or only mint/bridge the dust-removed amount so no dust remains in `ASDRouter`:

```solidity
            // create send params for the Layer Zero oapp
            uint256 conversionRate = ASDOFT(_payload._cantoAsdAddress).decimalConversionRate();
            uint256 amountWithoutDust = (_amount / conversionRate) * conversionRate;
            bytes memory sendOptions = OptionsBuilder.addExecutorLzReceiveOption(OptionsBuilder.newOptions(), 200000, 0);
            SendParam memory sendParams = SendParam({dstEid: _payload._dstLzEid, to: OFTComposeMsgCodec.addressToBytes32(_payload._dstReceiver), amountLD: amountWithoutDust, minAmountLD: amountWithoutDust, extraOptions: sendOptions, composeMsg: "", oftCmd: ""});
            MessagingFee memory fee = MessagingFee({nativeFee: _payload._feeForSend, lzTokenFee: 0});
```

findings.technical_details:
`ASDRouter.lzCompose` is invoked by a LayerZero executor in a separate transaction after the cross-chain OFT transfer has already credited tokens to `ASDRouter` during `lzReceive`. Because the composed message payload is immutable once sent on the source chain, `lzCompose` is designed to never revert; instead, every fallible step in `ASDRouter` (`_swapOFTForNote`, `_depositNoteToASDVault`, and the cross-chain `IOFT.send` path in `_sendASD`) uses a low-level `.call` and routes failures to `_refundToken` so the user receives a refund on Canto.

However, in `ASDRouter._sendASD` (`contracts/asd/asdRouter.sol`), the local Canto payout branch (`_payload._dstLzEid == cantoLzEID`) violates this settlement pattern:

```solidity
if (_payload._dstLzEid == cantoLzEID) {
    // just transfer the ASD tokens to the destination receiver
    emit ASDSent(_guid, _payload._dstReceiver, _payload._cantoAsdAddress, _amount, _payload._dstLzEid, false);
    ASDOFT(_payload._cantoAsdAddress).transfer(_payload._dstReceiver, _amount);
