findings.technical_details:
In `LockManager.sol`, players can lock native ETH (`_tokenContract == address(0)`) either directly via `lock` or by having another address call `lockOnBehalf` for them. Once the lock duration (`lockedToken.unlockTime`) expires, the player must call `unlock` to retrieve their principal.

At line 420, when `_tokenContract == address(0)`, `LockManager.unlock` transfers the native ETH back to `msg.sender` using Solidity's `.transfer()`:

```solidity
if (_tokenContract == address(0)) {
    payable(msg.sender).transfer(_quantity);
}
```

The `.transfer()` primitive imposes a hardcoded 2,300 gas limit on the call to `msg.sender`. Furthermore, `unlock` sends funds exclusively to `msg.sender` (which must be the registered main account that owns the lock, as sub-accounts cannot unlock and there is no `unlockTo` or recipient parameter).

If a player is a smart contract (such as a Gnosis Safe multisig, an ERC-4337 smart account, or a proxy wallet), its `receive()` or `fallback()` function typically performs storage reads, event emissions, or delegatecalls that require more than 2,300 gas. While such a contract can seamlessly register and lock ETH (or have ETH locked on its behalf via `lockOnBehalf`), every call to `unlock` will fail with an out-of-gas revert during `payable(msg.sender).transfer(_quantity)`. Consequently, the player's ETH is permanently frozen in `LockManager`.

findings.remediation:
Replace `payable(msg.sender).transfer(_quantity)` with a low-level `.call{value: _quantity}("")` and verify the returned success boolean (note that `unlock` is already protected by the `nonReentrant` modifier and follows the checks-effects-interactions pattern with respect to `lockedToken.quantity`).

```solidity
    function unlock(
        address _tokenContract,
        uint256 _quantity
    ) external notPaused nonReentrant {
        LockedToken storage lockedToken = lockedTokens[msg.sender][
            _tokenContract
        ];
        if (lockedToken.quantity < _quantity)
            revert InsufficientLockAmountError();
        if (lockedToken.unlockTime > uint32(block.timestamp))
            revert TokenStillLockedError();

        // force harvest to make sure that they get the schnibbles that they are entitled to
        accountManager.forceHarvest(msg.sender);

        lockedToken.quantity -= _quantity;

        // send token
        if (_tokenContract == address(0)) {
            (bool success, ) = payable(msg.sender).call{value: _quantity}("");
            if (!success) revert LockManagerInvalidCallError();
        } else {
            IERC20 token = IERC20(_tokenContract);
            token.transfer(msg.sender, _quantity);
        }

        emit Unlocked(msg.sender, _tokenContract, _quantity);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
`VoteWeighting._getSum()` checkpoints the global sum of nominee weights week-by-week from the last recorded timestamp `timeSum` up to the next week boundary following `block.timestamp`:

```solidity
function _getSum() internal returns (uint256) {
    // t is always > 0 as it is set in the constructor
    uint256 t = timeSum;
    Point memory pt = pointsSum[t];
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < MAX_NUM_WEEKS; i++) {
        if (t > block.timestamp) {
            break;
        }
        t += WEEK;
        ...
        pointsSum[t] = pt;
        if (t > block.timestamp) {
            timeSum = t;
        }
    }
    return pt.bias;
}
```

The loop is capped at `MAX_NUM_WEEKS = 53` iterations to bound gas consumption per call. In each iteration where `t <= block.timestamp`, `t` is incremented by `WEEK` and the decayed `Point` is stored at `pointsSum[t]`. However, the storage cursor `timeSum` is only updated at lines 243–245 if `t > block.timestamp`.

If `block.timestamp - timeSum >= MAX_NUM_WEEKS * WEEK` (53 weeks), then even after all 53 iterations of the loop, the final value of `t` is `timeSum + 53 * WEEK <= block.timestamp`. Consequently:
1. The condition `if (t > block.timestamp)` at line 243 never evaluates to `true`, so `timeSum` is never updated in storage.
2. Subsequent calls to `checkpoint()`, `checkpointNominee()`, or `nomineeRelativeWeightWrite()` read the same unchanged `timeSum` at line 225 and recompute the exact same 53 weeks (`timeSum + WEEK` through `timeSum + 53 * WEEK`), making it impossible to catch up across multiple transactions.
3. `_getSum()` returns the stale `pt.bias` at `timeSum + 53 * WEEK` instead of the bias at `nextTime = (block.timestamp + WEEK) / WEEK * WEEK`, and `pointsSum[w]` remains unpopulated (`0`) for all weeks `w > timeSum + 53 * WEEK`.
4. Any subsequent call to `voteForNomineeWeights()` writes `pointsSum[nextTime].bias` using the stale `_getSum()` return value without decaying intermediate slopes, and still leaves `timeSum` stuck in the past.

findings.remediation:
Update `timeSum = t` outside the `if (t > block.timestamp)` check (or after the loop) so that when more than `MAX_NUM_WEEKS` have elapsed, each call to `_getSum()` advances `timeSum` by `MAX_NUM_WEEKS` weeks until it catches up to `block.timestamp` (and apply the analogous fix to `timeWeight[nomineeHash]` in `_getWeight()`).

```solidity
    function _getSum() internal returns (uint256) {
        // t is always > 0 as it is set in the constructor
        uint256 t = timeSum;
        Point memory pt = pointsSum[t];
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < MAX_NUM_WEEKS; i++) {
            if (t > block.timestamp) {
                break;
            }
            t += WEEK;
            uint256 dBias = pt.slope * WEEK;
            if (pt.bias > dBias) {
                pt.bias -= dBias;
                uint256 dSlope = changesSum[t];
                pt.slope -= dSlope;
            } else {
                pt.bias = 0;
                pt.slope = 0;
            }

            pointsSum[t] = pt;
            timeSum = t;
        }
        return pt.bias;
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
`VoteWeighting` tracks each nominee's voting weight in `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][t]` and the aggregate weight across all active nominees in `pointsSum[t]`. Each `Point` stores a `bias` (current weight at timestamp `t`) and a `slope` (rate of linear weight decay per second), while `changesWeight` and `changesSum` store scheduled slope reductions at lock expiration timestamps (`lockEnd`).

In `_getSum()` (lines 223–248), the contract checkpoints missed weeks by advancing `t` in increments of `WEEK` and applying linear decay to `pointsSum[t]`:

```solidity
t += WEEK;
uint256 dBias = pt.slope * WEEK;
if (pt.bias > dBias) {
    pt.bias -= dBias;
    uint256 dSlope = changesSum[t];
    pt.slope -= dSlope;
} else {
    pt.bias = 0;
    pt.slope = 0;
}

pointsSum[t] = pt;
```

This decay logic relies on the invariant that `pointsSum[t].bias` equals the sum of remaining biases of all active votes contributing to `pointsSum[t].slope`, and that `pointsSum[t].slope` equals the sum of active slopes scheduled to expire in `changesSum`.

However, when `removeNominee()` is called (lines 603–612), it subtracts the removed nominee's entire remaining bias (`oldWeight`) from `pointsSum[nextTime].bias` (`pointsSum[nextTime].bias = oldSum - oldWeight`), while leaving `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` untouched (still containing the removed nominee's slope). Moreover, if voters subsequently reclaim their voting power from the removed nominee, `changesSum[oldSlope.end]` is decremented by `oldSlope.slope` even though `oldSlope.slope` was never removed from `pointsSum[nextTime].slope`.

When `_getSum()` subsequently executes lines 231–242 for future weeks:

1. **Accelerated Aggregate Bias Decay**: On line 232, `dBias = pt.slope * WEEK` still includes the removed nominee's slope. Line 234 (`pt.bias -= dBias`) therefore deducts the removed nominee's weekly decay a second time from `pointsSum[t].bias`, even though the removed nominee's full bias (`oldWeight`) was already removed from `pointsSum[nextTime].bias`. As a result, `pointsSum[t].bias` becomes strictly smaller than the sum of active nominees' biases (`pointsWeight[nomineeHash][t].bias`).
2. **Premature Zero-Weight Transition**: Because `pt.slope` is artificially high relative to `pt.bias`, the check `if (pt.bias > dBias)` on line 233 fails prematurely when `pt.bias <= dBias`, entering the `else` branch (lines 237–240) and resetting both `pt.bias = 0` and `pt.slope = 0` while active nominees still have positive biases and slopes.
3. **Permanent Phantom Slope After Slope-Change Cancellation**: If a voter revokes their voting power for the removed nominee, `changesSum[oldSlope.end]` is reduced by `oldSlope.slope`, so line 236 (`pt.slope -= dSlope`) fails to remove `oldSlope.slope` at `t = oldSlope.end`, leaving a permanent phantom slope in `pointsSum` that drains any remaining aggregate bias to zero.

Because `_nomineeRelativeWeight()` computes `weight = 1e18 * nomineeWeight / totalSum` where `totalSum = pointsSum[t].bias`:
- While `0 < totalSum < nomineeWeight`, a nominee's `relativeWeight` exceeds `1e18` (100%), causing `Dispenser.calculateStakingIncentives` to over-allocate staking incentives.
- Once `pt.bias <= dBias` triggers the `else` branch in `_getSum()`, `totalSum` becomes `0`, causing `_nomineeRelativeWeight()` to return `0` for all active nominees and freezing reward distribution until a new vote recalibrates `pointsSum`.

findings.remediation:
Ensure that `pointsSum` bias, slope, and scheduled slope changes (`changesSum`) remain synchronized with active nominees when checkpointing in `_getSum()` and when removing nominees:
1. When removing a nominee, subtract `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope` from `pointsSum[nextTime].slope`, zero `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope`, and ensure scheduled slope expirations for removed nominees do not corrupt `changesSum` (for example, by tracking removed nominees' scheduled slope changes or recomputing `_getSum()` from active nominees' weights).
2. In `_getSum()`, guard `pt.slope` subtraction against underflow when applying `changesSum[t]`.

```solidity
    function _getSum() internal returns (uint256) {
        // t is always > 0 as it is set in the constructor
        uint256 t = timeSum;
        Point memory pt = pointsSum[t];
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < MAX_NUM_WEEKS; i++) {
            if (t > block.timestamp) {
                break;
            }
            t += WEEK;
            uint256 dBias = pt.slope * WEEK;
            if (pt.bias > dBias) {
                pt.bias -= dBias;
                uint256 dSlope = changesSum[t];
                pt.slope = _maxAndSub(pt.slope, dSlope);
            } else {
                pt.bias = 0;
                pt.slope = 0;
            }

            pointsSum[t] = pt;
            if (t > block.timestamp) {
                timeSum = t;
            }
        }
        return pt.bias;
    }
```

And in `removeNominee`, synchronize `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` and `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope`:

```solidity
        // Set nominee weight and slope to zero
        uint256 oldWeight = _getWeight(account, chainId);
        uint256 oldSum = _getSum();
        uint256 nextTime = (block.timestamp + WEEK) / WEEK * WEEK;
        uint256 oldSlope = pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope;
        pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias = 0;
        pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope = 0;
        timeWeight[nomineeHash] = nextTime;

        // Account for the sum weight and slope change
        uint256 newSum = _maxAndSub(oldSum, oldWeight);
        pointsSum[nextTime].bias = newSum;
        pointsSum[nextTime].slope = _maxAndSub(pointsSum[nextTime].slope, oldSlope);
        timeSum = nextTime;
```

Update `timeWeight[nomineeHash]` and `timeSum` after the loop in `_getWeight` and `_getSum` so multi-transaction catch-up can progress, and ensure in `voteForNomineeWeights` that both `timeWeight[nomineeHash]` and `timeSum` have fully caught up to `nextTime` before scheduling changes at `nextTime`:

```solidity
        VotedSlope memory newSlope = VotedSlope({
            slope: uint256(uint128(userSlope)) * weight / MAX_WEIGHT,
            end: lockEnd,
            power: weight
        });

        uint256 newBias = newSlope.slope * (lockEnd - nextTime);

        uint256 powerUsed = voteUserPower[msg.sender];
        powerUsed = powerUsed + newSlope.power - oldSlope.power;
        voteUserPower[msg.sender] = powerUsed;
        if (powerUsed > MAX_WEIGHT) {
            revert Overflow(powerUsed, MAX_WEIGHT);
        }

        // Remove old and schedule new slope changes
        // Remove slope changes for old slopes
        // Schedule recording of initial slope for nextTime
        pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias = _maxAndSub(_getWeight(account, chainId) + newBias, oldBias);
        pointsSum[nextTime].bias = _maxAndSub(_getSum() + newBias, oldBias);
        if (timeWeight[nomineeHash] < nextTime) {
            revert Underflow(timeWeight[nomineeHash], nextTime);
        }
        if (timeSum < nextTime) {
            revert Underflow(timeSum, nextTime);
        }
```

findings.technical_details:
`VoteWeighting` tracks individual nominee weights (`pointsWeight[nomineeHash]`) and the aggregate weight across all nominees (`pointsSum`) as linear functions of time parameterized by `bias` and `slope`, with future slope expirations scheduled at lock-end timestamps (`oldSlope.end`, `newSlope.end`) in `changesWeight` and `changesSum`.

For the weekly checkpoint loops in `_getWeight` (lines 267–286) and `_getSum` (lines 227–246) to remain solvent and accurate, the contract relies on two fundamental ledger invariants at every week boundary `t`:
1. The aggregate active slope `pointsSum[t].slope` must equal the sum of active slopes across all non-removed nominees, and must equal the sum of all scheduled slope reductions $\sum_{t' > t} \text{changesSum}[t']$.
2. For each nominee, `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][t].slope` must equal $\sum_{t' > t} \text{changesWeight}[\text{nomineeHash}][t']$.

In the highlighted block of `voteForNomineeWeights` (lines 534–549), replacing an existing vote allocation (`oldSlope`) with a new allocation (`newSlope`) updates active slopes and scheduled slope changes with incompatible semantics:

```solidity
if (oldSlope.end > nextTime) {
    pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope =
        _maxAndSub(pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope, oldSlope.slope);
    pointsSum[nextTime].slope = _maxAndSub(pointsSum[nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope, oldSlope.slope);
} else {
    pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope += newSlope.slope;
    pointsSum[nextTime].slope += newSlope.slope;
}
if (oldSlope.end > block.timestamp) {
    // Cancel old slope changes if they still didn't happen
    changesWeight[nomineeHash][oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope;
    changesSum[oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope;
}
// Add slope changes for new slopes
changesWeight[nomineeHash][newSlope.end] += newSlope.slope;
changesSum[newSlope.end] += newSlope.slope;
```

Specifically:
1. **Divergence via Removal and Revocation / Missed Checkpoints**:
   - When `removeNominee` (lines 603–612) removes a nominee, it zeroes `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias` and subtracts `oldWeight` from `pointsSum[nextTime].bias`, but leaves `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` (and `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope`) unchanged.
   - When voters subsequently call `revokeRemovedNomineeVotingPower` (lines 658–661), `changesSum[oldSlope.end]` and `changesWeight[nomineeHash][oldSlope.end]` are decremented by `oldSlope.slope`, but `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` is still not decremented.
   - Because `pointsSum.bias` was reduced by the removed nominee's bias while `pointsSum.slope` still includes the removed nominee's slope, `_getSum()` decays `pointsSum.bias` at an inflated rate (`pt.slope * WEEK`). Consequently, `pt.bias <= dBias` is reached prematurely in `_getSum()` (lines 233–240), resetting both `pointsSum[t].bias = 0` and `pointsSum[t].slope = 0` while active nominees still have positive slopes and biases in `pointsWeight`.
   - Similarly, if a nominee goes uncheckpointed for more than `MAX_NUM_WEEKS` (53 weeks) while a voter holds a multi-year `veOLAS` lock (`oldSlope.end > nextTime`), `_getWeight` does not reach `nextTime`, leaving `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope` at `0`.

2. **Incompatible Saturating Update vs. Exact Cancellation (Lines 534–549)**:
   - When an existing voter on an active nominee updates their vote while `oldSlope.end > nextTime` after `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` (or `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope`) has dropped below the true sum of active slopes:
     - Line 537 computes `pointsSum[nextTime].slope = _maxAndSub(pointsSum[nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope, oldSlope.slope)`. Because `oldSlope.slope` was already wiped out when `pointsSum.slope` saturated to `0` (or was reduced), subtracting `oldSlope.slope` again via `_maxAndSub` either clamps `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` to `0` (if `pointsSum[nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope <= oldSlope.slope`) or deducts `oldSlope.slope` from `newSlope.slope` and/or other voters' active slopes.
     - Meanwhile, line 545 (`changesSum[oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope`) cancels the old scheduled reduction, and line 549 (`changesSum[newSlope.end] += newSlope.slope`) schedules the full `newSlope.slope` to be subtracted at `newSlope.end`.
   - As a result, `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` becomes strictly smaller than the sum of scheduled reductions in `changesSum` (e.g., `pointsSum[nextTime].slope == 0` or `newSlope.slope - oldSlope.slope`, while `changesSum[newSlope.end] == newSlope.slope`).
   - In subsequent weeks, `pointsSum.bias` decays too slowly in `_getSum()` (skewing `_nomineeRelativeWeight`), and when `_getSum()` reaches a week `t` where `pt.bias > dBias` and `changesSum[t] > pt.slope`, line 236 (`pt.slope -= dSlope`) reverts with an arithmetic underflow (Panic `0x11`), permanently bricking `_getSum()` and all state-changing operations that call it.

findings.remediation:
Ensure that active slopes (`pointsWeight` and `pointsSum`) and scheduled slope changes (`changesWeight` and `changesSum`) remain synchronized at all times so that replacing an active vote in `voteForNomineeWeights` always cancels the exact active slope contribution that is still present in both `pointsWeight` and `pointsSum`, and ensure removed nominees' slopes and scheduled changes are consistently removed when `removeNominee` / `revokeRemovedNomineeVotingPower` are executed.

```solidity
        if (oldSlope.end > nextTime) {
            pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope =
                pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope - oldSlope.slope;
            pointsSum[nextTime].slope = pointsSum[nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope - oldSlope.slope;

            // Cancel old slope changes if they still didn't happen
            changesWeight[nomineeHash][oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope;
            changesSum[oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope;
        } else {
            pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope += newSlope.slope;
            pointsSum[nextTime].slope += newSlope.slope;
        }
        // Add slope changes for new slopes
        changesWeight[nomineeHash][newSlope.end] += newSlope.slope;
        changesSum[newSlope.end] += newSlope.slope;
```

Additionally, ensure that `removeNominee` and `revokeRemovedNomineeVotingPower` maintain the invariant that `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` equals the sum of active non-removed nominee slopes and matches the remaining scheduled reductions in `changesSum`.

findings.technical_details:
In `VoteWeighting.voteForNomineeWeights`, all voting weight and sum checkpoints are scheduled on weekly epoch boundaries rounded up to the next week:
```solidity
uint256 nextTime = (block.timestamp + WEEK) / WEEK * WEEK;
```
Similarly, lock expiration timestamps (`lockEnd`) in `veOLAS` are rounded to whole `WEEK` multiples. When a user votes, their vote's linear decay rate (`newSlope.slope`) and lock expiration (`newSlope.end = lockEnd`) are recorded in `voteUserSlopes[msg.sender][nomineeHash]`, and the slope reduction at lock expiry is scheduled in `changesWeight[nomineeHash][newSlope.end]` and `changesSum[newSlope.end]`.

When the user subsequently updates their vote in `voteForNomineeWeights`:
1. Lines 510 and 534 check `if (oldSlope.end > nextTime)` to determine whether the user's previous slope (`oldSlope`) is still active after the upcoming weekly checkpoint `nextTime`:
   ```solidity
   pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias = _maxAndSub(_getWeight(account, chainId) + newBias, oldBias);
   pointsSum[nextTime].bias = _maxAndSub(_getSum() + newBias, oldBias);
   if (oldSlope.end > nextTime) {
       pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope =
           _maxAndSub(pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope, oldSlope.slope);
       pointsSum[nextTime].slope = _maxAndSub(pointsSum[nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope, oldSlope.slope);
   } else {
       pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope += newSlope.slope;
       pointsSum[nextTime].slope += newSlope.slope;
   }
   ```
2. Immediately afterward, lines 542–546 check `if (oldSlope.end > block.timestamp)` instead of `if (oldSlope.end > nextTime)` to cancel the previously scheduled slope changes:
   ```solidity
   if (oldSlope.end > block.timestamp) {
       // Cancel old slope changes if they still didn't happen
       changesWeight[nomineeHash][oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope;
       changesSum[oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope;
   }
   ```

Because `oldSlope.end` is a multiple of `WEEK` and `nextTime = (block.timestamp + WEEK) / WEEK * WEEK`, during the final week before the user's old lock expires (`oldSlope.end - WEEK <= block.timestamp < oldSlope.end`), `oldSlope.end == nextTime` while `oldSlope.end > block.timestamp` is `true`.

If the user extends their lock in `veOLAS` (so the new `lockEnd > nextTime` passes the `nextTime >= lockEnd` check at line 492) and calls `voteForNomineeWeights` during `[oldSlope.end - WEEK, oldSlope.end)`:
- `oldSlope.end > nextTime` evaluates to `false`, so lines 538–541 do not subtract `oldSlope.slope` from `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope` or `pointsSum[nextTime].slope`, relying on the scheduled slope change at `t = nextTime` (`oldSlope.end`) in `_getWeight` and `_getSum` to deduct `oldSlope.slope`.
- However, `oldSlope.end > block.timestamp` at line 542 evaluates to `true`, so lines 544–545 subtract `oldSlope.slope` from `changesWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime]` and `changesSum[nextTime]`.

If `_getWeight` or `_getSum` has Already reached `nextTime` at lines 532–533, the slope change at `oldSlope.end == nextTime` has already been applied to `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope` and `pointsSum[nextTime].slope`, making the mutation of `changesWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime]` and `changesSum[nextTime]` inconsistent with the checkpointed state. Conversely, if `timeWeight[nomineeHash]` or `timeSum` lags behind `nextTime` by more than `MAX_NUM_WEEKS` (so the bounded loop in `_getWeight` or `_getSum` at lines 532–533 has not yet reached `nextTime`), lines 544–545 cancel `oldSlope.slope` from `changesWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime]` and `changesSum[nextTime]` before the checkpoint loop reaches `nextTime`, while lines 538–541 also did not subtract `oldSlope.slope`. When the checkpoint loop subsequently advances across `nextTime`, `oldSlope.slope` is never subtracted from `pt.slope`, permanently inflating the nominee and total sum slopes.

findings.remediation:
Check `oldSlope.end > nextTime` consistently for both updating the slope at `nextTime` and cancelling scheduled slope changes at `oldSlope.end`:

```solidity
        if (oldSlope.end > nextTime) {
            pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope =
                _maxAndSub(pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope, oldSlope.slope);
            pointsSum[nextTime].slope = _maxAndSub(pointsSum[nextTime].slope + newSlope.slope, oldSlope.slope);

            // Cancel old slope changes if they still didn't happen
            changesWeight[nomineeHash][oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope;
            changesSum[oldSlope.end] -= oldSlope.slope;
        } else {
            pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope += newSlope.slope;
            pointsSum[nextTime].slope += newSlope.slope;
        }
        // Add slope changes for new slopes
        changesWeight[nomineeHash][newSlope.end] += newSlope.slope;
        changesSum[newSlope.end] += newSlope.slope;
```

findings.technical_details:
In `VoteWeighting.sol`, both `_getWeight(account, chainId)` and `_getSum()` catch up missed weekly checkpoints using a bounded loop of `MAX_NUM_WEEKS = 53` iterations:

```solidity
uint256 t = timeWeight[nomineeHash];
Point memory pt = pointsWeight[nomineeHash][t];
for (uint256 i = 0; i < MAX_NUM_WEEKS; i++) {
    if (t > block.timestamp) {
        break;
    }
    t += WEEK;
    ...
    pointsWeight[nomineeHash][t] = pt;
    if (t > block.timestamp) {
        timeWeight[nomineeHash] = t;
    }
}
return pt.bias;
```

While `MAX_NUM_WEEKS` is 53 weeks (~1 year), `veOLAS` locks can last up to 4 years (~208 weeks), and an individual nominee may go more than 53 weeks without being voted on or checkpointed via `checkpointNominee`. Meanwhile, `timeSum` is updated whenever any nominee is voted for or checkpointed via `checkpoint()`.

When `removeNominee(account, chainId)` is called:

```solidity
uint256 oldWeight = _getWeight(account, chainId);
uint256 oldSum = _getSum();
uint256 nextTime = (block.timestamp + WEEK) / WEEK * WEEK;
pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias = 0;
timeWeight[nomineeHash] = nextTime;

// Account for the the sum weight change
uint256 newSum = oldSum - oldWeight;
pointsSum[nextTime].bias = newSum;
timeSum = nextTime;
```

If `timeWeight[nomineeHash]` is more than `MAX_NUM_WEEKS` behind `block.timestamp`:
1. `_getWeight(account, chainId)` only advances `t` by 53 weeks to `t_partial = timeWeight[nomineeHash] + 53 * WEEK <= block.timestamp < nextTime`. Because `t` never exceeds `block.timestamp`, `timeWeight[nomineeHash]` is not updated inside `_getWeight`, and `oldWeight` is the nominee's historical bias at `t_partial` rather than at `nextTime`.
2. `_getSum()` (if `timeSum` was checkpointed within the last 53 weeks) advances `timeSum` all the way to `nextTime` and returns `oldSum` evaluated at `nextTime`, where the removed nominee's weight contribution in `pointsSum` has already decayed for the additional weeks between `t_partial` and `nextTime` (or decayed to zero).
3. `newSum = oldSum - oldWeight` subtracts the stale, less-decayed `oldWeight` (from `t_partial`) from `oldSum` (at `nextTime`). If `oldSum >= oldWeight` due to other nominees' weights, `pointsSum[nextTime].bias` is permanently understated by the decay difference; if `oldSum < oldWeight`, the subtraction underflows and reverts.
4. `timeWeight[nomineeHash] = nextTime` (and `timeSum = nextTime` if `timeSum` was also > 53 weeks behind) forcibly jumps the last scheduled checkpoint timestamp to `nextTime`, leaving all intermediate weeks `(t_partial, nextTime)` unpopulated (`bias = 0`) and permanently uncheckpointable.

findings.remediation:
Ensure that both `timeWeight[nomineeHash]` and `timeSum` have fully caught up to `nextTime` before computing `newSum` and updating the checkpoints in `removeNominee` (either by looping `_getWeight` and `_getSum` until caught up or by reverting if they have not reached `nextTime`):

```solidity
        uint256 nextTime = (block.timestamp + WEEK) / WEEK * WEEK;

        // Advance nominee weight and total sum until both reach nextTime
        uint256 oldWeight = _getWeight(account, chainId);
        while (timeWeight[nomineeHash] < nextTime) {
            oldWeight = _getWeight(account, chainId);
        }

        uint256 oldSum = _getSum();
        while (timeSum < nextTime) {
            oldSum = _getSum();
        }

        // Set nominee weight to zero
        pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias = 0;
        timeWeight[nomineeHash] = nextTime;

        // Account for the sum weight change
        uint256 newSum = oldSum - oldWeight;
        pointsSum[nextTime].bias = newSum;
        timeSum = nextTime;
```

findings.technical_details:
In `VoteWeighting`, each nominee's voting weight and the global total weight are tracked as linear decay functions (`Point { uint256 bias; uint256 slope; }`) at weekly checkpoints (`pointsWeight[nomineeHash][t]` and `pointsSum[t]`). When users vote in `voteForNomineeWeights` (lines 532–549), both the initial bias `newBias = newSlope.slope * (lockEnd - nextTime)` and the decay rate `newSlope.slope` are added to `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime]` and `pointsSum[nextTime]`, while slope expiration is scheduled in `changesWeight[nomineeHash][newSlope.end]` and `changesSum[newSlope.end]`.

To maintain the invariant that `pointsSum[t].bias` equals the sum of `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][t].bias` across all active nominees for all future weeks `t >= nextTime`, `pointsSum[t].slope` must equal the sum of active nominees' slopes.

In `VoteWeighting.removeNominee` (lines 603–612):
```solidity
uint256 oldWeight = _getWeight(account, chainId);
uint256 oldSum = _getSum();
uint256 nextTime = (block.timestamp + WEEK) / WEEK * WEEK;
pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias = 0;
timeWeight[nomineeHash] = nextTime;

// Account for the the sum weight change
uint256 newSum = oldSum - oldWeight;
pointsSum[nextTime].bias = newSum;
timeSum = nextTime;
```
1. `_getWeight(account, chainId)` and `_getSum()` advance the nominee and global checkpoints to `nextTime`.
2. Line 606 zeros `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias`, and lines 610–611 subtract the nominee's entire remaining bias `oldWeight` from `pointsSum[nextTime].bias`.
3. However, `removeNominee` does not zero `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope` and does not subtract `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope` from `pointsSum[nextTime].slope`.
4. Even if voters later call `revokeRemovedNomineeVotingPower` (lines 641–668), that function only subtracts `oldSlope.slope` from `changesWeight[nomineeHash][oldSlope.end]` and `changesSum[oldSlope.end]`; it never reduces `pointsSum[nextTime].slope`.

As a result, in every week after `nextTime`, `_getSum()` (lines 232–240) computes `uint256 dBias = pt.slope * WEEK` using a `pt.slope` that still includes the removed nominee's slope. The removed nominee's weight is thus deducted a second time week-over-week from `pointsSum`, causing `pointsSum[t].bias` to fall below the actual sum of active nominees' weights, or drop to `0` prematurely when `pt.bias <= dBias`.

findings.remediation:
In `removeNominee`, subtract `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope` from `pointsSum[nextTime].slope` and zero `pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope` (while ensuring `revokeRemovedNomineeVotingPower` and scheduled slope expirations in `changesSum` remain consistent with the slope removed at `nextTime`).

```solidity
        // Set nominee weight and slope to zero
        uint256 oldWeight = _getWeight(account, chainId);
        uint256 oldSum = _getSum();
        uint256 nextTime = (block.timestamp + WEEK) / WEEK * WEEK;
        uint256 oldSlope = pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope;
        pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].bias = 0;
        pointsWeight[nomineeHash][nextTime].slope = 0;
        timeWeight[nomineeHash] = nextTime;

        // Account for the sum weight and slope change
        uint256 newSum = _maxAndSub(oldSum, oldWeight);
        pointsSum[nextTime].bias = newSum;
        pointsSum[nextTime].slope = _maxAndSub(pointsSum[nextTime].slope, oldSlope);
        timeSum = nextTime;
```

Only perform the swap and `mapNomineeIds` update when the removed nominee is not the last element in `setNominees` (i.e., `id < setNominees.length - 1`), or clear `mapNomineeIds[nomineeHash] = 0` after updating `mapNomineeIds[replacedNomineeHash]`.

```solidity
