findings.technical_details:
When an isolated loan enters liquidation auction (`Constants.LOAN_STATUS_AUCTION`), `DataTypes.IsolateLoanData` records `bidStartTimestamp`, the timestamp at which the first bid was placed:

```solidity
struct IsolateLoanData {
  address reserveAsset;
  uint256 scaledAmount;
  uint8 reserveGroup;
  uint8 loanStatus;
  uint40 bidStartTimestamp;
  address firstBidder;
  address lastBidder;
  uint256 bidAmount;
}
```

However, neither the auction duration at the time of auction initiation nor the resulting `auctionEndTimestamp` is stored in `IsolateLoanData`. In `IsolateLogic.executeIsolateLiquidate` (`src/libraries/logic/IsolateLogic.sol`), the settlement boundary check computes the auction end timestamp on the fly from `nftAssetData.auctionDuration`:

```solidity
vars.auctionEndTimestamp = loanData.bidStartTimestamp + nftAssetData.auctionDuration;
require(block.timestamp > vars.auctionEndTimestamp, Errors.ISOLATE_BID_AUCTION_DURATION_NOT_END);
```

Because `nftAssetData.auctionDuration` is a mutable asset configuration parameter on `DataTypes.AssetData`, any update to `nftAssetData.auctionDuration` while auctions are active immediately alters `vars.auctionEndTimestamp` for all existing loans in `LOAN_STATUS_AUCTION`:

1. **Decreasing `auctionDuration`**: Active auctions have their deadlines retroactively shortened. If the new `loanData.bidStartTimestamp + nftAssetData.auctionDuration` falls below the current `block.timestamp`, the auction becomes immediately liquidatable via `executeIsolateLiquidate`. This strips borrowers of their remaining time window to repay/redeem their NFT collateral and prevents other liquidators from placing higher competing bids.
2. **Increasing `auctionDuration`**: Auctions that are near completion—or have already passed their original `auctionEndTimestamp` but have not yet been settled via `executeIsolateLiquidate`—have their deadlines extended into the future. Calls to `executeIsolateLiquidate` will revert with `Errors.ISOLATE_BID_AUCTION_DURATION_NOT_END`, locking the winning bidder's capital in the pool for longer than agreed and potentially re-opening an expired auction to further actions.

findings.remediation:
Store the auction end timestamp (e.g., `bidEndTimestamp = uint40(block.timestamp) + nftAssetData.auctionDuration`) directly in `DataTypes.IsolateLoanData` when the auction is initiated, and check `block.timestamp > loanData.bidEndTimestamp` in `IsolateLogic.executeIsolateLiquidate`.

In `src/libraries/logic/IsolateLogic.sol`:

```solidity
      ValidateLogic.validateIsolateLiquidateLoan(params, debtGroupData, loanData);

      vars.auctionEndTimestamp = loanData.bidEndTimestamp;
      require(block.timestamp > vars.auctionEndTimestamp, Errors.ISOLATE_BID_AUCTION_DURATION_NOT_END);

      vars.normalizedIndex = InterestLogic.getNormalizedBorrowDebt(debtAssetData, debtGroupData);
```

findings.technical_details:
In `IsolateLogic.executeIsolateLiquidate` (`src/libraries/logic/IsolateLogic.sol`), each auctioned NFT loan's full accrued debt is computed at line 427:

```solidity
vars.borrowAmount = loanData.scaledAmount.rayMul(vars.normalizedIndex);
```

When the winning bid is less than the accrued debt at the time of liquidation (for example, because interest continued to accrue during `nftAssetData.auctionDuration` after a bid equal to the initial debt was placed), lines 430–432 record the debt shortfall that the liquidator must cover:

```solidity
if (loanData.bidAmount < vars.borrowAmount) {
  vars.extraBorrowAmounts[vars.nidx] = vars.borrowAmount - loanData.bidAmount;
}
```

When `loanData.bidAmount > vars.borrowAmount`, lines 435–437 and 443–445 immediately refund the surplus `loanData.bidAmount - vars.borrowAmount` from `debtAssetData.totalBidAmout` to the borrower via `VaultLogic.erc20TransferOutBidAmount`.

Then, lines 447–449 accumulate the batch totals:

```solidity
vars.totalBorrowAmount += vars.borrowAmount;
vars.totalBidAmount += loanData.bidAmount;
vars.totalExtraAmount += vars.extraBorrowAmounts[vars.nidx];
```

After the loop, for every liquidated loan, the amount of escrowed bid remaining in `debtAssetData.totalBidAmout` to cover the debt is `min(loanData.bidAmount, vars.borrowAmount) = vars.borrowAmount - vars.extraBorrowAmounts[vars.nidx]`. Summing across all loans in the liquidation call, the escrowed bid amount remaining in `debtAssetData.totalBidAmout` for these loans is:

$$\text{Escrowed Bid Covering Debt} = \text{vars.totalBorrowAmount} - \text{vars.totalExtraAmount}$$

And the remaining shortfall `vars.totalExtraAmount` is transferred directly from `params.msgSender` into pool liquidity at lines 466–469 via `VaultLogic.erc20TransferInLiquidity(debtAssetData, params.msgSender, vars.totalExtraAmount)`.

However, lines 461–469 perform the settlement accounting as if `vars.totalBorrowAmount` did not already include `vars.totalExtraAmount`:

1. **Utilization and Interest Rate Distortion (Line 461):**
   ```solidity
   InterestLogic.updateInterestRates(poolData, debtAssetData, (vars.totalBorrowAmount + vars.totalExtraAmount), 0);
   ```
   `updateInterestRates` adds `liquidityAdded` to `assetData.availableLiquidity`. Because `vars.totalBorrowAmount` is the total debt repaid (comprising `vars.totalBorrowAmount - vars.totalExtraAmount` from bid escrow plus `vars.totalExtraAmount` from the liquidator), passing `vars.totalBorrowAmount + vars.totalExtraAmount` double-counts `vars.totalExtraAmount`, inflating the denominator `availableLiquidityPlusDebt` and understating the asset utilization rate and borrow/supply interest rates.

2. **Bid Escrow Underflow / Cross-Auction Contamination and Available Liquidity Inflation (Lines 464 & 468):**
   ```solidity
   VaultLogic.erc20TransferOutBidAmountToLiqudity(debtAssetData, vars.totalBorrowAmount);

   if (vars.totalExtraAmount > 0) {
     // transfer underlying asset from liquidator to pool
     VaultLogic.erc20TransferInLiquidity(debtAssetData, params.msgSender, vars.totalExtraAmount);
   }
   ```
   - `erc20TransferOutBidAmountToLiqudity` subtracts `vars.totalBorrowAmount` from `debtAssetData.totalBidAmout` and adds `vars.totalBorrowAmount` to `debtAssetData.availableLiquidity`.
   - Because these loans only have `vars.totalBorrowAmount - vars.totalExtraAmount` remaining in `debtAssetData.totalBidAmout`, subtracting `vars.totalBorrowAmount` deducts an extra `vars.totalExtraAmount` from `debtAssetData.totalBidAmout`.
   - If `debtAssetData.totalBidAmout < vars.totalBorrowAmount` (e.g., when no other auctions have escrowed bids), `assetData.totalBidAmout -= amount` reverts with an arithmetic underflow, permanently blocking `isolateLiquidate`.
   - If other active auctions have escrowed bids in `debtAssetData.totalBidAmout >= vars.totalExtraAmount`, the subtraction consumes `vars.totalExtraAmount` from those unrelated auctions' escrowed bids, and `erc20TransferInLiquidity` subsequently adds another `vars.totalExtraAmount` to `debtAssetData.availableLiquidity`. As a result, `debtAssetData.availableLiquidity` is increased by `vars.totalBorrowAmount + vars.totalExtraAmount` while only `vars.totalBorrowAmount` enters liquidity, and subsequent redemptions, outbids, or liquidations of other auctions revert due to insufficient `totalBidAmout`.

findings.remediation:
In `IsolateLogic.executeIsolateLiquidate`, pass `vars.totalBorrowAmount` (instead of `vars.totalBorrowAmount + vars.totalExtraAmount`) to `InterestLogic.updateInterestRates`, and pass `vars.totalBorrowAmount - vars.totalExtraAmount` (instead of `vars.totalBorrowAmount`) to `VaultLogic.erc20TransferOutBidAmountToLiqudity`.

```solidity
    // update interest rate according latest borrow amount (utilizaton)
    InterestLogic.updateInterestRates(poolData, debtAssetData, vars.totalBorrowAmount, 0);

    // bid already in pool and now repay the borrow but need to increase liquidity
    VaultLogic.erc20TransferOutBidAmountToLiqudity(
      debtAssetData,
      vars.totalBorrowAmount - vars.totalExtraAmount
    );

    if (vars.totalExtraAmount > 0) {
      // transfer underlying asset from liquidator to pool
      VaultLogic.erc20TransferInLiquidity(debtAssetData, params.msgSender, vars.totalExtraAmount);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
throughout the protocol, interest rates and borrow indices are denominated in `RAY` (`1e27`) and computed as `uint256` values:
- `IInterestRateModel.calculateGroupBorrowRate` returns the group borrow rate as a `uint256` in rays (`1e27`).
- `InterestLogic.getNormalizedBorrowDebt`, `QueryLogic.getAssetGroupData`, and `Events.AssetInterestBorrowDataUpdated` all handle and expose `borrowRate` and `borrowIndex` as `uint256`.

However, `DataTypes.GroupData` (lines 51–52 of `src/libraries/types/DataTypes.sol`) defines both fields as `uint128`:

```solidity
// interest state
uint128 borrowRate;
uint128 borrowIndex;
uint8 groupId;
```

Because `borrowIndex` is initialized to `1 RAY` (`10^27 ≈ 2^89.7`) and `type(uint128).max` is `2^128 - 1 ≈ 3.4028e38`, only ~38 bits of headroom (`≈ 3.4e11` multiplier) remain above the initial index value before exhausting the `uint128` range.

In `InterestLogic._updateBorrowIndex` (line 286) and `InterestLogic.updateInterestRates` (line 181), the newly computed `uint256` values are downcast via `SafeCastUpgradeable.toUint128()`:

```solidity
loopGroupData.borrowRate = vars.nextGroupBorrowRate.toUint128();
...
groupData.borrowIndex = nextBorrowIndex.toUint128();
```

In `InterestLogic.updateInterestIndexs` (lines 108–115) and `InterestLogic.updateInterestRates` (lines 173–197), the contract iterates through every group in `assetData.groupList`. Because `groupData.borrowIndex` compounds exponentially via `MathUtils.calculateCompoundedInterest` whenever `(groupData.totalScaledCrossBorrow != 0) || (groupData.totalScaledIsolateBorrow != 0)`, a group with high borrow rates (such as custom interest rate models or high-slope rate models at high utilization) and minimal dust debt will continuously compound its `borrowIndex`. Once `nextBorrowIndex > type(uint128).max` (or `nextGroupBorrowRate > type(uint128).max`), `toUint128()` reverts with `"SafeCast: value doesn't fit in 128 bits"`.

Because `InterestLogic.updateInterestIndexs` and `InterestLogic.updateInterestRates` are invoked at the start of every core asset action (`executeDepositERC20`, `executeWithdrawERC20`, `executeCrossBorrowERC20`, `executeCrossRepayERC20`, `executeIsolateBorrow`, `executeIsolateRepay`, `executeIsolateRedeem`, `executeIsolateLiquidate`, `executeCrossLiquidateERC20`, `executeCrossLiquidateERC721`, `executeYieldBorrowERC20`, and `executeYieldRepayERC20`), overflow in any single group bricks the entire asset across all groups.

findings.remediation:
Change `borrowRate` and `borrowIndex` in `DataTypes.GroupData` from `uint128` to `uint256` (and remove the corresponding `.toUint128()` downcasts when assigning them).

```solidity
  struct GroupData {
    // config parameters
    address rateModel;

    // user state
    uint256 totalScaledCrossBorrow;
    mapping(address => uint256) userScaledCrossBorrow;
    uint256 totalScaledIsolateBorrow;
    mapping(address => uint256) userScaledIsolateBorrow;

    // interest state
    uint256 borrowRate;
    uint256 borrowIndex;
    uint8 groupId;
  }
```

In `IsolateLogic.executeIsolateLiquidate`, pass `vars.totalBorrowAmount` as `liquidityAdded` to `InterestLogic.updateInterestRates`, and pass `vars.totalBorrowAmount - vars.totalExtraAmount` to `VaultLogic.erc20TransferOutBidAmountToLiqudity` so that `vars.totalExtraAmount` is only accounted for once when transferred in via `VaultLogic.erc20TransferInLiquidity`.

```solidity
    // update interest rate according latest borrow amount (utilization)
    InterestLogic.updateInterestRates(poolData, debtAssetData, vars.totalBorrowAmount, 0);

    // bid already in pool and now repay the borrow but need to increase liquidity
    VaultLogic.erc20TransferOutBidAmountToLiqudity(debtAssetData, (vars.totalBorrowAmount - vars.totalExtraAmount));

    if (vars.totalExtraAmount > 0) {
      // transfer underlying asset from liquidator to pool
      VaultLogic.erc20TransferInLiquidity(debtAssetData, params.msgSender, vars.totalExtraAmount);
    }
```

Clear every liquidated token’s `lockerAddr` before either collateral handoff path. Prefer doing this once in the common settlement block so both `supplyAsCollateral` branches preserve the invariant. Alternatively, make both liquidation-specific VaultLogic helpers clear the lock internally.

Add tests asserting that every successfully liquidated token has a zero locker address, including both collateral-delivery modes.

```solidity
for (uint256 i = 0; i < params.nftTokenIds.length; i++) {
  VaultLogic.erc721SetTokenLockerAddr(nftAssetData, params.nftTokenIds[i], address(0));
}

if (params.supplyAsCollateral) {
  VaultLogic.erc721TransferIsolateSupplyOnLiquidate(nftAssetData, params.msgSender, params.nftTokenIds);
} else {
  VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseIsolateSupplyOnLiquidate(nftAssetData, params.nftTokenIds);
  VaultLogic.erc721TransferOutLiquidity(nftAssetData, params.msgSender, params.nftTokenIds);
}
```

findings.technical_details:
When an isolated loan enters auction (`Constants.LOAN_STATUS_AUCTION`), bidders escrow their ERC20 bids into the pool (`debtAssetData.totalBidAmout`), and the loan struct `DataTypes.IsolateLoanData` records the winning bidder in `loanData.lastBidder` and the escrowed bid in `loanData.bidAmount`.

Once the auction period expires (`block.timestamp > loanData.bidStartTimestamp + nftAssetData.auctionDuration`), `IsolateLiquidation.isolateLiquidate` invokes `IsolateLogic.executeIsolateLiquidate` to settle the loan and transfer the liquidated NFT collateral to the winning bidder.

However, `executeIsolateLiquidate` and `ValidateLogic.validateIsolateLiquidateLoan` never check `loanData.lastBidder`:

1. `IsolateLiquidation.isolateLiquidate` (lines 75–102) is a `public` function with no caller restriction, passing the transaction sender as `params.msgSender`.
2. Inside `IsolateLogic.executeIsolateLiquidate`, `ValidateLogic.validateIsolateLiquidateLoan` (lines 601–610) only checks:
   ```solidity
   validateGroupBasic(debtGroupData);
   require(loanData.loanStatus == Constants.LOAN_STATUS_AUCTION, Errors.INVALID_LOAN_STATUS);
   require(loanData.reserveAsset == inputParams.asset, Errors.ISOLATE_LOAN_ASSET_NOT_MATCH);
   ```
   It does not verify `loanData.lastBidder == inputParams.msgSender`.
3. The loop in `executeIsolateLiquidate` (lines 413–453) settles the borrower's debt using `loanData.bidAmount` (which was funded by `loanData.lastBidder`) and deletes `loanData` at line 452 without ever reading `loanData.lastBidder`.
4. Finally, at lines 471–478, `executeIsolateLiquidate` transfers the ERC721 collateral to `params.msgSender`:
   ```solidity
   // transfer erc721 to bidder
   if (params.supplyAsCollateral) {
     VaultLogic.erc721TransferIsolateSupplyOnLiquidate(nftAssetData, params.msgSender, params.nftTokenIds);
   } else {
     VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseIsolateSupplyOnLiquidate(nftAssetData, params.nftTokenIds);

     VaultLogic.erc721TransferOutLiquidity(nftAssetData, params.msgSender, params.nftTokenIds);
   }
   ```

As a result, collateral ownership and isolated supply balances (`userScaledIsolateSupply` and `tokenData.owner` in `erc721TransferIsolateSupplyOnLiquidate`, or the underlying ERC721 tokens in `erc721TransferOutLiquidity`) are assigned to `params.msgSender` rather than `loanData.lastBidder`.

findings.remediation:
Validate that `params.msgSender` is the winning bidder (`loanData.lastBidder`) for each liquidated loan in `ValidateLogic.validateIsolateLiquidateLoan`, or transfer the liquidated ERC721 collateral to `loanData.lastBidder`.

Specifically, enforce that `inputParams.msgSender == loanData.lastBidder` in `ValidateLogic.validateIsolateLiquidateLoan`:

```solidity
  function validateIsolateLiquidateLoan(
    InputTypes.ExecuteIsolateLiquidateParams memory inputParams,
    DataTypes.GroupData storage debtGroupData,
    DataTypes.IsolateLoanData storage loanData
  ) internal view {
    validateGroupBasic(debtGroupData);

    require(loanData.loanStatus == Constants.LOAN_STATUS_AUCTION, Errors.INVALID_LOAN_STATUS);
    require(loanData.reserveAsset == inputParams.asset, Errors.ISOLATE_LOAN_ASSET_NOT_MATCH);
    require(loanData.lastBidder == inputParams.msgSender, Errors.INVALID_CALLER);
  }
```

findings.technical_details:
When a user supplies ERC721 tokens into the protocol in cross-margin mode (`Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS`), `VaultLogic.erc721IncreaseCrossSupply` records ownership per token in `assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenId].owner = user` and increments the aggregate counter `assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user] += tokenIds.length`.

When cross-margin ERC721 supply is decreased in `VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseCrossSupply` (`src/libraries/logic/VaultLogic.sol:593-608`), the function iterates through `tokenIds`, verifies only `tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS` (line 600), clears `tokenData.owner` and `tokenData.supplyMode` (lines 602-603), and decrements `assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user]` by `tokenIds.length` (line 607):

```solidity
  function erc721DecreaseCrossSupply(
    DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
    address user,
    uint256[] memory tokenIds
  ) internal {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
      DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
      require(tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);

      tokenData.owner = address(0);
      tokenData.supplyMode = 0;
    }

    assetData.totalScaledCrossSupply -= tokenIds.length;
    assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user] -= tokenIds.length;
  }
```

Notably, `erc721DecreaseCrossSupply` never validates `tokenData.owner == user` (the same check is also missing in `erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply` at lines 610-625). While callers such as `SupplyLogic.executeWithdrawERC721` and `SupplyLogic.executeSetERC721SupplyMode` happen to perform caller-side `tokenData.owner == onBehalf` checks, `LiquidationLogic._transferUserERC721CollateralToLiquidator` (`src/libraries/logic/LiquidationLogic.sol:433-441`, called from `executeCrossLiquidateERC721` when `params.supplyAsCollateral == false`) relies on `VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseCrossSupply` and then directly transfers `params.collateralTokenIds` out of the pool to `params.msgSender` via `VaultLogic.erc721TransferOutLiquidity`.

Consequently, when any borrower with cross-supplied ERC721 collateral becomes liquidatable, a liquidator can specify `params.borrower` alongside a `collateralTokenIds` entry belonging to a completely different user who supplied an NFT of the same collection in `SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS`. The borrower's `userScaledCrossSupply[borrower]` is decremented, while the victim's `tokenData.owner` is wiped and the victim's NFT is transferred to the liquidator.

findings.remediation:
Enforce in `VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseCrossSupply` (and `VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply`) that every token in `tokenIds` is owned by `user` before clearing its ownership and decrementing `user`'s scaled supply balance.

```solidity
  function erc721DecreaseCrossSupply(
    DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
    address user,
    uint256[] memory tokenIds
  ) internal {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
      DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
      require(tokenData.owner == user, Errors.INVALID_TOKEN_OWNER);
      require(tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);

      tokenData.owner = address(0);
      tokenData.supplyMode = 0;
    }

    assetData.totalScaledCrossSupply -= tokenIds.length;
    assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user] -= tokenIds.length;
  }

  function erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply(
    DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
    address user,
    uint256[] memory tokenIds
  ) internal {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
      DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
      require(tokenData.owner == user, Errors.INVALID_TOKEN_OWNER);
      require(tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_ISOLATE, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);

      tokenData.owner = address(0);
      tokenData.supplyMode = 0;
    }

    assetData.totalScaledIsolateSupply -= tokenIds.length;
    assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[user] -= tokenIds.length;
  }
```

Require `lockerAddr` to be zero inside both supply-increase helpers before changing token ownership, supply mode, or counters. Keep caller-level validation as defense in depth. Do not automatically clear a nonzero locker; provide a separate authorized cleanup operation that proves the prior lock obligation has terminated.

```solidity
function erc721IncreaseCrossSupply(
  DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
  address user,
  uint256[] memory tokenIds
) internal {
  for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
    DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
    require(tokenData.lockerAddr == address(0), Errors.ASSET_ALREADY_LOCKED_IN_USE);

    tokenData.owner = user;
    tokenData.supplyMode = Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS;
  }

  assetData.totalScaledCrossSupply += tokenIds.length;
  assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user] += tokenIds.length;
}

function erc721IncreaseIsolateSupply(
  DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
  address user,
  uint256[] memory tokenIds
) internal {
  for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
    DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
    require(tokenData.lockerAddr == address(0), Errors.ASSET_ALREADY_LOCKED_IN_USE);

    tokenData.owner = user;
    tokenData.supplyMode = Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_ISOLATE;
  }

  assetData.totalScaledIsolateSupply += tokenIds.length;
  assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[user] += tokenIds.length;
}
```

Reject a zero beneficiary before any per-token or aggregate state is modified. The validation should be enforced inside both internal methods so every current and future caller receives the same protection. The external deposit entry point should also reject a zero `onBehalf` value for clearer error reporting.

```solidity
