```

Any existing zero-beneficiary positions should be reconciled by returning or assigning their NFTs and correcting all associated global, per-user, and per-token state atomically.

findings.technical_details:
In `src/libraries/logic/VaultLogic.sol`, ERC721 collateral accounting maintains three layers of state inside `DataTypes.AssetData`:
1. Per-token state in `assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenId]` (`owner`, `supplyMode`, `lockerAddr`).
2. Per-user supply balances in `assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user]` and `assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[user]`.
3. Global pool supply totals in `assetData.totalScaledCrossSupply` and `assetData.totalScaledIsolateSupply`.

When decreasing supply in `erc721DecreaseCrossSupply` (lines 593–608) and `erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply` (lines 610–625), the functions explicitly verify each token's current `supplyMode` before resetting `tokenData.owner = address(0)` and `tokenData.supplyMode = 0`:

```solidity
require(tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);
tokenData.owner = address(0);
tokenData.supplyMode = 0;
```

Because the first occurrence of a `tokenId` clears `supplyMode` to `0`, any duplicate `tokenId` in the `tokenIds` array or any token not in the expected mode immediately triggers a revert with `Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE`.

In contrast, `erc721IncreaseCrossSupply` (lines 563–576) and `erc721IncreaseIsolateSupply` (lines 578–591) iterate over `tokenIds` and unconditionally overwrite `tokenData.owner` and `tokenData.supplyMode` without checking that `tokenData.owner == address(0)` and `tokenData.supplyMode == 0`:

```solidity
for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
  DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
  tokenData.owner = user;
  tokenData.supplyMode = Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS;
}

assetData.totalScaledCrossSupply += tokenIds.length;
assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user] += tokenIds.length;
```

Consequently:
- If `tokenIds` contains duplicate entries or references an already-supplied token ID, the loop simply overwrites the same `ERC721TokenData` storage slot multiple times (or hijacks ownership/mode of an already-registered token) without reverting.
- After the loop, `totalScaledCrossSupply` / `userScaledCrossSupply[user]` (or `totalScaledIsolateSupply` / `userScaledIsolateSupply[user]`) are incremented by `tokenIds.length` rather than the number of distinct newly supplied tokens.
- This creates a direct divergence between per-token state (`erc721TokenData`), per-user balances (`userScaledCrossSupply` / `userScaledIsolateSupply`), and global pool supply (`totalScaledCrossSupply` / `totalScaledIsolateSupply`).

findings.remediation:
Validate in both `erc721IncreaseCrossSupply` and `erc721IncreaseIsolateSupply` that each token is not currently supplied (`tokenData.owner == address(0)` and `tokenData.supplyMode == 0`) before setting its `owner` and `supplyMode`. This prevents both re-crediting already-supplied tokens and passing duplicate `tokenId` entries within the same batch.

```solidity
  function erc721IncreaseCrossSupply(
    DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
    address user,
    uint256[] memory tokenIds
  ) internal {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
      DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
      require(tokenData.owner == address(0), Errors.INVALID_TOKEN_OWNER);
      require(tokenData.supplyMode == 0, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);

      tokenData.owner = user;
      tokenData.supplyMode = Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS;
    }

    assetData.totalScaledCrossSupply += tokenIds.length;
    assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user] += tokenIds.length;
  }

  function erc721IncreaseIsolateSupply(
    DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
    address user,
    uint256[] memory tokenIds
  ) internal {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
      DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
      require(tokenData.owner == address(0), Errors.INVALID_TOKEN_OWNER);
      require(tokenData.supplyMode == 0, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);

      tokenData.owner = user;
      tokenData.supplyMode = Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_ISOLATE;
    }

    assetData.totalScaledIsolateSupply += tokenIds.length;
    assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[user] += tokenIds.length;
  }
```

Require the generic decrease helper to operate only after the active locker has been properly unwound, and delete the complete token metadata rather than resetting selected fields. If forced liquidation must remove actively locked collateral, implement a dedicated path that atomically unwinds the locker before invoking this helper.

```solidity
function erc721DecreaseCrossSupply(
  DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
  address user,
  uint256[] memory tokenIds
) internal {
  for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
    DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];

    require(tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_CROSS, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);
    require(tokenData.lockerAddr == address(0), Errors.ASSET_ALREADY_LOCKED_IN_USE);

    delete assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
  }

  assetData.totalScaledCrossSupply -= tokenIds.length;
  assetData.userScaledCrossSupply[user] -= tokenIds.length;
}
```

Existing stale records should be migrated only after verifying that their referenced lockers are no longer active.

findings.technical_details:
In `src/libraries/logic/VaultLogic.sol`, `erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply` is responsible for burning a user's isolated ERC721 supply accounting when isolated collateral is removed (such as during `SupplyLogic.executeWithdrawERC721` or supply mode transitions):

```solidity
  function erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply(
    DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
    address user,
    uint256[] memory tokenIds
  ) internal {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
      DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
      require(tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_ISOLATE, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);

      tokenData.owner = address(0);
      tokenData.supplyMode = 0;
    }

    assetData.totalScaledIsolateSupply -= tokenIds.length;
    assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[user] -= tokenIds.length;
  }
```

Inside the loop (lines 616–621), the function retrieves `tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]]` and verifies only that `tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_ISOLATE` (line 617). It then resets `tokenData.owner = address(0)` and `tokenData.supplyMode = 0` (lines 619–620), and subsequently decrements `assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[user]` by `tokenIds.length` (line 624).

Unlike `VaultLogic.erc721TransferIsolateSupply` (line 673), which explicitly enforces `require(tokenData.owner == from, Errors.INVALID_TOKEN_OWNER)`, or `VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseIsolateSupplyOnLiquidate` (line 635), which debits `assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[tokenData.owner]`, `erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply` fails to bind the ownership of each `tokenIds[i]` (`tokenData.owner`) to the debited `user` account.

Consequently, if a caller invokes a flow reaching `erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply` (such as `SupplyLogic.executeWithdrawERC721` in `SUPPLY_MODE_ISOLATE`) without an upstream per-token ownership check, any `user` who holds at least `tokenIds.length` in `userScaledIsolateSupply[user]` can pass `tokenIds` belonging to another depositor. The function will decrement `userScaledIsolateSupply[user]` while wiping `tokenData.owner` on the victim's tokens, allowing the caller to withdraw the victim's NFTs via `erc721TransferOutLiquidity` and leaving the victim with a phantom `userScaledIsolateSupply` balance backed by no owned tokens.

findings.remediation:
Enforce that `tokenData.owner == user` for each token ID in `VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply` (and similarly in `VaultLogic.erc721DecreaseCrossSupply` for defense-in-depth) before clearing ownership and debiting the user's scaled supply balance.

```solidity
  function erc721DecreaseIsolateSupply(
    DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
    address user,
    uint256[] memory tokenIds
  ) internal {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
      DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
      require(tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_ISOLATE, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);
      require(tokenData.owner == user, Errors.INVALID_TOKEN_OWNER);

      tokenData.owner = address(0);
      tokenData.supplyMode = 0;
    }

    assetData.totalScaledIsolateSupply -= tokenIds.length;
    assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[user] -= tokenIds.length;
  }
```

Before releasing liquidated collateral, the protocol should unwind any active locker integration and verify that the associated external state has been settled. It should then delete the complete per-token record rather than resetting selected fields. This prevents future struct fields from being accidentally retained as well.

A corrected storage-cleanup implementation is:

```solidity
function erc721DecreaseIsolateSupplyOnLiquidate(
  DataTypes.AssetData storage assetData,
  uint256[] memory tokenIds
) internal {
  for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokenIds.length; i++) {
    DataTypes.ERC721TokenData storage tokenData = assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
    require(tokenData.supplyMode == Constants.SUPPLY_MODE_ISOLATE, Errors.INVALID_SUPPLY_MODE);

    assetData.userScaledIsolateSupply[tokenData.owner] -= 1;

    delete assetData.erc721TokenData[tokenIds[i]];
  }

  assetData.totalScaledIsolateSupply -= tokenIds.length;
}
```

If the locker maintains state outside this mapping, deleting the record must occur only after a mandatory liquidation-specific unlock or settlement call. Merely requiring an empty locker without providing such an unwind path would let borrowers block liquidation and is therefore not a sufficient standalone fix.

Ensure that bid validation in `IsolateLogic.executeIsolateAuction` accounts for the interest that will accrue through the end of the auction (`auctionEndTimestamp`), or require bids to cover the projected borrow debt at `auctionEndTimestamp`:

```solidity
      // first time bid
      if (loanData.loanStatus == Constants.LOAN_STATUS_ACTIVE) {
        // loan's accumulated debt must exceed threshold (heath factor below 1.0)
        require(vars.borrowAmount > vars.thresholdPrice, Errors.ISOLATE_BORROW_NOT_EXCEED_LIQUIDATION_THRESHOLD);

        loanData.firstBidder = params.msgSender;
        loanData.loanStatus = Constants.LOAN_STATUS_AUCTION;
        loanData.bidStartTimestamp = uint40(block.timestamp);
        vars.auctionEndTimestamp = loanData.bidStartTimestamp + nftAssetData.auctionDuration;

        uint256 maxBorrowAmount = loanData.scaledAmount.rayMul(
          MathUtils.calculateCompoundedInterest(
            debtGroupData.borrowRate,
            debtAssetData.lastUpdateTimestamp,
            vars.auctionEndTimestamp
          ).rayMul(debtGroupData.borrowIndex)
        );

        // bid price must greater than borrow debt at auction end
        require(params.amounts[vars.nidx] >= maxBorrowAmount, Errors.ISOLATE_BID_PRICE_LESS_THAN_BORROW);

        // bid price must greater than liquidate price
        require(params.amounts[vars.nidx] >= vars.liquidatePrice, Errors.ISOLATE_BID_PRICE_LESS_THAN_LIQUIDATION_PRICE);
      } else {
        vars.auctionEndTimestamp = loanData.bidStartTimestamp + nftAssetData.auctionDuration;
        require(block.timestamp <= vars.auctionEndTimestamp, Errors.ISOLATE_BID_AUCTION_DURATION_HAS_END);

        uint256 maxBorrowAmount = loanData.scaledAmount.rayMul(
          MathUtils.calculateCompoundedInterest(
            debtGroupData.borrowRate,
            debtAssetData.lastUpdateTimestamp,
            vars.auctionEndTimestamp
          ).rayMul(debtGroupData.borrowIndex)
        );

        // bid price must greater than borrow debt at auction end
        require(params.amounts[vars.nidx] >= maxBorrowAmount, Errors.ISOLATE_BID_PRICE_LESS_THAN_BORROW);

        // bid price must greater than liquidate price
        require(params.amounts[vars.nidx] >= vars.liquidatePrice, Errors.ISOLATE_BID_PRICE_LESS_THAN_LIQUIDATION_PRICE);
```

