```

findings.technical_details:
When claiming an art credential configured with the `"SIGNATURE"` verification type via `PhiFactory.claim` (lines 284–300), the decompressed input `encodedata_` is decoded into `(artId, minter_, ref_, verifier_, expiresIn_, quantity_, data_, imageURI_, signature_)`.

At lines 297–300, `claim` constructs the signed message payload `claimData` and the execution arguments `mintArgs` separately:

```solidity
bytes memory claimData = abi.encode(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId, block.chainid, data_);
MintArgs memory mintArgs = MintArgs({ tokenId: tokenId, quantity: quantity_, imageURI: imageURI_ });
uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId, quantity_);
this.signatureClaim{ value: mintFee }(signature_, claimData, mintArgs);
```

In `PhiFactory.signatureClaim` (lines 327–346), signature verification is performed exclusively over `keccak256(encodeData_)` (i.e., `claimData`):

```solidity
if (_recoverSigner(keccak256(encodeData_), signature_) != phiSignerAddress) revert AddressNotSigned();

_validateAndUpdateClaimState(artId_, minter_, mintArgs_.quantity);
_processClaim(artId_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, mintArgs_.quantity, data_, mintArgs_.imageURI, msg.value);
```

Because neither `mintArgs_.imageURI` nor `mintArgs_.quantity` is included in `claimData` at line 297 or verified in `signatureClaim` at line 340, the cryptographic signature from `phiSignerAddress` does not bind these execution parameters. When `_processClaim` invokes `claimFromFactory` on the `PhiNFT1155` contract, the unverified `imageURI_` and `quantity_` values are passed directly into `mint(minter_, tokenId_, quantity_, imageURI_, data_)`.

As a result, any caller holding a valid signature for `(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId, block.chainid, data_)` can substitute an arbitrary `imageURI_` (corrupting or spoofing the NFT metadata URI associated with the minter's token) or alter `quantity_` (up to `art.maxSupply`) both through `claim` (by re-compressing modified `encodedata_`) and through direct calls to `signatureClaim`.

findings.remediation:
Include `quantity_` and `imageURI_` (or the hash of `imageURI_`) in the signed `claimData` payload constructed in `claim` (and `Claimable.signatureClaim`) and decode/verify them in `PhiFactory.signatureClaim`.

```solidity
            bytes memory claimData =
                abi.encode(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId, block.chainid, data_, quantity_, imageURI_);
            MintArgs memory mintArgs = MintArgs({ tokenId: tokenId, quantity: quantity_, imageURI: imageURI_ });
            uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId, quantity_);
            this.signatureClaim{ value: mintFee }(signature_, claimData, mintArgs);
```

Additionally, ensure `PhiFactory.signatureClaim` validates that the decoded `quantity_` and `imageURI_` from `encodeData_` match `mintArgs_.quantity` and `mintArgs_.imageURI`:

```solidity
        (
            uint256 expiresIn_,
            address minter_,
            address ref_,
            address verifier_,
            uint256 artId_,
            uint256 chainId_,
            bytes32 data_,
            uint256 signedQuantity_,
            string memory signedImageURI_
        ) = abi.decode(encodeData_, (uint256, address, address, address, uint256, uint256, bytes32, uint256, string));

        if (expiresIn_ <= block.timestamp) revert SignatureExpired();
        if (
            chainId_ != block.chainid || signedQuantity_ != mintArgs_.quantity
                || !signedImageURI_.eq(mintArgs_.imageURI)
                || _recoverSigner(keccak256(encodeData_), signature_) != phiSignerAddress
        ) revert AddressNotSigned();
```

findings.technical_details:
The `PhiFactory` contract supports claiming `"SIGNATURE"`-verified credential art NFTs through two entry points on `PhiFactory` (as well as through `Claimable.signatureClaim` on `PhiNFT1155`):

1. `PhiFactory.claim(bytes calldata encodeData_)` (`src/PhiFactory.sol:264-304`):
   At `297-300`, the function constructs `claimData` on-chain using the current chain's `block.chainid`:
   ```solidity
   bytes memory claimData = abi.encode(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId, block.chainid, data_);
   MintArgs memory mintArgs = MintArgs({ tokenId: tokenId, quantity: quantity_, imageURI: imageURI_ });
   uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId, quantity_);
   this.signatureClaim{ value: mintFee }(signature_, claimData, mintArgs);
   ```
   Similarly, `Claimable.signatureClaim()` (`src/abstract/Claimable.sol:38`) encodes `block.chainid` into `claimData_` before forwarding the call to `PhiFactory.signatureClaim`.

2. `PhiFactory.signatureClaim(bytes calldata signature_, bytes calldata encodeData_, MintArgs calldata mintArgs_)` (`src/PhiFactory.sol:327-346`):
   Because `this.signatureClaim` and `phiFactoryContract.signatureClaim` invoke it externally, `signatureClaim` has `external` visibility. In `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` (`src/PhiFactory.sol:706`), direct calls from externally owned accounts (`tx.origin == _msgSender()`) are explicitly permitted.

However, inside `PhiFactory.signatureClaim` (`src/PhiFactory.sol:336-340`), `encodeData_` is supplied directly by the caller, decoded, and hashed for ECDSA recovery without checking the encoded chain ID:

```solidity
(uint256 expiresIn_, address minter_, address ref_, address verifier_, uint256 artId_,, bytes32 data_) =
    abi.decode(encodeData_, (uint256, address, address, address, uint256, uint256, bytes32));

if (expiresIn_ <= block.timestamp) revert SignatureExpired();
if (_recoverSigner(keccak256(encodeData_), signature_) != phiSignerAddress) revert AddressNotSigned();
```

Notice that the 6th element of the decoded tuple (corresponding to `block.chainid` encoded at `297`) is discarded (`artId_,,`). Because `signatureClaim` hashes the raw `encodeData_` provided by the caller and never verifies that the decoded `chainId` equals `block.chainid`, the cross-chain replay protection enforced in `claim()` (`297`) is completely bypassed when calling `signatureClaim()` directly.

Since `PhiFactory` is deployed across multiple chains with the same `phiSignerAddress` (`script/Deploy.s.sol:14`) and `artIdCounter` starts at `1` on each deployment (`src/PhiFactory.sol:144`), a valid signature issued for `(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId, chainIdA, data_)` on Chain A will pass `_recoverSigner(keccak256(encodeData_), signature_) == phiSignerAddress` when submitted directly to `PhiFactory.signatureClaim` on Chain B.

findings.remediation:
Decode the `chainId_` parameter in `PhiFactory.signatureClaim` (or reconstruct the signed payload using `block.chainid`) and revert if `chainId_ != block.chainid`. Additionally, ensure `arts[artId_].verificationType.eq("SIGNATURE")` is enforced consistently across all signature claim entry points.

```solidity
    function signatureClaim(
        bytes calldata signature_,
        bytes calldata encodeData_,
        MintArgs calldata mintArgs_
    )
        external
        payable
        whenNotPaused
    {
        (
            uint256 expiresIn_,
            address minter_,
            address ref_,
            address verifier_,
            uint256 artId_,
            uint256 chainId_,
            bytes32 data_
        ) = abi.decode(encodeData_, (uint256, address, address, address, uint256, uint256, bytes32));

        if (chainId_ != block.chainid) revert InvalidChainId();
        if (!arts[artId_].verificationType.eq("SIGNATURE")) revert InvalidVerificationType();
        if (expiresIn_ <= block.timestamp) revert SignatureExpired();
        if (_recoverSigner(keccak256(encodeData_), signature_) != phiSignerAddress) revert AddressNotSigned();

        _validateAndUpdateClaimState(artId_, minter_, mintArgs_.quantity);
        _processClaim(artId_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, mintArgs_.quantity, data_, mintArgs_.imageURI, msg.value);

        emit ArtClaimedData(artId_, "SIGNATURE", minter_, ref_, verifier_, arts[artId_].artAddress, mintArgs_.quantity);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
When a user claims an art NFT via `PhiFactory.signatureClaim` (lines `327–346`), the contract decodes `(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId_,, data_)` from `encodeData_` (lines `336–337`), verifies that `expiresIn_ > block.timestamp` (line `339`) and that `_recoverSigner(keccak256(encodeData_), signature_) == phiSignerAddress` (line `340`), and then invokes `_validateAndUpdateClaimState(artId_, minter_, mintArgs_.quantity)` (line `342`) followed by `_processClaim(...)` (line `343`).

In `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` (lines `702–721`), the contract updates per-recipient state:
```solidity
if (!credMinted[art.credChainId][art.credId][minter_]) {
    credMinted[art.credChainId][art.credId][minter_] = true;
}
artMinted[artId_][minter_] = true;
art.numberMinted += quantity_;
```
Although `IPhiFactory` defines `error AddressAlreadyMinted()` (line `10`), `PhiFactory` maintains `mapping(uint256 artId => mapping(address minter => bool)) private artMinted` (line `46`), and `isArtMinted(uint256 artId_, address address_)` (lines `448–450`) queries whether an address has already claimed a given `artId_`, neither `signatureClaim` nor `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` checks `if (artMinted[artId_][minter_]) revert AddressAlreadyMinted();` before minting.

Furthermore, the signed payload `encodeData_` contains no nonce or unique claim identifier and is not marked as consumed upon use. As a result, once `phiSignerAddress` issues a signature authorizing `minter_` to claim `artId_`, that exact `(signature_, encodeData_)` pair remains valid for unlimited invocations of `signatureClaim` (as well as through `claim`, `batchClaim`, or `Claimable.signatureClaim`) until `expiresIn_` is reached or `art.numberMinted` reaches `art.maxSupply`.

findings.remediation:
Enforce per-recipient claim consumption by checking `artMinted[artId_][minter_]` before updating state and processing the claim, reverting with `AddressAlreadyMinted()` if the recipient has already claimed the art:

```solidity
    function signatureClaim(
        bytes calldata signature_,
        bytes calldata encodeData_,
        MintArgs calldata mintArgs_
    )
        external
        payable
        whenNotPaused
    {
        (uint256 expiresIn_, address minter_, address ref_, address verifier_, uint256 artId_,, bytes32 data_) =
            abi.decode(encodeData_, (uint256, address, address, address, uint256, uint256, bytes32));

        if (expiresIn_ <= block.timestamp) revert SignatureExpired();
        if (_recoverSigner(keccak256(encodeData_), signature_) != phiSignerAddress) revert AddressNotSigned();
        if (artMinted[artId_][minter_]) revert AddressAlreadyMinted();

        _validateAndUpdateClaimState(artId_, minter_, mintArgs_.quantity);
        _processClaim(artId_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, mintArgs_.quantity, data_, mintArgs_.imageURI, msg.value);

        emit ArtClaimedData(artId_, "SIGNATURE", minter_, ref_, verifier_, arts[artId_].artAddress, mintArgs_.quantity);
    }
```

Alternatively, place the `if (artMinted[artId_][minter_]) revert AddressAlreadyMinted();` check directly inside `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` so that both `signatureClaim` and `merkleClaim` consistently enforce one-time per-recipient claiming per `artId`.

Apply the `nonReentrant` modifier to the claim entrypoints (`claim`, `batchClaim`, `signatureClaim`, `merkleClaim`) or guard `_processClaim` against reentrancy, and ensure `PhiNFT1155.claimFromFactory` follows the checks-effects-interactions pattern (or uses `nonReentrant`) by settling rewards before invoking the external `_mint` acceptance callback.

In `src/PhiFactory.sol`, protect `_processClaim` with reentrancy lock checks:

```solidity
    function _processClaim(
        uint256 artId_,
        address minter_,
        address ref_,
        address verifier_,
        uint256 quantity_,
        bytes32 data_,
        string memory imageURI_,
        uint256 etherValue_
    )
        private
        nonReentrant
    {
        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        // Handle refund
        uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_);
        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(mintProtocolFee * quantity_);

        (bool success_,) = art.artAddress.call{ value: mintFee - mintProtocolFee * quantity_ }(
            abi.encodeWithSignature(
                "claimFromFactory(uint256,address,address,address,uint256,bytes32,string)",
                artId_,
                minter_,
                ref_,
                verifier_,
                quantity_,
                data_,
                imageURI_
            )
        );

        if (!success_) revert ClaimFailed();

        if ((etherValue_ - mintFee) > 0) {
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(etherValue_ - mintFee);
        }
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
`PhiFactory` supports claiming art NFTs both directly and through the `Claimable` interface inherited by `PhiNFT1155` (`art.artAddress`). Specifically, in `src/abstract/Claimable.sol`, `PhiNFT1155.signatureClaim()` (lines 22–44) and `PhiNFT1155.merkleClaim()` (lines 47–65) decode the claim payload and forward the call along with the full `msg.value` to `PhiFactory`:

```solidity
phiFactoryContract.signatureClaim{ value: msg.value }(signature, claimData_, mintArgs_);
```

and

```solidity
phiFactory.merkleClaim{ value: msg.value }(proof, claimData, mintArgs, leafPart);
```

Inside `PhiFactory._validateAndUpdateClaimState` (line 706), calls originating from `art.artAddress` and `address(this)` are explicitly permitted alongside direct EOA calls:

```solidity
if (tx.origin != _msgSender() && msg.sender != art.artAddress && msg.sender != address(this)) {
    revert TxOriginMismatch();
}
```

Subsequently, `PhiFactory._processClaim` (lines 735–758) calculates `mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_)` and attempts to refund any excess ETH (`etherValue_ - mintFee > 0`):

```solidity
uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_);
if ((etherValue_ - mintFee) > 0) {
    _msgSender().safeTransferETH(etherValue_ - mintFee);
}
```

Because `PhiFactory` inherits `ContextUpgradeable` (where `_msgSender()` returns `msg.sender`), `_msgSender()` inside `_processClaim` is NOT the user who initiated the transaction when the claim is routed through `PhiNFT1155` (`Claimable`):
1. When called via `PhiNFT1155.signatureClaim()` or `PhiNFT1155.merkleClaim()`, `_msgSender()` is `art.artAddress` (`PhiNFT1155`). `SafeTransferLib.safeTransferETH` executes a low-level call with value and empty calldata to `PhiNFT1155`. Because `PhiNFT1155` is deployed via `LibClone.cloneDeterministic` (an ERC-1167 minimal proxy that delegates calls with empty calldata to the implementation) and `PhiNFT1155` implements neither a `receive()` nor a `payable fallback()` function, the ETH transfer callback into `PhiNFT1155` unconditionally reverts with `ETHTransferFailed()`.
2. Even if `art.artAddress` (or `address(this)` when called via external self-calls in `PhiFactory.claim`) could accept ETH, refunding `_msgSender()` sends the excess ETH back to the intermediary contract rather than the actual payer/minter.

findings.remediation:
Refund the excess ETH to the actual payer (`tx.origin`) or `minter_` rather than `_msgSender()`, which may be the `PhiNFT1155` contract (`art.artAddress`) or `PhiFactory` (`address(this)`).

```solidity
    function _processClaim(
        uint256 artId_,
        address minter_,
        address ref_,
        address verifier_,
        uint256 quantity_,
        bytes32 data_,
        string memory imageURI_,
        uint256 etherValue_
    )
        private
    {
        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        // Handle refund
        uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_);
        if ((etherValue_ - mintFee) > 0) {
            tx.origin.safeTransferETH(etherValue_ - mintFee);
        }
        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(mintProtocolFee * quantity_);

        (bool success_,) = art.artAddress.call{ value: mintFee - mintProtocolFee * quantity_ }(
            abi.encodeWithSignature(
                "claimFromFactory(uint256,address,address,address,uint256,bytes32,string)",
                artId_,
                minter_,
                ref_,
                verifier_,
                quantity_,
                data_,
                imageURI_
            )
        );

        if (!success_) revert ClaimFailed();
    }
```

Include the `MintArgs` fields (`tokenId_`, `quantity_`, and `imageURI_`) in the signed `claimData_` payload in `Claimable.signatureClaim` and validate them during signature recovery in `PhiFactory.signatureClaim`, as well as preventing signature replay by enforcing that a minter (or nonce) cannot claim multiple times with the same authorization.

In `src/abstract/Claimable.sol`:
```solidity
    function signatureClaim() external payable {
        (
            bytes32 r_,
            bytes32 vs_,
            address ref_,
            address verifier_,
            address minter_,
            uint256 tokenId_,
            uint256 quantity_,
            uint256 expiresIn_,
            string memory imageURI_,
            bytes32 data_
        ) = abi.decode(
            msg.data[4:], (bytes32, bytes32, address, address, address, uint256, uint256, uint256, string, bytes32)
        );
        uint256 artId = getFactoryArtId(tokenId_);
        bytes memory claimData_ =
            abi.encode(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId, block.chainid, data_, tokenId_, quantity_, imageURI_);
        bytes memory signature = abi.encodePacked(r_, vs_);

        IPhiFactory phiFactoryContract = getPhiFactoryContract();
        IPhiFactory.MintArgs memory mintArgs_ = IPhiFactory.MintArgs(tokenId_, quantity_, imageURI_);
        phiFactoryContract.signatureClaim{ value: msg.value }(signature, claimData_, mintArgs_);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `PhiFactory.createArt`, the caller supplies `signedData_`, `signature_`, and an unsigned `CreateConfig memory createConfig_` struct:

```solidity
function createArt(
    bytes calldata signedData_,
    bytes calldata signature_,
    CreateConfig memory createConfig_
) external payable nonReentrant whenNotPaused returns (address) {
    _validateArtCreationSignature(signedData_, signature_);
    (, string memory uri_, bytes memory credData) = abi.decode(signedData_, (uint256, string, bytes));
    ERC1155Data memory erc1155Data = _createERC1155Data(artIdCounter, createConfig_, uri_, credData);
    address artAddress = createERC1155Internal(artIdCounter, erc1155Data);
    artIdCounter++;
    return artAddress;
}
```

`_validateArtCreationSignature` (lines 589–593) checks the signature as follows:

```solidity
function _validateArtCreationSignature(bytes calldata signedData_, bytes calldata signature_) private view {
    if (_recoverSigner(keccak256(signedData_), signature_) != phiSignerAddress) revert AddressNotSigned();
    (uint256 expiresIn_,,) = abi.decode(signedData_, (uint256, string, bytes));
    if (expiresIn_ <= block.timestamp) revert SignatureExpired();
}
```

This signature validation has two closely related flaws:

1. **Missing caller and `CreateConfig` parameter binding:** The digest `keccak256(signedData_)` only covers `expiresIn_`, `uri_`, and `credData`. None of the fields in `createConfig_` (`artist`, `receiver`, `endTime`, `startTime`, `maxSupply`, `mintFee`, `soulBounded`) or the caller (`msg.sender`) are included in the signed payload. Any user or front-runner can take a valid `(signedData_, signature_)` pair and pass their own `createConfig_`, setting themselves as `artist` and `receiver` and choosing arbitrary `mintFee`, `maxSupply`, and time bounds.
2. **Repeated signature consumption:** `_validateArtCreation` (line 584) checks `if (arts[createData_.artId].artAddress != address(0)) revert ArtAlreadyCreated();`. Because `createData_.artId` is populated from the auto-incrementing `artIdCounter` on every call, `arts[artIdCounter].artAddress` is always `address(0)`. Since neither `signedData_` nor `signature_` is tracked as consumed, the exact same `(signedData_, signature_)` pair remains valid until `expiresIn_` and can be submitted repeatedly to mint unlimited new `artId` instances for the same credential.

findings.remediation:
Include `msg.sender`, the `CreateConfig` parameters, `block.chainid`, `address(this)`, and a nonce (or track consumed signature hashes) in the signed digest verified by `_validateArtCreationSignature`.

```solidity
    mapping(bytes32 signatureHash => bool used) private usedArtSignatures;

    function _validateArtCreationSignature(
        bytes calldata signedData_,
        bytes calldata signature_,
        CreateConfig memory createConfig_
    ) private {
        (uint256 expiresIn_, string memory uri_, bytes memory credData) =
            abi.decode(signedData_, (uint256, string, bytes));
        if (expiresIn_ <= block.timestamp) revert SignatureExpired();

        bytes32 structHash = keccak256(
            abi.encode(
                expiresIn_,
                uri_,
                credData,
                createConfig_.artist,
                createConfig_.receiver,
                createConfig_.endTime,
                createConfig_.startTime,
                createConfig_.maxSupply,
                createConfig_.mintFee,
                createConfig_.soulBounded,
                msg.sender
            )
        );
        if (usedArtSignatures[structHash]) revert ArtAlreadyCreated();
        if (_recoverSigner(structHash, signature_) != phiSignerAddress) revert AddressNotSigned();
        usedArtSignatures[structHash] = true;
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
When an artwork is created via `PhiFactory.createArt`, `createERC1155Internal` invokes `_initializePhiArt(currentArt, createData_)` (lines 595–618) before deploying or reusing the credential's ERC1155 contract:

```solidity
function _initializePhiArt(PhiArt storage art, ERC1155Data memory createData_) private {
    (
        uint256 credId,
        address credCreator,
        string memory verificationType,
        uint256 credChainId,
        bytes32 merkleRootHash
    ) = abi.decode(createData_.credData, (uint256, address, string, uint256, bytes32));

    art.credId = credId;
    art.credCreator = credCreator;
    art.credChainId = credChainId;
    art.verificationType = verificationType;
    ...
    credMerkleRoot[credChainId][credId] = merkleRootHash;
}
```

Multiple `PhiArt` entries (`arts[artId]`) can be created for the same `(credChainId, credId)` pair and share a single ERC1155 contract stored in `credNFTContracts[credChainId][credId]` as well as a single Merkle root entry stored in `credMerkleRoot[credChainId][credId]`. However, `_initializePhiArt` and `createERC1155Internal` fail to isolate or validate credential-level parameters across arts sharing the same credential:

1. **Unconditional Overwrite of Shared `credMerkleRoot`**:
   Line 617 unconditionally assigns `credMerkleRoot[credChainId][credId] = merkleRootHash;` every time a new art is created for `(credChainId, credId)`. If an existing art for `(credChainId, credId)` uses `"MERKLE"` verification with a valid `merkleRootHash`, and a subsequent art for the same `(credChainId, credId)` is initialized with `"SIGNATURE"` verification (where `merkleRootHash` is `bytes32(0)`) or a different root hash, `credMerkleRoot[credChainId][credId]` is overwritten. Any subsequent call to `PhiFactory.merkleClaim` for the first art reads `credMerkleRoot[art.credChainId][art.credId]` (line 365) and either reverts with `InvalidMerkleProof()` (line 367) when the root was zeroed out or verifies against the wrong Merkle tree.

2. **Cross-Contract Verification Policy Desynchronization**:
   In `_initializePhiArt`, `art.verificationType` is stored per `artId` (line 607). However, when the first art for `(credChainId, credId)` is created, `_createNewNFTContract` initializes the shared `PhiNFT1155` clone with that first art's `verificationType` (line 636). When a subsequent art is created for the same `(credChainId, credId)`, `createERC1155Internal` branches to `_useExistingNFTContract` (line 564), which reuses `credNFTContracts[credChainId][credId]` without verifying that the new art's `verificationType` matches the existing `PhiNFT1155` contract's `verificationType` or updating the NFT contract. Consequently, the state depends on the order in which arts are created: if the first art is created with `"SIGNATURE"` and the second art for the same credential is created with `"MERKLE"` (or vice versa), `arts[newArtId].verificationType` in `PhiFactory` conflicts with the `verificationType` stored in the shared `PhiNFT1155` contract, causing direct claims on the `PhiNFT1155` contract (`Claimable`) to fail or enforce the wrong verification method.

findings.remediation:
Isolate per-art state from per-credential state, or strictly enforce that all artworks sharing a `(credChainId, credId)` have consistent `verificationType`, `credCreator`, and `merkleRootHash` values (and do not overwrite a non-zero `credMerkleRoot` with `bytes32(0)`):

```solidity
    function _initializePhiArt(PhiArt storage art, ERC1155Data memory createData_) private {
        (
            uint256 credId,
            address credCreator,
            string memory verificationType,
            uint256 credChainId,
            bytes32 merkleRootHash
        ) = abi.decode(createData_.credData, (uint256, address, string, uint256, bytes32));

        bytes32 existingRoot = credMerkleRoot[credChainId][credId];
        if (existingRoot != bytes32(0) && existingRoot != merkleRootHash) {
            revert InvalidVerificationType();
        }
        if (verificationType.eq("MERKLE")) {
            if (merkleRootHash == bytes32(0)) revert InvalidMerkleProof();
            credMerkleRoot[credChainId][credId] = merkleRootHash;
        } else if (existingRoot != bytes32(0)) {
            revert InvalidVerificationType();
        }

        art.credId = credId;
        art.credCreator = credCreator;
        art.credChainId = credChainId;
        art.verificationType = verificationType;
        art.uri = createData_.uri;
        art.artist = createData_.artist;
        art.receiver = createData_.receiver;
        art.maxSupply = createData_.maxSupply;
        art.mintFee = createData_.mintFee;
        art.startTime = createData_.startTime;
        art.endTime = createData_.endTime;
        art.soulBounded = createData_.soulBounded;
    }
```

Alternatively, store `merkleRootHash` per `PhiArt` (`art.merkleRootHash = merkleRootHash;`) instead of in the shared `credMerkleRoot[credChainId][credId]` mapping, and validate `verificationType` per `artId`.

Transfer ownership of the newly initialized `PhiNFT1155` clone to the `PhiFactory` owner immediately after calling `initialize` in `_createNewNFTContract` (or pass the initial owner address explicitly to `PhiNFT1155.initialize`).

```solidity
    function _createNewNFTContract(
        PhiArt storage art,
        uint256 newArtId,
        ERC1155Data memory createData_,
        uint256 credId,
        uint256 credChainId,
        string memory verificationType
    )
        private
        returns (address)
    {
        address payable newArt =
            payable(erc1155ArtAddress.cloneDeterministic(keccak256(abi.encodePacked(block.chainid, newArtId, credId))));

        art.artAddress = newArt;

        IPhiNFT1155Ownable(newArt).initialize(credChainId, credId, verificationType, protocolFeeDestination);
        IPhiNFT1155Ownable(newArt).transferOwnership(owner());

        credNFTContracts[credChainId][credId] = address(newArt);

        (bool success_, bytes memory response) =
            newArt.call{ value: msg.value }(abi.encodeWithSignature("createArtFromFactory(uint256)", newArtId));

        if (!success_) revert CreateFailed();
        uint256 tokenId = abi.decode(response, (uint256));
        emit ArtContractCreated(createData_.artist, address(newArt), credId);
        emit NewArtCreated(createData_.artist, credId, credChainId, newArtId, createData_.uri, address(newArt), tokenId);

        return address(newArt);
    }
```

In `PhiFactory._createNewNFTContract` and `PhiFactory._useExistingNFTContract`, forward only `artCreateFee` to `createArtFromFactory` and refund any excess `msg.value - artCreateFee` directly to `msg.sender` (checking that `msg.value >= artCreateFee`).

```solidity
    function _createNewNFTContract(
        PhiArt storage art,
        uint256 newArtId,
        ERC1155Data memory createData_,
        uint256 credId,
        uint256 credChainId,
        string memory verificationType
    )
        private
        returns (address)
    {
        if (msg.value < artCreateFee) revert InvalidEthValue();
        address payable newArt =
            payable(erc1155ArtAddress.cloneDeterministic(keccak256(abi.encodePacked(block.chainid, newArtId, credId))));

        art.artAddress = newArt;

        IPhiNFT1155Ownable(newArt).initialize(credChainId, credId, verificationType, protocolFeeDestination);

        credNFTContracts[credChainId][credId] = address(newArt);

        (bool success_, bytes memory response) =
            newArt.call{ value: artCreateFee }(abi.encodeWithSignature("createArtFromFactory(uint256)", newArtId));

        if (!success_) revert CreateFailed();
        uint256 tokenId = abi.decode(response, (uint256));
        emit ArtContractCreated(createData_.artist, address(newArt), credId);
        emit NewArtCreated(createData_.artist, credId, credChainId, newArtId, createData_.uri, address(newArt), tokenId);

        if (msg.value > artCreateFee) {
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(msg.value - artCreateFee);
        }

        return address(newArt);
    }

    function _useExistingNFTContract(
        PhiArt storage art,
        uint256 newArtId,
        ERC1155Data memory createData_,
        uint256 credId,
        uint256 credChainId
    )
        private
        returns (address)
    {
        if (msg.value < artCreateFee) revert InvalidEthValue();
        address existingArt = credNFTContracts[credChainId][credId];
        art.artAddress = existingArt;
        (bool success_, bytes memory response) =
            existingArt.call{ value: artCreateFee }(abi.encodeWithSignature("createArtFromFactory(uint256)", newArtId));

        if (!success_) revert CreateFailed();
        uint256 tokenId = abi.decode(response, (uint256));
        emit NewArtCreated(createData_.artist, credId, credChainId, newArtId, createData_.uri, existingArt, tokenId);

        if (msg.value > artCreateFee) {
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(msg.value - artCreateFee);
        }

        return existingArt;
    }
```

Isolate per-art configuration and state by storing `merkleRootHash` and `verificationType` per `artId` (or per `tokenId` within `PhiNFT1155`), or enforce in `_useExistingNFTContract` that the credential configuration matches the existing `PhiNFT1155` instance and isolate per-art state in `PhiFactory` and `PhiNFT1155`.

```solidity
    function _useExistingNFTContract(
        PhiArt storage art,
        uint256 newArtId,
        ERC1155Data memory createData_,
        uint256 credId,
        uint256 credChainId,
        string memory verificationType
    )
        private
        returns (address)
    {
        address existingArt = credNFTContracts[credChainId][credId];
        if (
            keccak256(bytes(IPhiNFT1155Ownable(existingArt).verificationType()))
                != keccak256(bytes(verificationType))
        ) {
            revert InvalidVerificationType();
        }
        art.artAddress = existingArt;
        (bool success_, bytes memory response) =
            existingArt.call{ value: msg.value }(abi.encodeWithSignature("createArtFromFactory(uint256)", newArtId));

        if (!success_) revert CreateFailed();
        uint256 tokenId = abi.decode(response, (uint256));
        emit NewArtCreated(createData_.artist, credId, credChainId, newArtId, createData_.uri, existingArt, tokenId);

        return existingArt;
    }
```

Additionally, store `merkleRootHash` per `artId` (e.g., `mapping(uint256 artId => bytes32) public artMerkleRoot` or inside the `PhiArt` struct) rather than overwriting `credMerkleRoot[credChainId][credId]`, and read `phiFactoryContract.protocolFeeDestination()` dynamically in `PhiNFT1155.createArtFromFactory`.

findings.technical_details:
The Phi Protocol documentation (Section 7.1, *Credential Creators*) specifies that credential creators earn verifier rewards (`verifierReward`) by performing `MERKLE` verification or acting as verifiers for `SIGNATURE` verification.

When a claim is processed through `PhiFactory._processClaim` and `PhiNFT1155.claimFromFactory`, `PhiNFT1155` retrieves the artist's `receiver` from `phiFactoryContract.artData(artId_).receiver`, encodes `(minter_, aristRewardReceiver, ref_, verifier_)` into `addressesData_`, and calls `PhiRewards.handleRewardsAndGetValueSent`.

In `PhiRewards.depositRewards` (`src/reward/PhiRewards.sol`), the recipient validation and reward distribution logic is implemented as follows:

```solidity
(address minter_, address receiver_, address referral_, address verifier_) =
    abi.decode(addressesData_, (address, address, address, address));

if (receiver_ == address(0) || minter_ == address(0) || verifier_ == address(0)) {
    revert InvalidAddressZero();
}

if (referral_ == minter_ || referral_ == address(0)) {
    artistTotalReward_ += referralTotalReward_;
    referralTotalReward_ = 0;
} else if (referral_ != address(0)) {
    balanceOf[referral_] += referralTotalReward_;
}

balanceOf[verifier_] += verifierTotalReward_;
balanceOf[receiver_] += artistTotalReward_;
```

This logic has a critical flaw in how `verifier_` is validated and rewarded:
1. Unlike `receiver_` (which `PhiNFT1155.claimFromFactory` resolves from `phiFactoryContract.artData(artId_).receiver`), `verifier_` is not verified against `artData(artId_).credCreator` (which is stored in `PhiFactory` and exposed via `artData(artId_).credCreator` at line 483 of `src/PhiFactory.sol`).
2. Unlike `referral_` at line 97 (which checks `referral_ == minter_ || referral_ == address(0)` and redirects the reward to `artistTotalReward_`), `depositRewards` only checks `verifier_ == address(0)` at line 93 and unconditionally credits `balanceOf[verifier_] += verifierTotalReward_` at line 104 without checking whether `verifier_ == minter_` or whether `verifier_` matches the authorized credential creator.

Consequently, whenever an unverified or self-matching `verifier_` address is passed in `addressesData_`, `PhiRewards.depositRewards` credits `verifierTotalReward_` (`quantity_ * verifierReward`) to that address instead of the legitimate credential creator or artist.

findings.remediation:
Validate `verifier_` against the authoritative credential creator (or apply the same self-dealing fallback check used for `referral_` so that when `verifier_ == minter_` or `verifier_ == address(0)`, `verifierTotalReward_` is redirected to the artist or credential creator):

```solidity
        if (receiver_ == address(0) || minter_ == address(0)) {
            revert InvalidAddressZero();
        }

        if (referral_ == minter_ || referral_ == address(0)) {
            artistTotalReward_ += referralTotalReward_;
            referralTotalReward_ = 0;
        } else {
            balanceOf[referral_] += referralTotalReward_;
        }

        if (verifier_ == minter_ || verifier_ == address(0)) {
            artistTotalReward_ += verifierTotalReward_;
            verifierTotalReward_ = 0;
        } else {
            balanceOf[verifier_] += verifierTotalReward_;
        }

        balanceOf[receiver_] += artistTotalReward_;
```

findings.technical_details:
When an NFT is claimed through `PhiFactory._processClaim`, the minter is charged `getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_)`, which calls `PhiRewards.computeMintReward(quantity_, mintFee_)`:

```solidity
function computeMintReward(uint256 quantity_, uint256 mintFee_) public view returns (uint256) {
    return quantity_ * (artistReward + mintFee_ + referralReward + verifierReward + curateReward);
}
```

The `referralReward` (`0.00005 ether` per minted token) is always included in the total fee paid by the minter. Per the protocol design (`docs/overview.md`, Section 5.1), when a mint occurs without a valid referral, the artist (`receiver_`) is entitled to receive both `artistReward` (`0.0001 ether`) and `referralReward` (`0.00005 ether`), totaling `0.00015 ether` per token (plus any paid `mintFee_`). When a valid referrer is present, the artist receives `0.0001 ether` and the referrer receives `0.00005 ether`.

To prevent a minter from designating themselves as the referrer to rebate `0.00005 ether` per token at the artist's expense, `PhiRewards.depositRewards` (`src/reward/PhiRewards.sol`, lines 97–102) performs the following check:

```solidity
if (referral_ == minter_ || referral_ == address(0)) {
    artistTotalReward_ += referralTotalReward_;
    referralTotalReward_ = 0;
} else if (referral_ != address(0)) {
    balanceOf[referral_] += referralTotalReward_;
}
```

This validation is insufficient to prevent self-referral:

1. **No referrer eligibility check in `PhiRewards`:** `depositRewards` only checks `referral_ == minter_` and does not verify whether `referral_` is an eligible referrer (for example, an existing holder of the credential/art token or a registered participant).
2. **Unconstrained referrer input in claim flows:** In Merkle claims (`test_claimMerkle`), the Merkle leaf only commits to `abi.encode(to, value)` (`minter_` and `data_`), while `referrer` is an unverified parameter encoded directly by the caller into the claim payload. Even in other claim flows, a user can trivially specify a second wallet address they control (`referral_ != minter_`).
3. **Reward diversion from `receiver_`:** By supplying any secondary controlled address `referral_ != minter_`, execution bypasses line 97 and enters lines 100–102, crediting `balanceOf[referral_] += referralTotalReward_` instead of adding `referralTotalReward_` to `artistTotalReward_` (line 98) and `balanceOf[receiver_]` (line 105).

findings.remediation:
Enforce referrer eligibility so that `referral_` must be a verified participant (for example, requiring `referral_` to have already minted/hold the art token or credential, and ensuring `referral_` is bound to the signed/Merkle-verified claim data rather than arbitrary calldata). If `referral_` is not an eligible referrer, redirect `referralTotalReward_` to `artistTotalReward_`:

```solidity
        if (referral_ == address(0) || referral_ == minter_ || !_isEligibleReferrer(artId_, credId_, referral_)) {
            artistTotalReward_ += referralTotalReward_;
            referralTotalReward_ = 0;
        } else {
            balanceOf[referral_] += referralTotalReward_;
        }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `src/reward/PhiRewards.sol`, `depositRewards` distributes minting rewards among the artist (`receiver_`), referral (`referral_`), verifier (`verifier_`), and curators:

```solidity
if (chainSync_ && address(curatorRewardsDistributor) != address(0)) {
    //slither-disable-next-line arbitrary-send-eth
    curatorRewardsDistributor.deposit{ value: curateTotalReward_ }(credId_, curateTotalReward_);
    rewardsData = abi.encode(artistTotalReward_, referralTotalReward_, verifierTotalReward_, curateTotalReward_);
} else {
    balanceOf[receiver_] += curateTotalReward_;
    rewardsData =
        abi.encode(artistTotalReward_ + curateTotalReward_, referralTotalReward_, verifierTotalReward_, 0);
}
```

When `PhiNFT1155.claimFromFactory` invokes `IPhiRewards.handleRewardsAndGetValueSent`, it passes `chainSync_ = (credChainId == block.chainid)`. If `chainSync_` is `true` and `curatorRewardsDistributor` is configured, `PhiRewards.depositRewards` unconditionally calls `curatorRewardsDistributor.deposit{ value: curateTotalReward_ }(credId_, curateTotalReward_)`.

However, `CuratorRewardsDistributor.deposit` enforces:

```solidity
if (!credContract.isExist(credId)) revert InvalidCredId();
```

where `Cred.isExist(credId_)` checks `creds[credId_].creator != address(0)`. Because `PhiRewards.depositRewards` neither checks whether `credId_` is valid/existing on the distributor's `credContract` nor wraps the external call to `curatorRewardsDistributor.deposit` in a `try/catch` block to fall back to the `else` branch (which credits `balanceOf[receiver_] += curateTotalReward_`), any revert in `curatorRewardsDistributor.deposit` (such as when `credId_` does not exist or has been cleared/invalidated in `credContract`) propagates up through `handleRewardsAndGetValueSent` and `PhiNFT1155.claimFromFactory`, causing the entire mint and reward distribution transaction to revert.

findings.remediation:
Wrap the external call to `curatorRewardsDistributor.deposit` in a `try/catch` block so that if the downstream deposit fails, the contract gracefully falls back to crediting `curateTotalReward_` to `receiver_`.

```solidity
        if (chainSync_ && address(curatorRewardsDistributor) != address(0)) {
            //slither-disable-next-line arbitrary-send-eth
            try curatorRewardsDistributor.deposit{ value: curateTotalReward_ }(credId_, curateTotalReward_) {
                rewardsData =
                    abi.encode(artistTotalReward_, referralTotalReward_, verifierTotalReward_, curateTotalReward_);
            } catch {
                balanceOf[receiver_] += curateTotalReward_;
                rewardsData =
                    abi.encode(artistTotalReward_ + curateTotalReward_, referralTotalReward_, verifierTotalReward_, 0);
            }
        } else {
            balanceOf[receiver_] += curateTotalReward_;
            rewardsData =
                abi.encode(artistTotalReward_ + curateTotalReward_, referralTotalReward_, verifierTotalReward_, 0);
        }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `src/Cred.sol`, shares purchased for a credential ID (`credId_`) are subject to a mandatory holding lock period defined by `SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD` (`10 minutes`). Whenever shares are bought, `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` is set to `block.timestamp` (line 644 in `_handleTrade` and line 759 in `_executeBatchTrade`). When a curator attempts to sell shares via `sellShareCred` (line 182) or `batchSellShareCred` (line 213), the contract enforces that the lock period has elapsed across the user's entire share balance for that credential:

```solidity
if (block.timestamp <= lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD) {
    revert ShareLockPeriodNotPassed(
        block.timestamp, lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD
    );
}
```

However, both `buyShareCredFor` (lines 186–189) and `batchBuyShareCred` (lines 191–211) accept an arbitrary `curator_` address parameter without requiring authorization or consent from `curator_`:

```solidity
function buyShareCredFor(uint256 credId_, uint256 amount_, address curator_, uint256 maxPrice_) public payable {
    if (curator_ == address(0)) revert InvalidAddressZero();
    _handleTrade(credId_, amount_, true, curator_, maxPrice_);
}
```

Because `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` tracks a single timestamp per `(credId_, curator_)` pair rather than per-purchase locks or sender-authorized actions, any third party can buy a minimal amount of shares (`amount_ = 1`) for a target `curator_` address. This overwrites `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` with the current `block.timestamp` and locks the victim's entire existing share balance for another `SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD` (`10 minutes`).

findings.remediation:
Remove the ability for arbitrary third parties to buy shares on behalf of another address (`buyShareCredFor` and the `curator_` parameter in `batchBuyShareCred`), or restrict delegated purchases so that either the beneficiary must explicitly opt-in/authorize the caller or `lastTradeTimestamp` is only enforced against `_msgSender()`.

The simplest fix is to remove `buyShareCredFor` and use `_msgSender()` as the curator in `batchBuyShareCred`:

```solidity
    function sellShareCred(uint256 credId_, uint256 amount_, uint256 minPrice_) public {
        _handleTrade(credId_, amount_, false, _msgSender(), minPrice_);
    }

    function batchBuyShareCred(
        uint256[] calldata credIds_,
        uint256[] calldata amounts_,
        uint256[] calldata maxPrices_
    )
        public
        payable
    {
        (uint256 totalCost, uint256[] memory prices, uint256[] memory protocolFees, uint256[] memory creatorFees) =
            _validateAndCalculateBatch(credIds_, amounts_, maxPrices_, true);

        if (totalCost > msg.value) revert InsufficientBatchPayment();

        _executeBatchBuy(credIds_, amounts_, _msgSender(), prices, protocolFees, creatorFees);

        uint256 refund = msg.value - totalCost;
        if (refund > 0) {
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(refund);
        }
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
The `Cred` contract enforces a `SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD` (10 minutes) between buying and selling shares of a credential (`credId_`) to prevent flash-loan-assisted manipulation and instant reward extraction from `CuratorRewardsDistributor`.

However, there is an inconsistent reentrancy protection and state finalization order across trade execution paths:
1. `_executeBatchTrade` (line 745) is protected by the `nonReentrant` modifier, whereas `_handleTrade` (line 588) — used by `buyShareCred`, `buyShareCredFor`, and `sellShareCred` — only uses `whenNotPaused` and has no `nonReentrant` guard.
2. During a buy operation in `_handleTrade` (`isBuy == true`), the curator's share balance and the credential's `currentSupply` are updated first (lines 636 and 639):
   ```solidity
   _updateCuratorShareBalance(credId_, curator_, amount_, isBuy);

   if (isBuy) {
       cred.currentSupply += amount_;
       uint256 excessPayment = msg.value - price - protocolFee - creatorFee;
       if (excessPayment > 0) {
           _msgSender().safeTransferETH(excessPayment);
       }
       lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] = block.timestamp;
   }
   ```
3. When `msg.value` exceeds `price + protocolFee + creatorFee`, `_msgSender().safeTransferETH(excessPayment)` (line 642) hands execution control to `_msgSender()` before `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` is set to `block.timestamp` (line 644) and before protocol/creator fees are transferred (lines 650–653).

When `_msgSender()` is a smart contract, its `receive()` fallback is invoked while the trade state is only partially finalized:
- `shareBalance[credId_]` already reflects the newly purchased shares (`amount_`).
- `cred.currentSupply` has already been incremented by `amount_`.
- `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` still holds its prior value (`0` if `curator_` has never bought shares for `credId_`, or a timestamp from a trade more than `SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD` ago).

Consequently, within the `receive()` callback, the attacker can immediately call `CuratorRewardsDistributor.distribute(credId_)` to capture curator rewards proportional to the newly acquired shares and then re-enter `Cred.sellShareCred(credId_, amount_, minPrice_)` (line 182) in the exact same transaction. In the re-entered `sellShareCred` call, the lock check at line 625:
```solidity
if (block.timestamp <= lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD)
```
evaluates against the stale `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` and succeeds, allowing the shares to be sold immediately within the same transaction.

findings.remediation:
Apply the `nonReentrant` modifier to `_handleTrade` (consistent with `_executeBatchTrade`) and follow the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern by updating `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` and `cred.latestActiveTimestamp` before performing any external ETH transfers or external calls:

```solidity
    function _handleTrade(
        uint256 credId_,
        uint256 amount_,
        bool isBuy,
        address curator_,
        uint256 priceLimit
    )
        internal
        whenNotPaused
        nonReentrant
    {
        if (amount_ == 0) {
            revert InvalidAmount();
        }

        if (!isExist(credId_)) {
            revert InvalidCredId();
        }

        PhiCred storage cred = creds[credId_];

        uint256 supply = cred.currentSupply;
        address creator = cred.creator;

        (uint256 price, uint256 protocolFee, uint256 creatorFee) =
            IBondingCurve(cred.bondingCurve).getPriceData(credId_, supply, amount_, isBuy);

        if (isBuy) {
            if (priceLimit != 0 && price + protocolFee + creatorFee > priceLimit) revert PriceExceedsLimit();
            if (supply + amount_ > MAX_SUPPLY) {
                revert MaxSupplyReached();
            }

            if (msg.value < price + protocolFee + creatorFee) {
                revert InsufficientPayment();
            }
        } else {
            if (priceLimit != 0 && price - protocolFee - creatorFee < priceLimit) revert PriceBelowLimit();
            if (block.timestamp <= lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD) {
                revert ShareLockPeriodNotPassed(
                    block.timestamp, lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD
                );
            }
            (, uint256 nums) = shareBalance[credId_].tryGet(curator_);
            if (nums < amount_) {
                revert InsufficientShares();
            }
        }

        _updateCuratorShareBalance(credId_, curator_, amount_, isBuy);
        cred.latestActiveTimestamp = block.timestamp;

        if (isBuy) {
            cred.currentSupply += amount_;
            lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] = block.timestamp;
            uint256 excessPayment = msg.value - price - protocolFee - creatorFee;
            if (excessPayment > 0) {
                _msgSender().safeTransferETH(excessPayment);
            }
        } else {
            cred.currentSupply -= amount_;
            curator_.safeTransferETH(price - protocolFee - creatorFee);
        }

        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(protocolFee);
        IPhiRewards(phiRewardsAddress).deposit{ value: creatorFee }(
            creator, bytes4(keccak256("CREATOR_ROYALTY_FEE")), ""
        );
        emit Royalty(creator, credId_, creatorFee);

        emit Trade(curator_, credId_, isBuy, amount_, price, protocolFee, cred.currentSupply);
    }
```

Update `FixedPriceBondingCurve.getPriceData` to only enforce the `MAX_SUPPLY` upper-bound check when `isSign_` is `true` (buying), and validate that `supply_ >= amount_` when `isSign_` is `false` (selling).

```solidity
    function getPriceData(
        uint256 credId_,
        uint256 supply_,
        uint256 amount_,
        bool isSign_
    )
        public
        view
        returns (uint256 price, uint256 protocolFee, uint256 creatorFee)
    {
        if (isSign_ ? supply_ + amount_ > MAX_SUPPLY : supply_ < amount_) revert InvalidSupply();
        price = FIXED_PRICE * amount_;
        protocolFee = _getProtocolFee(price);
        (uint16 buyShareRoyalty, uint16 sellShareRoyalty) = credContract.getCreatorRoyalty(credId_);
        uint16 royaltyRate = isSign_ ? buyShareRoyalty : sellShareRoyalty;
        creatorFee = (price * royaltyRate) / RATIO_BASE;
    }
```

Add the `nonReentrant` modifier to `_handleTrade` (consistent with `_executeBatchTrade`) and reorder the operations in `_handleTrade` to strictly follow the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern so that all state updates (`cred.currentSupply`, `lastTradeTimestamp`, `cred.latestActiveTimestamp`) and event emissions occur before external ETH transfers.

```solidity
    function _handleTrade(
        uint256 credId_,
        uint256 amount_,
        bool isBuy,
        address curator_,
        uint256 priceLimit
    )
        internal
        whenNotPaused
        nonReentrant
    {
        if (amount_ == 0) {
            revert InvalidAmount();
        }

        if (!isExist(credId_)) {
            revert InvalidCredId();
        }

        PhiCred storage cred = creds[credId_];

        uint256 supply = cred.currentSupply;
        address creator = cred.creator;

        (uint256 price, uint256 protocolFee, uint256 creatorFee) =
            IBondingCurve(cred.bondingCurve).getPriceData(credId_, supply, amount_, isBuy);

        if (isBuy) {
            if (priceLimit != 0 && price + protocolFee + creatorFee > priceLimit) revert PriceExceedsLimit();
            if (supply + amount_ > MAX_SUPPLY) {
                revert MaxSupplyReached();
            }

            if (msg.value < price + protocolFee + creatorFee) {
                revert InsufficientPayment();
            }
        } else {
            if (priceLimit != 0 && price - protocolFee - creatorFee < priceLimit) revert PriceBelowLimit();
            if (block.timestamp <= lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD) {
                revert ShareLockPeriodNotPassed(
                    block.timestamp, lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD
                );
            }
            (, uint256 nums) = shareBalance[credId_].tryGet(curator_);
            if (nums < amount_) {
                revert InsufficientShares();
            }
        }

        _updateCuratorShareBalance(credId_, curator_, amount_, isBuy);

        if (isBuy) {
            cred.currentSupply += amount_;
            lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] = block.timestamp;
        } else {
            cred.currentSupply -= amount_;
        }

        cred.latestActiveTimestamp = block.timestamp;

        emit Royalty(creator, credId_, creatorFee);
        emit Trade(curator_, credId_, isBuy, amount_, price, protocolFee, cred.currentSupply);

        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(protocolFee);
        IPhiRewards(phiRewardsAddress).deposit{ value: creatorFee }(
            creator, bytes4(keccak256("CREATOR_ROYALTY_FEE")), ""
        );

        if (isBuy) {
            uint256 excessPayment = msg.value - price - protocolFee - creatorFee;
            if (excessPayment > 0) {
                _msgSender().safeTransferETH(excessPayment);
            }
        } else {
            curator_.safeTransferETH(price - protocolFee - creatorFee);
        }
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
The `Cred` contract tracks per-credential curator share balances using an OpenZeppelin `EnumerableMap.AddressToUintMap`:

```solidity
mapping(uint256 credId => EnumerableMap.AddressToUintMap balance) private shareBalance;
```

In `_updateCuratorShareBalance` (`src/Cred.sol`, lines 666–682), when `isBuy` is `false` and the curator sells their entire balance (`(currentNum - amount_) == 0`), the contract cleans up the curator's credential tracking state (`_credIdsPerAddress` and `_credIdExistsPerAddress`), but still updates `shareBalance` via `.set`:

```solidity
if ((currentNum - amount_) == 0) {
    _removeCredIdPerAddress(credId_, sender_);
    _credIdExistsPerAddress[sender_][credId_] = false;
}
shareBalance[credId_].set(sender_, currentNum - amount_);
```

In OpenZeppelin's `EnumerableMap`, calling `set(key, 0)` does not remove `key` from the underlying `EnumerableSet.Bytes32Set _keys`; it merely sets `_values[key] = 0` while keeping `key` in `_keys`.

This inconsistent zero-balance membership transition causes several state integrity issues across `Cred`:

1. **Unbounded growth of `shareBalance[credId_]` and DoS of unpaginated queries / reward distribution:** Although active shares are capped at `MAX_SUPPLY = 999`, there is no limit on the number of distinct addresses that can buy shares and subsequently sell them after `SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD`. Every former curator remains permanently stored in `shareBalance[credId_]`. When `CuratorRewardsDistributor.distribute` (or any caller) invokes `getCuratorAddresses(credId_, 0, 0)`, `_getCuratorData` iterates from `0` to `shareBalance[credId_].length()`, performing storage reads (`at(i)` and `isShareHolder`) for every historical curator with a `0` balance. Over time or through repeated buy/sell cycles from multiple addresses, this loop can exceed block gas limits.
2. **Broken pagination in `_getCuratorData` (`getCuratorAddresses` and `getCuratorAddressesWithAmount`):** Because `start_` and `stop_` index into the raw `shareBalance[credId_]` map (which contains zero-balance former curators) and `_getCuratorData` filters out entries where `isShareHolder(credId_, key)` is `false`, a paginated query for range `[start_, stop_)` will return fewer than `stop_ - start_` curators—or an empty array—if the range is occupied by former curators.
3. **Incorrect curator count in `getCuratorAddressLength`:** `getCuratorAddressLength(credId_)` returns `shareBalance[credId_].length()`, which includes all historical curators with zero shares rather than current shareholders.

findings.remediation:
In `Cred._updateCuratorShareBalance`, remove `sender_` from `shareBalance[credId_]` when their remaining share balance reaches zero, and only call `shareBalance[credId_].set` when the remaining balance is non-zero:

```solidity
    function _updateCuratorShareBalance(uint256 credId_, address sender_, uint256 amount_, bool isBuy) internal {
        (, uint256 currentNum) = shareBalance[credId_].tryGet(sender_);

        if (isBuy) {
            if (currentNum == 0 && !_credIdExistsPerAddress[sender_][credId_]) {
                _addCredIdPerAddress(credId_, sender_);
                _credIdExistsPerAddress[sender_][credId_] = true;
            }
            shareBalance[credId_].set(sender_, currentNum + amount_);
        } else {
            if ((currentNum - amount_) == 0) {
                _removeCredIdPerAddress(credId_, sender_);
                _credIdExistsPerAddress[sender_][credId_] = false;
                shareBalance[credId_].remove(sender_);
            } else {
                shareBalance[credId_].set(sender_, currentNum - amount_);
            }
        }
    }
```

Change the visibility of `_addCredIdPerAddress` and `_removeCredIdPerAddress` from `public` to `internal` (or `private`) so they can only be invoked via `_updateCuratorShareBalance`.

```solidity
    // Function to add a new credId to the address's list
    function _addCredIdPerAddress(uint256 credId_, address sender_) internal {
        // Add the new credId to the array
        _credIdsPerAddress[sender_].push(credId_);
        // Store the index of the new credId
        _credIdsPerAddressCredIdIndex[sender_][credId_] = _credIdsPerAddressArrLength[sender_];
        // Increment the array length counter
        _credIdsPerAddressArrLength[sender_]++;
    }

    // Function to remove a credId from the address's list
    function _removeCredIdPerAddress(uint256 credId_, address sender_) internal {
        // Check if the array is empty
        if (_credIdsPerAddress[sender_].length == 0) revert EmptyArray();

        // Get the index of the credId to remove
        uint256 indexToRemove = _credIdsPerAddressCredIdIndex[sender_][credId_];
        // Check if the index is valid
        if (indexToRemove >= _credIdsPerAddress[sender_].length) revert IndexOutofBounds();

        // Verify that the credId at the index matches the one we want to remove
        uint256 credIdToRemove = _credIdsPerAddress[sender_][indexToRemove];
        if (credId_ != credIdToRemove) revert WrongCredId();

        // Get the last element in the array
        uint256 lastIndex = _credIdsPerAddress[sender_].length - 1;
        uint256 lastCredId = _credIdsPerAddress[sender_][lastIndex];
        // Move the last element to the position of the element we're removing
        _credIdsPerAddress[sender_][indexToRemove] = lastCredId;

        // Update the index of the moved element, if it's not the one we're removing
        if (indexToRemove < lastIndex) {
            _credIdsPerAddressCredIdIndex[sender_][lastCredId] = indexToRemove;
        }

        // Remove the last element (which is now a duplicate)
        _credIdsPerAddress[sender_].pop();

        // Remove the index mapping for the removed credId
        delete _credIdsPerAddressCredIdIndex[sender_][credIdToRemove];

        // Decrement the array length counter, if it's greater than 0
        if (_credIdsPerAddressArrLength[sender_] > 0) {
            _credIdsPerAddressArrLength[sender_]--;
        }
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `CuratorRewardsDistributor.sol`, rewards for a credential (`credId`) accumulate in `balanceOf[credId]` via `deposit()` as users mint art associated with that credential. However, reward distribution is deferred until someone calls the permissionless `distribute(uint256 credId)` function.

When `distribute(credId)` is executed, it fetches the current list of curators and their current share holdings at that exact block:

```solidity
uint256 royaltyfee = (totalBalance * withdrawRoyalty) / RATIO_BASE;
uint256 distributeAmount = totalBalance - royaltyfee;

uint256 actualDistributeAmount = 0;
for (uint256 i = 0; i < distributeAddresses.length; i++) {
    address user = distributeAddresses[i];

    uint256 userAmounts = credContract.getShareNumber(credId, user);
    uint256 userRewards = (distributeAmount * userAmounts) / totalNum;
```

Because `CuratorRewardsDistributor` does not track reward debt per share (such as a standard MasterChef / Synthetix `rewardPerTokenStored` accumulator updated on every share mint/burn and deposit) and `Cred` only enforces `SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD` (10 minutes) when selling shares (`sellShareCred`) rather than requiring shares to be held during reward accrual:

1. Newly purchased shares immediately qualify for 100% of previously accumulated, undistributed rewards in `balanceOf[credId]`.
2. Calling `distribute(credId)` is permissionless and can be bundled in the same transaction immediately after `Cred.buyShareCred`.
3. Furthermore, anyone can call `CuratorRewardsDistributor.deposit` directly or front-run an existing `distribute` call by purchasing shares up to `MAX_SUPPLY` (999), calling `distribute(credId)` to claim both the `royaltyfee` (1%) and the pro-rata share of `distributeAmount` (99%), and selling the shares back once the 10-minute `SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD` expires.

findings.remediation:
Replace the pull-based snapshot loop in `CuratorRewardsDistributor` with a continuous reward-per-share accumulator (e.g., MasterChef / Synthetix pattern) that updates whenever shares are bought or sold in `Cred` and whenever rewards are deposited.

Alternatively, if keeping the current architecture, `Cred` should call `curatorRewardsDistributor.distribute(credId)` (if `balanceOf[credId] > 0`) *before* modifying curator share balances in `_handleTrade` and `_executeBatchTrade`, ensuring all previously accrued rewards are distributed to existing shareholders prior to any new share purchase or sale:

```solidity
// In CuratorRewardsDistributor.sol, allow Cred or a hook to settle rewards before share changes,
// or track cumulative rewardPerShare on deposit:
mapping(uint256 credId => uint256) public accRewardPerShare;
mapping(uint256 credId => mapping(address curator => int256)) public rewardDebt;
```

findings.technical_details:
In `src/reward/CuratorRewardsDistributor.sol`, `distribute(uint256 credId)` computes the net distributable pool `distributeAmount` after subtracting the caller incentive `royaltyfee` (lines 98–99):

```solidity
uint256 royaltyfee = (totalBalance * withdrawRoyalty) / RATIO_BASE;
uint256 distributeAmount = totalBalance - royaltyfee;
```

It then iterates over `distributeAddresses` and calculates each curator's share of the reward pool using integer division (lines 106–116):

```solidity
uint256 actualDistributeAmount = 0;
for (uint256 i = 0; i < distributeAddresses.length; i++) {
    address user = distributeAddresses[i];

    uint256 userAmounts = credContract.getShareNumber(credId, user);
    uint256 userRewards = (distributeAmount * userAmounts) / totalNum;

    if (userRewards > 0) {
        amounts[i] = userRewards;
        actualDistributeAmount += userRewards;
    }
}
```

Following the loop, line 118 deducts the entire `totalBalance` from `balanceOf[credId]`, and line 120 transfers `royaltyfee + distributeAmount - actualDistributeAmount` to `_msgSender()`.

This creates a flawed accounting and reward attribution mechanism:

1. **Complete or Partial Reward Truncation**: Whenever `distributeAmount * userAmounts < totalNum` for a curator, `userRewards` evaluates to `0`. If this holds for all curators (for example, when `distributeAmount` is smaller than `totalNum / maxUserShares`), `actualDistributeAmount` is `0`. `phiRewardsContract.depositBatch{ value: 0 }(...)` does not revert when `msg.value == 0` and all elements in `amounts` are `0`.
2. **Residual Misattribution and Conservation Violation**: Instead of retaining the unallocated remainder `distributeAmount - actualDistributeAmount` in `balanceOf[credId]` (by only deducting `royaltyfee + actualDistributeAmount`) or requiring a minimum distribution threshold, the contract permanently removes the full `totalBalance` from `balanceOf[credId]` and sends the unallocated curator rewards to `_msgSender()`.

findings.remediation:
Keep unallocated rounding dust in `balanceOf[credId]` by only deducting the actually distributed amount plus the royalty fee, transfer only `royaltyfee` to `_msgSender()`, and revert if `actualDistributeAmount == 0`.

```solidity
        uint256 royaltyfee = (totalBalance * withdrawRoyalty) / RATIO_BASE;
        uint256 distributeAmount = totalBalance - royaltyfee;

        uint256 actualDistributeAmount = 0;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < distributeAddresses.length; i++) {
            address user = distributeAddresses[i];

            uint256 userAmounts = credContract.getShareNumber(credId, user);
            uint256 userRewards = (distributeAmount * userAmounts) / totalNum;

            if (userRewards > 0) {
                amounts[i] = userRewards;
                actualDistributeAmount += userRewards;
            }
        }

        if (actualDistributeAmount == 0) {
            revert NoBalanceToDistribute();
        }

        balanceOf[credId] -= (actualDistributeAmount + royaltyfee);

        if (royaltyfee > 0) {
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(royaltyfee);
        }

        //slither-disable-next-line arbitrary-send-eth
        phiRewardsContract.depositBatch{ value: actualDistributeAmount }(
            distributeAddresses, amounts, reasons, "deposit from curator rewards distributor"
        );

        emit RewardsDistributed(
            credId, _msgSender(), royaltyfee, actualDistributeAmount, actualDistributeAmount + royaltyfee
        );
```

Prevent `soulBounded` from being modified once any tokens for `artId_` have been minted (or remove `soulBounded_` from `updateArtSettings` so that transferability is immutable after creation).

```solidity
        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        if (art.numberMinted > maxSupply_) {
            revert ExceedMaxSupply();
        }
        if (art.numberMinted > 0 && art.soulBounded != soulBounded_) {
            revert SoulBoundedCannotBeChanged();
        }

        art.receiver = receiver_;
        art.maxSupply = maxSupply_;
        art.mintFee = mintFee_;
        art.startTime = startTime_;
        art.endTime = endTime_;
        art.soulBounded = soulBounded_;
        art.uri = url_;
```

findings.technical_details:
In `PhiFactory.sol`, the function `updateArtSettings` (lines 215–257) allows the creator of an art piece (`onlyArtCreator(artId_)`) to update various parameters of their art, including its secondary market royalty configuration (`IPhiNFT1155Ownable.RoyaltyConfiguration memory configuration`):

```solidity
uint256 tokenId = IPhiNFT1155Ownable(art.artAddress).getTokenIdFromFactoryArtId(artId_);
IPhiNFT1155Ownable(art.artAddress).updateRoyalties(tokenId, configuration);
```

This call invokes `PhiNFT1155.updateRoyalties` (which can also be called directly by the artist), which in turn calls `CreatorRoyaltiesControl._updateRoyalties`:

```solidity
function _updateRoyalties(uint256 tokenId, RoyaltyConfiguration memory configuration) internal {
    if (configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0) && configuration.royaltyBPS > 0) {
        revert InvalidRoyaltyRecipient();
    }

    royalties[tokenId] = configuration;

    emit UpdatedRoyalties(tokenId, msg.sender, configuration);
}
```

In `CreatorRoyaltiesControl`, `ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT` is defined as `10_000` (representing 100% in basis points), and `royaltyInfo` calculates secondary sale royalties as:

```solidity
function royaltyInfo(
    uint256 tokenId,
    uint256 salePrice
)
    public
    view
    returns (address receiver, uint256 royaltyAmount)
{
    RoyaltyConfiguration memory config = getRoyalties(tokenId);
    royaltyAmount = (config.royaltyBPS * salePrice) / ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT;
    receiver = config.royaltyRecipient;
}
```

Neither `PhiFactory.updateArtSettings` nor `CreatorRoyaltiesControl._updateRoyalties` checks that `configuration.royaltyBPS <= ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT` (or a reasonable protocol maximum). Because `royaltyBPS` is a `uint32`, an art creator can set `royaltyBPS` to any value up to `4,294,967,295` (~429,496%). When `royaltyBPS > 10_000`, `royaltyInfo` returns a `royaltyAmount` strictly greater than `salePrice`.

Under EIP-2981, marketplaces deduct `royaltyAmount` from `salePrice` to pay the royalty recipient and send the remainder (`salePrice - royaltyAmount`) to the seller. When `royaltyAmount > salePrice`, this subtraction underflows and reverts, effectively freezing secondary market trading of the NFT on compliant marketplaces (or allowing the artist to front-run a secondary sale settlement to cause a denial of service).

findings.remediation:
Validate that `configuration.royaltyBPS` does not exceed `ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT` (`10_000`) when updating royalties in `PhiFactory.updateArtSettings` and `CreatorRoyaltiesControl._updateRoyalties`.

In `src/PhiFactory.sol`:
```solidity
    function updateArtSettings(
        uint256 artId_,
        string memory url_,
        address receiver_,
        uint256 maxSupply_,
        uint256 mintFee_,
        uint256 startTime_,
        uint256 endTime_,
        bool soulBounded_,
        IPhiNFT1155Ownable.RoyaltyConfiguration memory configuration
    )
        external
        onlyArtCreator(artId_)
    {
        if (receiver_ == address(0)) {
            revert InvalidAddressZero();
        }

        if (endTime_ < startTime_) {
            revert InvalidTimeRange();
        }
        if (endTime_ < block.timestamp) {
            revert EndTimeInPast();
        }

        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        if (art.numberMinted > maxSupply_) {
            revert ExceedMaxSupply();
        }

        art.receiver = receiver_;
        art.maxSupply = maxSupply_;
        art.mintFee = mintFee_;
        art.startTime = startTime_;
        art.endTime = endTime_;
        art.soulBounded = soulBounded_;
        art.uri = url_;

        uint256 tokenId = IPhiNFT1155Ownable(art.artAddress).getTokenIdFromFactoryArtId(artId_);
        IPhiNFT1155Ownable(art.artAddress).updateRoyalties(tokenId, configuration);
        emit ArtUpdated(artId_, url_, receiver_, maxSupply_, mintFee_, startTime_, endTime_, soulBounded_);
    }
```

And in `src/abstract/CreatorRoyaltiesControl.sol`:
```solidity
    error InvalidRoyaltyBPS();

    function _updateRoyalties(uint256 tokenId, RoyaltyConfiguration memory configuration) internal {
        if (configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0) && configuration.royaltyBPS > 0) {
            revert InvalidRoyaltyRecipient();
        }
        if (configuration.royaltyBPS > ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT) {
            revert InvalidRoyaltyBPS();
        }

        royalties[tokenId] = configuration;

        emit UpdatedRoyalties(tokenId, msg.sender, configuration);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
The claim flow in `PhiFactory` supports three invocation paths, as reflected in `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` (line 706):
```solidity
if (tx.origin != _msgSender() && msg.sender != art.artAddress && msg.sender != address(this)) {
    revert TxOriginMismatch();
}
```
However, native ETH value validation (`_validateAndUpdateClaimState`, line 709) and excess fee refunding (`_processClaim`, lines 723–741) fail to preserve per-call funding isolation, value attribution across nested self-calls, and the true refund beneficiary:

1. **Refund Beneficiary Misattribution in `_processClaim` (lines 738–741):**
   ```solidity
   uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_);
   if ((etherValue_ - mintFee) > 0) {
       _msgSender().safeTransferETH(etherValue_ - mintFee);
   }
   ```
   - When a user claims through `PhiNFT1155.signatureClaim()` or `PhiNFT1155.merkleClaim()` (inherited from `Claimable.sol`, lines 22–65), `PhiNFT1155` forwards `{ value: msg.value }` to `PhiFactory`. Inside `PhiFactory`, `_msgSender()` resolves to `msg.sender`, which is `art.artAddress` (`PhiNFT1155`) rather than the user who paid for the transaction. If the user sends any ETH above `mintFee`, `_processClaim` attempts to transfer the refund (`etherValue_ - mintFee`) to `PhiNFT1155`. Because `PhiNFT1155` has no `receive()` or payable `fallback()` function, `safeTransferETH` reverts with `ETHTransferFailed()`, DoSing any overpaid claim via `PhiNFT1155` (or sending the refund to the NFT contract rather than the user).
   - Similarly, when `PhiFactory` invokes `this.merkleClaim` or `this.signatureClaim` via an external self-call, `_msgSender()` inside the self-call is `address(this)` (`PhiFactory`), not the user.

2. **Lost Excess Value and Shared-Balance Consumption Across Self-Calls in `claim` and `batchClaim` (lines 264–321):**
   - In `PhiFactory.claim` (lines 282–283 and 299–300):
     ```solidity
     uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId, quantity_);
     this.merkleClaim{ value: mintFee }(proof, claimData, mintArgs, leafPart_);
     ```
     and
     ```solidity
     uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId, quantity_);
     this.signatureClaim{ value: mintFee }(signature_, claimData, mintArgs);
     ```
   - `claim()` never checks that the caller's `msg.value >= mintFee`. Instead, it initiates an external call to `this.merkleClaim` or `this.signatureClaim` with `{ value: mintFee }` funded from `address(this).balance`.
   - Inside the nested call, `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` (line 709) checks `if (msg.value < getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_)) revert InvalidMintFee();`, which always passes because the self-call's `msg.value` was explicitly set to `mintFee`.
   - Inside `_processClaim`, `etherValue_` receives the self-call's `msg.value` (`mintFee`), so `etherValue_ - mintFee` is always `0`. Consequently:
     - If a user sends `msg.value > mintFee` to `claim()` (or specifies `ethValue_[i] > mintFee` in `batchClaim()`), the excess ETH is never forwarded to `_processClaim` and is never refunded, remaining permanently stuck in `PhiFactory`.
     - Because `this.merkleClaim{ value: mintFee }` and `this.signatureClaim{ value: mintFee }` draw from `PhiFactory`'s contract balance without validating the outer `msg.value`, any user can call `claim()` with `msg.value < mintFee` (including `msg.value == 0`) to consume any ETH sitting in `PhiFactory` to pay for their own mints.

findings.remediation:
Refactor the claim functions to use internal helpers rather than external self-calls (`this.`), explicitly track per-call ETH value in `claim` and `batchClaim`, and send refunds to the actual payer (`tx.origin`, which `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` already enforces to be the initiator, or an explicit `refundRecipient` parameter):

```solidity
    function claim(bytes calldata encodeData_) external payable {
        _claim(encodeData_, msg.value);
    }

    function batchClaim(bytes[] calldata encodeDatas_, uint256[] calldata ethValue_) external payable {
        if (encodeDatas_.length != ethValue_.length) revert ArrayLengthMismatch();

        uint256 totalEthValue;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < encodeDatas_.length; i++) {
            totalEthValue = totalEthValue + ethValue_[i];
        }
        if (msg.value != totalEthValue) revert InvalidEthValue();

        for (uint256 i = 0; i < encodeDatas_.length; i++) {
            _claim(encodeDatas_[i], ethValue_[i]);
        }
    }

    function _validateAndUpdateClaimState(
        uint256 artId_,
        address minter_,
        uint256 quantity_,
        uint256 etherValue_
    )
        private
    {
        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        // Common validations
        if (tx.origin != _msgSender() && msg.sender != art.artAddress && msg.sender != address(this)) {
            revert TxOriginMismatch();
        }
        if (etherValue_ < getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_)) revert InvalidMintFee();
        if (block.timestamp < art.startTime) revert ArtNotStarted();
        if (block.timestamp > art.endTime) revert ArtEnded();
        if (quantity_ == 0) revert InvalidQuantity();
        if (art.numberMinted + quantity_ > art.maxSupply) revert OverMaxAllowedToMint();

        // Common state updates
        if (!credMinted[art.credChainId][art.credId][minter_]) {
            credMinted[art.credChainId][art.credId][minter_] = true;
        }
        artMinted[artId_][minter_] = true;
        art.numberMinted += quantity_;
    }

    function _processClaim(
        uint256 artId_,
        address minter_,
        address ref_,
        address verifier_,
        uint256 quantity_,
        bytes32 data_,
        string memory imageURI_,
        uint256 etherValue_
    )
        private
    {
        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        // Handle refund to the transaction initiator
        uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_);
        if ((etherValue_ - mintFee) > 0) {
            tx.origin.safeTransferETH(etherValue_ - mintFee);
        }
        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(mintProtocolFee * quantity_);

        (bool success_,) = art.artAddress.call{ value: mintFee - mintProtocolFee * quantity_ }(
            abi.encodeWithSignature(
                "claimFromFactory(uint256,address,address,address,uint256,bytes32,string)",
                artId_,
                minter_,
                ref_,
                verifier_,
                quantity_,
                data_,
                imageURI_
            )
        );

        if (!success_) revert ClaimFailed();
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `src/PhiFactory.sol`, `merkleClaim` decodes the claim context from `encodeData_` and verifies inclusion against `credMerkleRoot[art.credChainId][art.credId]`:

```solidity
(address minter_, address ref_, uint256 artId_) = abi.decode(encodeData_, (address, address, uint256));
PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

bytes32 credMerkleRootHash = credMerkleRoot[art.credChainId][art.credId];
if (minter_ == address(0) || !art.verificationType.eq("MERKLE") || credMerkleRootHash == bytes32(0)) {
    revert InvalidMerkleProof();
}
if (
    !MerkleProofLib.verifyCalldata(
        proof_, credMerkleRootHash, keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(minter_, leafPart_))))
    )
) {
    revert InvalidMerkleProof();
}
```

Unlike `signatureClaim`—which hashes `encodeData_` containing `(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId_, block.chainid, data_)`—`merkleClaim` only hashes `abi.encode(minter_, leafPart_)` when constructing the leaf on line 371. This creates three intertwined authorization binding weaknesses:

1. **Incomplete Leaf-to-Claim Parameter Binding:** The parameters decoded from `encodeData_` (`ref_`, `artId_`) and passed via `mintArgs_` (`tokenId`, `quantity`, `imageURI`) are never committed to the Merkle leaf. A caller with a valid `(minter_, leafPart_)` proof can freely alter `ref_` (e.g., to self-refer or redirect referral fees), `quantity`, `imageURI`, and `artId_`. Furthermore, because `merkleClaim` does not verify that `_msgSender() == minter_`, any third party can take a publicly available `(minter_, leafPart_, proof_)` tuple from Arweave or the mempool and submit a claim on behalf of `minter_` while injecting their own `ref_` address or arbitrary `imageURI`.
2. **Insufficient Proof Domain Separation:** The leaf hash `keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(minter_, leafPart_))))` lacks domain parameters such as `block.chainid`, `address(this)`, `artId_`, `credId`, or an expiration/nonce. Consequently, a single proof is valid across chains and can be replayed repeatedly up to `art.maxSupply`.
3. **Credential-to-Root Association:** Because `credMerkleRoot` is keyed by `[art.credChainId][art.credId]` and the leaf omits `artId_`, a valid proof for one credential automatically authorizes claims across any `PhiArt` linked to the same `(credChainId, credId)` (or sharing the same Merkle root) without art-specific authorization.

findings.remediation:
Bind all security-critical claim parameters and domain identifiers into the Merkle leaf (or require `msg.sender == minter_` and include `artId_`, `credId`, `block.chainid`, and authorized claim metadata in the leaf hash):

```solidity
    function merkleClaim(
        bytes32[] calldata proof_,
        bytes calldata encodeData_,
        MintArgs calldata mintArgs_,
        bytes32 leafPart_
    )
        external
        payable
        whenNotPaused
    {
        (address minter_, address ref_, uint256 artId_) = abi.decode(encodeData_, (address, address, uint256));
        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        bytes32 credMerkleRootHash = credMerkleRoot[art.credChainId][art.credId];
        if (
            minter_ == address(0) || !art.verificationType.eq("MERKLE") || credMerkleRootHash == bytes32(0)
        ) {
            revert InvalidMerkleProof();
        }
        bytes32 leaf = keccak256(
            bytes.concat(
                keccak256(
                    abi.encode(
                        block.chainid,
                        address(this),
                        art.credChainId,
                        art.credId,
                        artId_,
                        minter_,
                        ref_,
                        mintArgs_.tokenId,
                        mintArgs_.quantity,
                        keccak256(bytes(mintArgs_.imageURI)),
                        leafPart_
                    )
                )
            )
        );
        if (!MerkleProofLib.verifyCalldata(proof_, credMerkleRootHash, leaf)) {
            revert InvalidMerkleProof();
        }

        _validateAndUpdateClaimState(artId_, minter_, mintArgs_.quantity);
        _processClaim(
            artId_, minter_, ref_, art.credCreator, mintArgs_.quantity, leafPart_, mintArgs_.imageURI, msg.value
        );

        emit ArtClaimedData(artId_, "MERKLE", minter_, ref_, art.credCreator, art.artAddress, mintArgs_.quantity);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `src/PhiFactory.sol`, `merkleClaim` decodes `minter_`, `ref_`, and `artId_` from the caller-supplied `encodeData_` parameter (line 362) and checks that `(minter_, leafPart_)` is part of the Merkle tree associated with `credMerkleRoot[art.credChainId][art.credId]` (lines 365–375):

```solidity
(address minter_, address ref_, uint256 artId_) = abi.decode(encodeData_, (address, address, uint256));
PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

bytes32 credMerkleRootHash = credMerkleRoot[art.credChainId][art.credId];
if (minter_ == address(0) || !art.verificationType.eq("MERKLE") || credMerkleRootHash == bytes32(0)) {
    revert InvalidMerkleProof();
}
if (
    !MerkleProofLib.verifyCalldata(
        proof_, credMerkleRootHash, keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(minter_, leafPart_))))
    )
) {
    revert InvalidMerkleProof();
}
```

According to the protocol documentation (`docs/arweave.md`), the address list and Merkle tree for `MERKLE`-type credentials are stored publicly on Arweave. Because `merkleClaim` only checks `minter_ == address(0)` on line 366 and never verifies that `minter_` matches the caller (`_msgSender()` on direct calls, or the initiating caller when routed through `PhiFactory.claim` or `Claimable.merkleClaim`), the Merkle proof acts only as proof that `minter_` is eligible, not that `minter_` authorized the call.

Furthermore, `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` (line 706) only checks:
```solidity
if (tx.origin != _msgSender() && msg.sender != art.artAddress && msg.sender != address(this)) {
    revert TxOriginMismatch();
}
```
This does not bind `minter_` to `_msgSender()` or `tx.origin`. As a result, any EOA can pass a valid `(minter_, leafPart_, proof_)` tuple for another user and supply arbitrary `ref_`, `mintArgs_.quantity`, and `mintArgs_.imageURI` values.

findings.remediation:
Validate in `merkleClaim` that the transaction caller authorized the claim as `minter_`. Because `merkleClaim` can be invoked directly on `PhiFactory`, via `PhiFactory.claim` (`msg.sender == address(this)`), or via `Claimable.merkleClaim` on the `art.artAddress` contract (`msg.sender == art.artAddress`), verify that `minter_` matches `_msgSender()` on direct calls or `tx.origin` when routed through the factory or art contract:

```solidity
    function merkleClaim(
        bytes32[] calldata proof_,
        bytes calldata encodeData_,
        MintArgs calldata mintArgs_,
        bytes32 leafPart_
    )
        external
        payable
        whenNotPaused
    {
        (address minter_, address ref_, uint256 artId_) = abi.decode(encodeData_, (address, address, uint256));
        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        address caller = (msg.sender == address(this) || msg.sender == art.artAddress) ? tx.origin : _msgSender();
        if (minter_ != caller) {
            revert TxOriginMismatch();
        }

        bytes32 credMerkleRootHash = credMerkleRoot[art.credChainId][art.credId];
        if (minter_ == address(0) || !art.verificationType.eq("MERKLE") || credMerkleRootHash == bytes32(0)) {
            revert InvalidMerkleProof();
        }
        if (
            !MerkleProofLib.verifyCalldata(
                proof_, credMerkleRootHash, keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(minter_, leafPart_))))
            )
        ) {
            revert InvalidMerkleProof();
        }

        _validateAndUpdateClaimState(artId_, minter_, mintArgs_.quantity);
        _processClaim(
            artId_, minter_, ref_, art.credCreator, mintArgs_.quantity, leafPart_, mintArgs_.imageURI, msg.value
        );

        emit ArtClaimedData(artId_, "MERKLE", minter_, ref_, art.credCreator, art.artAddress, mintArgs_.quantity);
    }
```

Include `ref_` in the Merkle leaf and regenerate the corresponding trees and roots. The off-chain tree generator and on-chain verification must use exactly the same encoding.

```solidity
bytes32 leaf =
    keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(minter_, ref_, leafPart_))));

if (!MerkleProofLib.verifyCalldata(proof_, credMerkleRootHash, leaf)) {
    revert InvalidMerkleProof();
}
```

Existing roots should be versioned or replaced because old proofs will not authenticate the referrer. If referrers must remain selectable after tree creation, require a separate minter signature authorizing the selected referrer. As a restrictive alternative, disable referral rewards for Merkle claims rather than forwarding unauthenticated referral addresses.

findings.technical_details:
In `src/PhiFactory.sol`, both `signatureClaim` (line 342) and `merkleClaim` (line 377) call `_validateAndUpdateClaimState(artId_, minter_, mintArgs_.quantity)` before executing `_processClaim`.

Within `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` (lines 702–721), the contract performs several validations (lines 706–713) and then updates the claim tracking state (lines 716–720):

```solidity
        // Common state updates
        if (!credMinted[art.credChainId][art.credId][minter_]) {
            credMinted[art.credChainId][art.credId][minter_] = true;
        }
        artMinted[artId_][minter_] = true;
        art.numberMinted += quantity_;
```

Although `IPhiFactory` defines `error AddressAlreadyMinted()` (line 10) and `PhiFactory` tracks per-user claims in `artMinted[artId_][minter_]` (exposed via `isArtMinted`), `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` never checks `if (artMinted[artId_][minter_]) revert AddressAlreadyMinted();` prior to setting `artMinted[artId_][minter_] = true`.

Furthermore:
1. In `merkleClaim`, the Merkle proof only validates `(minter_, leafPart_)` against `credMerkleRoot[art.credChainId][art.credId]`, which remains static across calls.
2. In `signatureClaim`, the signature is computed over `(expiresIn_, minter_, ref_, verifier_, artId_, block.chainid, data_)` without a nonce, remaining valid until `expiresIn_`.
3. During `_processClaim`, `PhiNFT1155.claimFromFactory` mints ERC1155 tokens to `minter_`, invoking `onERC1155Received` on contract recipients, which also permits re-entering the claim functions via `Claimable` or `PhiFactory`.

Because `artMinted[artId_][minter_]` is never checked, an eligible minter can reuse the exact same Merkle proof or un-expired signature across multiple calls (or within a single `batchClaim` call) to mint NFTs repeatedly until `art.maxSupply` is exhausted.

findings.remediation:
Check whether `artMinted[artId_][minter_]` is already `true` in `_validateAndUpdateClaimState` and revert with `AddressAlreadyMinted()` before updating state.

```solidity
    function _validateAndUpdateClaimState(uint256 artId_, address minter_, uint256 quantity_) private {
        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        // Common validations
        if (tx.origin != _msgSender() && msg.sender != art.artAddress && msg.sender != address(this)) {
            revert TxOriginMismatch();
        }
        if (msg.value < getArtMintFee(artId_, quantity_)) revert InvalidMintFee();
        if (block.timestamp < art.startTime) revert ArtNotStarted();
        if (block.timestamp > art.endTime) revert ArtEnded();
        if (quantity_ == 0) revert InvalidQuantity();
        if (art.numberMinted + quantity_ > art.maxSupply) revert OverMaxAllowedToMint();
        if (artMinted[artId_][minter_]) revert AddressAlreadyMinted();

        // Common state updates
        if (!credMinted[art.credChainId][art.credId][minter_]) {
            credMinted[art.credChainId][art.credId][minter_] = true;
        }
        artMinted[artId_][minter_] = true;
        art.numberMinted += quantity_;
    }
```

Validate the outer call’s payment before making either self-call. Prefer requiring exact payment so that no shared factory balance can subsidize the operation:

```solidity
uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId, quantity_);
if (msg.value != mintFee) revert InvalidMintFee();
this.merkleClaim{ value: msg.value }(proof, claimData, mintArgs, leafPart_);
```

```solidity
uint256 mintFee = getArtMintFee(artId, quantity_);
if (msg.value != mintFee) revert InvalidMintFee();
this.signatureClaim{ value: msg.value }(signature_, claimData, mintArgs);
```

As a stronger architectural alternative, remove the external self-calls and route both claim variants through internal functions that retain the original payment context. For batch claims, require every supplied per-claim value to match that claim’s calculated fee before execution.

A check inside `_processClaim` that merely compares `etherValue_` with `mintFee` is insufficient because the self-call already manufactures `etherValue_ == mintFee`.

findings.technical_details:
When a user claims an art token via `PhiFactory.claim`, `PhiFactory.merkleClaim`, or `PhiFactory.signatureClaim` (or through `Claimable`), `PhiFactory._processClaim` invokes `PhiNFT1155.claimFromFactory`, which calls `PhiNFT1155.mint`:

```solidity
function mint(
    address to_,
    uint256 tokenId_,
    uint256 quantity_,
    string calldata imageURI_,
    bytes32 data_
)
    internal
{
    minterData[tokenId_][to_] = data_;
    advancedTokenURI[tokenId_][to_] = imageURI_;
    if (!minted[to_]) {
        minted[to_] = true;
    }

    _mint(to_, tokenId_, quantity_, "0x00");
}
```

This design exhibits three compounding issues centered on `PhiNFT1155.mint`:

1. **Unverified `imageURI_` parameter:** In both `PhiFactory.signatureClaim` (lines 336–340) and `PhiFactory.merkleClaim` (lines 370–372), `mintArgs_.imageURI` is passed outside of `encodeData_` and the Merkle leaf (`keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(minter_, leafPart_))))`). Thus, `imageURI_` is never cryptographically bound to the signer's signature or the credential's Merkle root.
2. **Lack of caller-to-recipient authorization:** Neither `PhiFactory` nor `PhiNFT1155.mint` requires the transaction sender (`tx.origin` / `_msgSender()`) to match the recipient `to_` (`minter_`). Anyone can submit a claim transaction specifying another eligible user as `minter_`.
3. **Unconditional overwriting on repeat mints:** The protocol permits multiple claims for the same art token by the same `minter_`, and signatures/Merkle proofs are reusable until `art.endTime` or `expiresIn_`. In `PhiNFT1155.mint` (lines 292–293), `minterData[tokenId_][to_]` and `advancedTokenURI[tokenId_][to_]` are blindly overwritten on each mint without checking if they are already populated or if the recipient initiated the update.

Consequently, `PhiNFT1155.uri(uint256 tokenId_, address minter_)` (lines 241–246), which returns `advancedTokenURI[tokenId_][minter_]` whenever its byte length is non-zero, will return attacker-supplied URI strings for the victim `to_`.

findings.remediation:
Ensure that `minterData` and `advancedTokenURI` can only be set or updated when the transaction initiator is the recipient `to_` (or prevent overwriting already-initialized recipient metadata unless authorized by `to_`), and bind `imageURI_` into the signed/Merkle-verified claim data. Within `PhiNFT1155.mint`:

```solidity
    function mint(
        address to_,
        uint256 tokenId_,
        uint256 quantity_,
        string calldata imageURI_,
        bytes32 data_
    )
        internal
    {
        if (tx.origin == to_) {
            minterData[tokenId_][to_] = data_;
            advancedTokenURI[tokenId_][to_] = imageURI_;
        }
        if (!minted[to_]) {
            minted[to_] = true;
        }

        _mint(to_, tokenId_, quantity_, "0x00");
    }
```

Prefer enforcing soulbound status in the common ERC-1155 update path so the policy applies consistently to single and batch transfers and to any future transfer entry points. Permit minting, and optionally burning, by applying the restriction only when both endpoints are nonzero.

```solidity
function _update(
    address from_,
    address to_,
    uint256[] memory ids_,
    uint256[] memory values_
)
    internal
    override
{
    if (from_ != address(0) && to_ != address(0)) {
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < ids_.length; ++i) {
            if (soulBounded(ids_[i])) revert TokenNotTransferable();
        }
    }

    super._update(from_, to_, ids_, values_);
}
```

As a less robust alternative, explicitly override `safeBatchTransferFrom` and check every token ID before calling the parent implementation. Centralized enforcement in `_update` is preferred because it cannot be bypassed by another transfer wrapper.

findings.technical_details:
The `Cred` contract enforces a `SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD` (`10 minutes`) between buying and selling shares of a credential (`credId_`) to prevent flash-loan attacks where an actor temporarily buys a large number of shares, triggers `CuratorRewardsDistributor.distribute(credId_)`, captures the majority of curator rewards, and immediately sells the shares back in the same transaction.

However, in `Cred._handleTrade` (`src/Cred.sol#L588-L659`), which is invoked by `buyShareCred` (`src/Cred.sol#L178`), `buyShareCredFor` (`src/Cred.sol#L186`), and `sellShareCred` (`src/Cred.sol#L182`):

1. Unlike `_executeBatchTrade` (`src/Cred.sol#L745`), `_handleTrade` does not have the `nonReentrant` modifier.
2. When `isBuy` is `true`, the curator's share balance and `cred.currentSupply` are incremented first (`src/Cred.sol#L636-L639`):
   ```solidity
   _updateCuratorShareBalance(credId_, curator_, amount_, isBuy);

   if (isBuy) {
       cred.currentSupply += amount_;
       uint256 excessPayment = msg.value - price - protocolFee - creatorFee;
       if (excessPayment > 0) {
           _msgSender().safeTransferETH(excessPayment);
       }
       lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] = block.timestamp;
   }
   ```
3. If the caller supplies `msg.value > price + protocolFee + creatorFee` (so `excessPayment > 0`), `_msgSender().safeTransferETH(excessPayment)` at line 642 makes an external call to `_msgSender()` **before** `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` is updated to `block.timestamp` at line 644.

During the `receive()` callback triggered by `safeTransferETH(excessPayment)`:
- `shareBalance[credId_]` for `curator_` and `cred.currentSupply` have already been updated with the newly purchased `amount_`.
- `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` still holds its prior value (`0` if the address has never bought shares for `credId_`, or a timestamp older than `10 minutes`).

Consequently, inside the callback, the attacker contract can:
1. Call `CuratorRewardsDistributor.distribute(credId_)` while holding the newly purchased shares to receive a pro-rata share of all accumulated rewards for `credId_`.
2. Re-enter `Cred.sellShareCred(credId_, amount_, minPrice_)` (`src/Cred.sol#L182`) (or `batchSellShareCred` at `src/Cred.sol#L213`).
3. In the re-entered `_handleTrade` sell path (`src/Cred.sol#L625`), the lock check:
   ```solidity
   if (block.timestamp <= lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD)
   ```
   evaluates against the stale `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` (`0 + 10 minutes < block.timestamp`) and succeeds, allowing the attacker to immediately sell all purchased shares in the exact same transaction.

findings.remediation:
Apply the `nonReentrant` modifier to `_handleTrade` (or to the external entrypoints `buyShareCred`, `buyShareCredFor`, and `sellShareCred`) and follow the Checks-Effects-Interactions (CEI) pattern by updating `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` and `cred.latestActiveTimestamp` before performing any external ETH transfers or external calls.

```solidity
    function _handleTrade(
        uint256 credId_,
        uint256 amount_,
        bool isBuy,
        address curator_,
        uint256 priceLimit
    )
        internal
        whenNotPaused
        nonReentrant
    {
        if (amount_ == 0) {
            revert InvalidAmount();
        }

        if (!isExist(credId_)) {
            revert InvalidCredId();
        }

        PhiCred storage cred = creds[credId_];

        uint256 supply = cred.currentSupply;
        address creator = cred.creator;

        (uint256 price, uint256 protocolFee, uint256 creatorFee) =
            IBondingCurve(cred.bondingCurve).getPriceData(credId_, supply, amount_, isBuy);

        if (isBuy) {
            if (priceLimit != 0 && price + protocolFee + creatorFee > priceLimit) revert PriceExceedsLimit();
            if (supply + amount_ > MAX_SUPPLY) {
                revert MaxSupplyReached();
            }

            if (msg.value < price + protocolFee + creatorFee) {
                revert InsufficientPayment();
            }
        } else {
            if (priceLimit != 0 && price - protocolFee - creatorFee < priceLimit) revert PriceBelowLimit();
            if (block.timestamp <= lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD) {
                revert ShareLockPeriodNotPassed(
                    block.timestamp, lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD
                );
            }
            (, uint256 nums) = shareBalance[credId_].tryGet(curator_);
            if (nums < amount_) {
                revert InsufficientShares();
            }
        }

        _updateCuratorShareBalance(credId_, curator_, amount_, isBuy);
        cred.latestActiveTimestamp = block.timestamp;

        if (isBuy) {
            cred.currentSupply += amount_;
            lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] = block.timestamp;
            uint256 excessPayment = msg.value - price - protocolFee - creatorFee;
            if (excessPayment > 0) {
                _msgSender().safeTransferETH(excessPayment);
            }
        } else {
            cred.currentSupply -= amount_;
            curator_.safeTransferETH(price - protocolFee - creatorFee);
        }

        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(protocolFee);
        IPhiRewards(phiRewardsAddress).deposit{ value: creatorFee }(
            creator, bytes4(keccak256("CREATOR_ROYALTY_FEE")), ""
        );
        emit Royalty(creator, credId_, creatorFee);

        emit Trade(curator_, credId_, isBuy, amount_, price, protocolFee, cred.currentSupply);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `src/Cred.sol`, the protocol enforces a holding lock period (`SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD = 10 minutes`) before a curator can sell their shares in a credential. Specifically, when a curator calls `sellShareCred` (lines 182–184) or `batchSellShareCred` (lines 213–226), `_handleTrade` (line 625) and `_executeBatchTrade` (line 761) validate:

```solidity
if (block.timestamp <= lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD) {
    revert ShareLockPeriodNotPassed(
        block.timestamp, lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] + SHARE_LOCK_PERIOD
    );
}
```

However, `Cred` exposes `buyShareCredFor` (lines 186–189) and `batchBuyShareCred` (lines 191–211), both of which accept an arbitrary `curator_` parameter without verifying that `curator_` has authorized `_msgSender()` to purchase shares on their behalf:

```solidity
function buyShareCredFor(uint256 credId_, uint256 amount_, address curator_, uint256 maxPrice_) public payable {
    if (curator_ == address(0)) revert InvalidAddressZero();
    _handleTrade(credId_, amount_, true, curator_, maxPrice_);
}
```

Inside `_handleTrade` (line 644) and `_executeBatchTrade` (line 759), any buy operation updates the beneficiary's lock timestamp across their entire balance for that credential:

```solidity
lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] = block.timestamp;
```

Because `lastTradeTimestamp` is tracked as a single timestamp per `(credId, curator)` pair rather than per-purchase or restricted to self-initiated trades, any third party can purchase the minimum amount (`amount_ = 1`) on behalf of a target `curator_`. Doing so resets `lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_]` to the current `block.timestamp`, locking all of the victim's previously accumulated shares for another `10 minutes`.

findings.remediation:
Remove the arbitrary `curator_` parameter from `buyShareCredFor` and `batchBuyShareCred` so users can only buy shares for themselves, or require explicit opt-in / signature authorization from `curator_` before allowing a third party to buy shares on their behalf. Alternatively, if gifting shares is required, require `curator_` approval:

```solidity
    mapping(address curator => mapping(address sender => bool allowed)) public isApprovedBuyer;

    function setApprovedBuyer(address buyer_, bool allowed_) external {
        isApprovedBuyer[_msgSender()][buyer_] = allowed_;
    }

    function sellShareCred(uint256 credId_, uint256 amount_, uint256 minPrice_) public {
        _handleTrade(credId_, amount_, false, _msgSender(), minPrice_);
    }

    function buyShareCredFor(uint256 credId_, uint256 amount_, address curator_, uint256 maxPrice_) public payable {
        if (curator_ == address(0)) revert InvalidAddressZero();
        if (curator_ != _msgSender() && !isApprovedBuyer[curator_][_msgSender()]) revert UnauthorizedCaller();
        _handleTrade(credId_, amount_, true, curator_, maxPrice_);
    }

    function batchBuyShareCred(
        uint256[] calldata credIds_,
        uint256[] calldata amounts_,
        uint256[] calldata maxPrices_,
        address curator_
    )
        public
        payable
    {
        if (curator_ != _msgSender() && !isApprovedBuyer[curator_][_msgSender()]) revert UnauthorizedCaller();
        (uint256 totalCost, uint256[] memory prices, uint256[] memory protocolFees, uint256[] memory creatorFees) =
            _validateAndCalculateBatch(credIds_, amounts_, maxPrices_, true);

        if (totalCost > msg.value) revert InsufficientBatchPayment();

        _executeBatchBuy(credIds_, amounts_, curator_, prices, protocolFees, creatorFees);

        uint256 refund = msg.value - totalCost;
        if (refund > 0) {
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(refund);
        }
    }
```

Record each signed payload as consumed and reject subsequent uses. Key replay protection by the signed-data hash rather than by raw signature bytes, and mark the authorization consumed before entering credential creation or making external calls. For this upgradeable contract, append the mapping without disturbing the existing storage layout.

```solidity
mapping(bytes32 authorizationHash => bool consumed) private consumedCreateAuthorizations;

error CreateAuthorizationAlreadyUsed(bytes32 authorizationHash);

function createCred(
    address creator_,
    bytes calldata signedData_,
    bytes calldata signature_,
    uint16 buyShareRoyalty_,
    uint16 sellShareRoyalty_
)
    public
    payable
    whenNotPaused
{
    bytes32 authorizationHash = keccak256(signedData_);

    if (_recoverSigner(authorizationHash, signature_) != phiSignerAddress) revert AddressNotSigned();
    if (consumedCreateAuthorizations[authorizationHash]) {
        revert CreateAuthorizationAlreadyUsed(authorizationHash);
    }

    (
        uint256 expiresIn,
        address sender,
        ,
        address bondingCurve,
        string memory credURL,
        string memory credType,
        string memory verificationType,
        bytes32 merkleRoot_
    ) = abi.decode(signedData_, (uint256, address, uint256, address, string, string, string, bytes32));

    if (expiresIn <= block.timestamp) revert SignatureExpired();
    if (sender != _msgSender()) revert Unauthorized();
    if (!curatePriceWhitelist[bondingCurve]) revert Unauthorized();

    if (!credType.eq("BASIC") && !credType.eq("ADVANCED")) {
        revert InvalidCredType();
    }
    if (verificationType.eq("MERKLE") && merkleRoot_ == bytes32(0)) {
        revert InvalidMerkleRoot();
    }
    if (!verificationType.eq("MERKLE") && !verificationType.eq("SIGNATURE")) {
        revert InvalidVerificationType();
    }
    if (buyShareRoyalty_ > MAX_ROYALTY_RANGE || sellShareRoyalty_ > MAX_ROYALTY_RANGE) {
        revert InvalidRoyaltyRange();
    }

    consumedCreateAuthorizations[authorizationHash] = true;

    uint256 credId = _createCredInternal(
        creator_, credURL, credType, verificationType, bondingCurve, buyShareRoyalty_, sellShareRoyalty_
    );

    if (verificationType.eq("MERKLE")) {
        credsMerkeRoot[credId] = merkleRoot_;
        emit MerkleTreeSetUp(_msgSender(), credId, merkleRoot_);
    }
}
```

If byte-identical creations must be independently authorized, include a signer-generated unique nonce or authorization ID in each signed payload and consume that identifier instead.

Decode the `chainId` parameter from `signedData_` and require that it matches `block.chainid`.

