findings.technical_details:
In `src/PhiFactory.sol`, `updateArtSettings` allows the creator of an art piece (`onlyArtCreator(artId_)`) to update the art parameters and its ERC-2981 royalty configuration:

```solidity
uint256 tokenId = IPhiNFT1155Ownable(art.artAddress).getTokenIdFromFactoryArtId(artId_);
IPhiNFT1155Ownable(art.artAddress).updateRoyalties(tokenId, configuration);
```

`updateArtSettings` validates `receiver_`, `startTime_`, `endTime_`, and `maxSupply_` (lines 229–244), and then calls `PhiNFT1155.updateRoyalties(tokenId, configuration)` at line 255, which delegates to `CreatorRoyaltiesControl._updateRoyalties`:

```solidity
function _updateRoyalties(uint256 tokenId, RoyaltyConfiguration memory configuration) internal {
    if (configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0) && configuration.royaltyBPS > 0) {
        revert InvalidRoyaltyRecipient();
    }

    royalties[tokenId] = configuration;

    emit UpdatedRoyalties(tokenId, msg.sender, configuration);
}
```

In `CreatorRoyaltiesControl`, `ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT` is defined as `10_000` (representing 100%), and `royaltyInfo(uint256 tokenId, uint256 salePrice)` computes the royalty payment as:

```solidity
royaltyAmount = (config.royaltyBPS * salePrice) / ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT;
```

However, neither `PhiFactory.updateArtSettings` nor `CreatorRoyaltiesControl._updateRoyalties` checks that `configuration.royaltyBPS <= 10_000`. Because `royaltyBPS` is of type `uint32`, the art creator can set `configuration.royaltyBPS` to any value up to `type(uint32).max` (`4,294,967,295`, or `429,496.7295%`).

When `configuration.royaltyBPS > 10_000`, `royaltyInfo(tokenId, salePrice)` returns a `royaltyAmount` strictly greater than `salePrice`. This violates the EIP-2981 invariant (enforced in OpenZeppelin's reference `ERC2981._setTokenRoyalty` via `if (feeNumerator > denominator) revert`) that royalty payments must not exceed the sale price. Secondary marketplaces that deduct `royaltyAmount` from `salePrice` during settlement will revert due to arithmetic underflow (`salePrice - royaltyAmount`), effectively freezing secondary trading of the token on ERC-2981-compliant marketplaces.

findings.remediation:
Validate in `PhiFactory.updateArtSettings` (and in `CreatorRoyaltiesControl._updateRoyalties`) that `configuration.royaltyBPS` does not exceed `10_000` (or the protocol's maximum allowed royalty basis points) before updating the royalty configuration.

```solidity
    function updateArtSettings(
        uint256 artId_,
        string memory url_,
        address receiver_,
        uint256 maxSupply_,
        uint256 mintFee_,
        uint256 startTime_,
        uint256 endTime_,
        bool soulBounded_,
        IPhiNFT1155Ownable.RoyaltyConfiguration memory configuration
    )
        external
        onlyArtCreator(artId_)
    {
        if (receiver_ == address(0)) {
            revert InvalidAddressZero();
        }

        if (endTime_ < startTime_) {
            revert InvalidTimeRange();
        }
        if (endTime_ < block.timestamp) {
            revert EndTimeInPast();
        }
        if (configuration.royaltyBPS > 10_000) {
            revert InvalidRoyaltyBPS();
        }

        PhiArt storage art = arts[artId_];

        if (art.numberMinted > maxSupply_) {
            revert ExceedMaxSupply();
        }

        art.receiver = receiver_;
        art.maxSupply = maxSupply_;
        art.mintFee = mintFee_;
        art.startTime = startTime_;
        art.endTime = endTime_;
        art.soulBounded = soulBounded_;
        art.uri = url_;

        uint256 tokenId = IPhiNFT1155Ownable(art.artAddress).getTokenIdFromFactoryArtId(artId_);
        IPhiNFT1155Ownable(art.artAddress).updateRoyalties(tokenId, configuration);
        emit ArtUpdated(artId_, url_, receiver_, maxSupply_, mintFee_, startTime_, endTime_, soulBounded_);
    }
```

findings.technical_details:
In `src/abstract/CreatorRoyaltiesControl.sol`, the `_updateRoyalties` function validates the incoming `RoyaltyConfiguration` as follows:

```solidity
if (configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0) && configuration.royaltyBPS > 0) {
    revert InvalidRoyaltyRecipient();
}
royalties[tokenId] = configuration;
```

This validation check (`configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0) && configuration.royaltyBPS > 0`) intentionally allows `configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0)` when `configuration.royaltyBPS == 0`, which is the natural representation for disabling royalties (0% royalty, no recipient).

However, when `royaltyInfo(tokenId, salePrice)` is queried by an ERC-2981 compliant marketplace, it calls `getRoyalties(tokenId)`:

```solidity
function getRoyalties(uint256 tokenId) public view returns (RoyaltyConfiguration memory) {
    if (!initilaized) revert NotInitialized();
    RoyaltyConfiguration memory config = royalties[tokenId];
    if (config.royaltyRecipient != address(0)) {
        return config;
    }
    // Return default configuration
    return RoyaltyConfiguration({ royaltyBPS: 500, royaltyRecipient: royaltyRecipient });
}
```

Because `getRoyalties` only checks `if (config.royaltyRecipient != address(0))` to determine whether a custom token royalty configuration exists, any configuration with `royaltyRecipient == address(0)` is treated as unconfigured. Consequently, when an art creator sets `RoyaltyConfiguration({ royaltyBPS: 0, royaltyRecipient: address(0) })` (either during `createArt` or via `updateRoyalties` / `updateArtSettings`) intending to have 0% royalties, `getRoyalties` ignores the stored `0` BPS and instead returns the default configuration of `500` BPS (5%) payable to the default `royaltyRecipient` (`protocolFeeDestination`).

Alternatively, if `_updateRoyalties` was never meant to allow `royaltyRecipient == address(0)` unless resetting to the default 5% royalty, allowing `{royaltyBPS: 0, royaltyRecipient: address(0)}` while emitting `UpdatedRoyalties(tokenId, msg.sender, {0, address(0)})` creates a direct contradiction between the emitted event / stored state and the actual 5% royalty enforced by `getRoyalties` and `royaltyInfo`.

findings.remediation:
If `royaltyRecipient == address(0)` is never valid for a custom configuration (even when `royaltyBPS == 0`), disallow `address(0)` unconditionally in `_updateRoyalties` so creators must provide a non-zero recipient when setting `royaltyBPS = 0`:

```solidity
    function _updateRoyalties(uint256 tokenId, RoyaltyConfiguration memory configuration) internal {
        if (configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0)) {
            revert InvalidRoyaltyRecipient();
        }

        royalties[tokenId] = configuration;

        emit UpdatedRoyalties(tokenId, msg.sender, configuration);
    }
```

Alternatively, if `{royaltyBPS: 0, royaltyRecipient: address(0)}` is intended to be a valid 0% royalty configuration, track whether a custom configuration has been set for `tokenId` (e.g., via a boolean flag in `RoyaltyConfiguration` or a separate mapping) and check that flag in `getRoyalties`.

findings.technical_details:
In `src/abstract/CreatorRoyaltiesControl.sol`, royalty rates are expressed in basis points relative to `ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT = 10_000` (100%). The `royaltyInfo` function calculates the secondary sale royalty payout as:

```solidity
royaltyAmount = (config.royaltyBPS * salePrice) / ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT;
```

According to EIP-2981, the royalty amount represents a fraction of the sale price and must not exceed `salePrice` itself (i.e., `royaltyBPS <= 10_000`).

However, when updating a token's royalty configuration in `_updateRoyalties` (lines 58–66), the function only checks that a non-zero `royaltyBPS` is not paired with a zero `royaltyRecipient`:

```solidity
if (configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0) && configuration.royaltyBPS > 0) {
    revert InvalidRoyaltyRecipient();
}
```

There is no upper-bound check enforcing `configuration.royaltyBPS <= ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT`. Because `RoyaltyConfiguration.royaltyBPS` is typed as `uint32` (in `src/interfaces/ICreatorRoyaltiesControl.sol`), `updateRoyalties` in `PhiNFT1155` (or `PhiFactory.updateArtSettings`) allows setting `royaltyBPS` to any value up to `4,294,967,295` (~429,496.73% of `salePrice`).

When `royaltyBPS > 10_000`, `royaltyInfo(tokenId, salePrice)` returns `royaltyAmount > salePrice`. Any EIP-2981-compliant NFT marketplace that deducts `royaltyAmount` from `salePrice` will revert due to arithmetic underflow (`salePrice - royaltyAmount`) or explicit royalty-bound checks, rendering the token untradable on those marketplaces.

findings.remediation:
Add an upper-bound validation in `CreatorRoyaltiesControl._updateRoyalties` to ensure `configuration.royaltyBPS` cannot exceed `ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT` (or a protocol-defined maximum royalty cap).

```solidity
    error InvalidRoyaltyBPS();

    function _updateRoyalties(uint256 tokenId, RoyaltyConfiguration memory configuration) internal {
        if (configuration.royaltyRecipient == address(0) && configuration.royaltyBPS > 0) {
            revert InvalidRoyaltyRecipient();
        }
        if (configuration.royaltyBPS > ROYALTY_BPS_TO_PERCENT) {
            revert InvalidRoyaltyBPS();
        }

        royalties[tokenId] = configuration;

        emit UpdatedRoyalties(tokenId, msg.sender, configuration);
    }
```

Restrict metadata updates in `PhiNFT1155.mint` so that `minterData` and `advancedTokenURI` are only written on the recipient's initial mint (when their balance is zero and metadata is unset) or when the recipient themselves initiates the transaction (`tx.origin == to_`).

