findings.technical_details:
The `Float128` library supports two normalized mantissa sizes: medium (`M`, 38 digits) and large (`L`, 72 digits), and states in the documentation that the maximum number of digits the library is meant to handle accurately is 72, prioritizing precision over gas efficiency.

In `Float128.toPackedFloat(int mantissa, int exponent)` (`src/Float128.sol`), if `mantissa` does not already have exactly 38 or 72 digits (lines 1095–1098), the function counts its digits and determines whether the normalized result should use a 38-digit (`M`) or 72-digit (`L`) mantissa:

```solidity
digitsMantissa = findNumberOfDigits(uint(mantissa));
assembly {
    mantissaMultiplier := sub(digitsMantissa, MAX_DIGITS_M)
    let isResultL := slt(MAXIMUM_EXPONENT, add(exponent, mantissaMultiplier))
    if isResultL {
        mantissaMultiplier := sub(mantissaMultiplier, DIGIT_DIFF_L_M)
        float := or(float, MANTISSA_L_FLAG_MASK)
    }
    exponent := add(exponent, mantissaMultiplier)
    let negativeMultiplier := and(TWO_COMPLEMENT_SIGN_MASK, mantissaMultiplier)
    if negativeMultiplier {
        mantissa := mul(mantissa, exp(BASE, sub(0, mantissaMultiplier)))
    }
    if iszero(negativeMultiplier) {
        mantissa := div(mantissa, exp(BASE, mantissaMultiplier))
    }
}
```

At line 1102, `isResultL` only checks `slt(MAXIMUM_EXPONENT, add(exponent, mantissaMultiplier))` (i.e., whether `exponent + (digitsMantissa - 38) > -18`). It fails to check whether `digitsMantissa > MAX_DIGITS_M` (`38`).

As a result:
1. When `mantissa` has between 39 and 71 significant digits (or more than 72 digits) and `exponent + (digitsMantissa - 38) <= -18`, `isResultL` evaluates to `0`.
2. `mantissaMultiplier = digitsMantissa - 38` remains positive (`negativeMultiplier == 0`), so line 1113 executes `mantissa := div(mantissa, exp(BASE, mantissaMultiplier))`.
3. Instead of scaling the mantissa to 72 digits (`L` format) to preserve the provided significant digits, `toPackedFloat` divides the mantissa by $10^{\text{digitsMantissa} - 38}$, discarding up to 33 significant digits and encoding the result as a 38-digit `M` float.

This also creates a direct representation inconsistency for equivalent floating-point values: passing a 72-digit mantissa ending in `0` with `exponent = -72` bypasses line 1095 and is encoded at lines 1125–1129 as an `L` float preserving all significant digits, whereas passing the mathematically identical value as a 71-digit mantissa with `exponent = -71` enters line 1095, sets `isResultL = 0` at line 1102 (`-71 + 33 = -38 <= -18`), and truncates 33 digits of precision.

findings.remediation:
Update `isResultL` in `Float128.toPackedFloat` so that mantissas with more than `MAX_DIGITS_M` (38) digits are also normalized into the large (`L`, 72-digit) format rather than truncated down to 38 digits:

```solidity
                digitsMantissa = findNumberOfDigits(uint(mantissa));
                assembly {
                    mantissaMultiplier := sub(digitsMantissa, MAX_DIGITS_M)
                    let isResultL := or(
                        slt(MAXIMUM_EXPONENT, add(exponent, mantissaMultiplier)),
                        gt(digitsMantissa, MAX_DIGITS_M)
                    )
                    if isResultL {
                        mantissaMultiplier := sub(mantissaMultiplier, DIGIT_DIFF_L_M)
                        float := or(float, MANTISSA_L_FLAG_MASK)
                    }
                    exponent := add(exponent, mantissaMultiplier)
                    let negativeMultiplier := and(TWO_COMPLEMENT_SIGN_MASK, mantissaMultiplier)
                    if negativeMultiplier {
                        mantissa := mul(mantissa, exp(BASE, sub(0, mantissaMultiplier)))
                    }
                    if iszero(negativeMultiplier) {
                        mantissa := div(mantissa, exp(BASE, mantissaMultiplier))
